Gutach: Even more safety for pedestrians
They are not 100 meters apart from each other: On Tuesday, the new B 33 pedestrian traffic light at the village parking lot has been put into operation - within sight of the older town hall traffic light.
Two facilities so close to each other: Intention or shield citizen prank?
Gutach's mayor answered this question clearly yesterday.
"The town hall traffic light was installed at that time because it secures the way to school," Eckert explained yesterday.
The Kluser traffic light secures both cyclists as well as bus passengers and Bergle residents.
The plant, which was officially commissioned yesterday, is important for the Sulzbachweg/Kirchstraße intersection area.
We have the museum, two churches, spa park, the bus stop, a doctor and a bank as well as the traffic flow from the residential area ›Grub‹.
"With the high volume of traffic and pedestrians, another traffic light had to be used for their safety," says Eckert.
This is also confirmed by Peter Arnold from the Landratsamt Offenburg.
"According to current measurements, about 12 000 vehicles pass through the municipality of Gutach every day on the B 33, of which about ten percent are heavy-duty traffic," emphasizes Arnold.
Therefore, the construction of another traffic light is more than necessary: "Safety simply proceeds here," says Arnold.
A total of four traffic shows had been carried out, a roundabout was also considered, but because of the narrowness in the intersection area Sulzbachweg/Kirchstraße again rejected.
According to Arnold, everything was previously testeded in the choice of location of the traffic light poles: "By means of a van loaded especially for us with particularly long wooden logs, we have tested whether these vehicles can extend from the Sulzbachweg onto the B 33 without bending the traffic light mast".
The traffic light system itself, which costs around €15 000, is "the most modern thing currently available on the market," Arnold explained.
The system is equipped with coloured LEDs, which shine so vigorously that the lights can be easily recognized by motorists, for example, even when the sun is low.
And it is also economical: the older lighting systems consume about 100 watts, the new only eight watts.
There are three lighting systems per direction of travel.
Arnold explained the technology of the new plant: This is equipped with two radar sensors.
If the pedestrian presses the traffic light button, the upper radar sensor tests the traffic situation.
If the road is clear, greenery comes directly for the pedestrian, if not, it takes about 15 seconds.
A further radar sensor checks whether the green phase for the pedestrian can be terminated.
"Should a group or disabled people walk across the road, the green phase will be extended, so everyone comes safely across the carriageway," Arnold explained.
Of course, the motorist has to think as a partner and observe the road.
This was not the case yesterday: As soon as the traffic light for pedestrians showed green, an upper-class vehicle rushed through - with bright red.
Josef Winkler has been writing for more than 30 years the hardships of his childhood and adolescence from the soul.
Carinthian poet has often described the disasters of his Catholic village childhood - the speechlessness, the tendency to brute violence and dull sexuality, the narrowness and joylessness.
The Büchner Prize winner is best known as a prose writer, theatre texts are rare in his work.
Collage from prose texts Gerhard Fresacher puts together a collage of prose texts for his performance "Wetterleuchten auf der Tongenspitze", which can now be seen in Garage X on Peter's Square.
The theatre maker, for example, combines elements from the autobiographically influenced novel "Der Leibigen" (1987) with prosaminiatures from "Leichnam, his family belauernd" (2003).
On the largely empty stage - an important prop: a crumpled sofa on whit glance copulating and masturbating - the eight-piece ensemble sways through the textual material.
Director Fresacher does not seem to trust much.
The 70-minute performance covers the template with a wealth of directing, known from the repertoire of post-dramatic forms of play.
Above all, the actresses come with the sometimes somewhat questionable scenic implementations.
They are touched hard, dipped with their head under water, stabbered against the wall with their evening robes.
Conquered into cellophane or bodices, they stalk on dangerously high heels through the staging, either monologuing loudly or lying completely silently on the stage floor.
However, the text hardly conveys itself in this strenuous way.
The evening has the best moments when it is sung - the spectrum ranges from deep purple to popular songs.
Only towards the end does the overwrought performance somewhat come to rest, and Winkler's straight absurd humour flashes up.
A black box in the car?
U.S. street planners are looking for a source of money to repair the decaying highway system, and believe to have found the solution in a small black box that finds space in the dashboard of every car.
The devices, which record every kilometer driven and report the information to the authorities, are at the heart of a controversial attempt by Washington and the planning offices of the states to revise the outdated system for financing U.S. roads.
The usually rather dull area of road planning has suddenly sparked an intense debate with colorful alliances.
Libertarians have allied themselves with environmental groups and advocate that the government can use the small boxes to record the kilometres driven – and possibly also where they were driven – and then use the information to calculate tax rulings.
The Tea Party is horrified.
The U.S. Civil Rights Association (ACLU) is also deeply concerned and voices a number of privacy concerns.
But while you can't agree on a course of action in Congress, several states don't wait any longer.
They are currently examining how they can switch over the course of the next ten years to a system where drivers pay per mile driven.
Thousands of motorists have already tested the tachographs, some of which are equipped with GPS surveillance.
This is really a must for our country.
"It's nothing we're just going to possibly use," said Hasan Ikhrata, managing director of Southern California Assn. of Governments, which is planning a record of the miles driven among all California motorists in the state from 2025.
The way we pay those taxes will change.
The technology for this is there.
The initiative comes at a time when the Highway Trust Fund, which is funded from the taxes that Americans pay at the fuel dispenser is broke.
However, there is not as much refueling in America as before.
Cars consume less gasoline.
The state petroleum tax of 18.4 cents per gallon (less than 4 euro cents per litre) hasn't risen for 20 years.
Politicians don't dare to raise the tax even by a penny at high fuel prices.
“The gasoline tax is simply not sustainable,” said Lee Munnich, an expert in traffic legislation at the University of Minnesota.
His state has recently equipped 500 cars with tachographs designed to test a mile-based payment system.
“This is the most useful alternative in the long term,” he said.
Bureaucrats refer to it as a mile-based user fee.
It is not surprising that the idea appeals to urban liberals, because the tax could be used, for example, to influence driving behavior in such a way that congestion and climate-damaging exhaust gases are reduced.
California planners rely on the system for the development of strategies designed to achieve the state's ambitious, legally enshrined climate change targets.
Yet Republican Bill Shuster of Pennsylvania, chairman of the House Transportation Committee, has also stated that he sees it as the most viable long-term alternative.
Reason Foundation's vacant marketers are also impressed by the idea of letting drivers pay after a distance.
“This isn’t a tax that disappears into a black hole,” explains Adrian Moore, vice president for guidelines at Reason.
People pay directly for what they get.
The movement is also backed by two former U.S. transport ministers who, in a report in 2011, had called on Congress to move towards a mile-based reckoning.
U.S. Senate last year approved a £90-million-dollar pilot project that would have included 10,000 cars.
However, the majority in the House of Representatives prevented the push, responding to the concerns of MPs from rural areas representing people who often have to travel many miles on their way to work or to the city.
Nevertheless, several states and major cities are moving on their own in this direction.
The most dedicated is Oregon, which is currently attracting 5,000 riders for the country's largest experiment.
These drivers will soon pay the mile fees instead of the petroleum tax to the state.
Nevada has already completed a pilot project.
New York City is also considering one.
Illinois tests it to a limited extent with trucks.
And the I-95 coalition, which includes the transportation ministries of 17 states on the East Coast (including Maryland, Pennsylvania, Virginia and Florida), is currently investigating how to introduce the change.
The concept is not a universal hit.
In Nevada, where 50 volunteers were recently equipped with the devices, motorists were sceptical at the thought the government could pursue any of their movements.
“Concerns about Big Brother and things like this have been a big problem,” explains Alauddin Khan, head of strategy and results management at Nevada’s Transportation Department.
People didn't want it.
When the test started, the ACLU of Nevada warned on its website: “It would be relatively easy to turn the tachographs into full-blown monitoring devices.”
There is no need to build a gigantic, bulky technological infrastructure that would inevitably be used to capture data on the daily movements of individuals.
Nevada is among a number of states that are now looking out for affordable technology that allows the state to capture the kilometres driven, but not exactly when and where.
According to Khan, the public would also be more reassured.
The hunting for this technology has led some authorities to a small start-up company called True Mileage in California.
The company did not originally compete to help states tax motorists.
Rather, their goal was to gain a foothold in an emerging automotive insurance market where drivers are supposed to pay based on the miles driven.
But the devices it tested are also interesting for the street planners, because they do not work with GPS and provide only limited information, which is regularly uploaded by modem.
“People are more willing to get involved if their speed and locations aren’t recorded,” explained Ryan Morrison, managing director of True Mileage.
Big mistakes have been made in some of these public pilot programs.
There are significantly cheaper and less intrusive ways to implement this.
In Oregon, planners are experimenting by giving motorists a range of choices.
You can opt for a device with or without GPS.
Or they don't choose a device at all and instead pay a flat fee based on the miles driven on average by all residents of the state.
Other bodies hope to be able to sell the concept of a suspicious public by equipping the devices with more features than with less.
In New York City, traffic officials want to develop a device for taxation that can also be used to pay parking fees, an insurance only has to be paid for mileages driven, and speed data from other vehicles in real-time, thanks to which motorists can dodge traffic jams.
“Motorists would be motivated to participate by the added value of the benefits that the system offers,” the city’s planning document said.
However, some traffic planners wonder whether all the talk about paying per mile isn’t just a huge distraction.
At the Metropolitan Transportation Commission for the San Francisco Bay area, officials said Congress could solve the problem of the bankrupt Highway Trust Fund simply by increasing the fuel tax.
An additional one-time fee or an annual levy could be imposed on motorists with hybrid vehicles or other vehicles that consume little gasoline, so that they also pay a fair share.
"There is no reason for surgery if an aspirin is also sufficient," explains Randy Rentschler, the Commission's head of legislation and public affairs.
When we do that, hundreds of millions of motorists worry about their privacy and numerous other things.
Königsfeld: Small team beats bravely
The volunteer firefighting fire brigades mastered its autumn main rehearsal despite lack of staff with flying colours.
Only eleven men took part in the exercise.
In his exercise assumption, department commander Hans Kammerer had chosen the Feder estate in Burgberger Straße.
A short circuit is said to have been created there during sawing.
Due to the resulting fire and smoke development, two people were injured, but one of them was able to send an emergency call.
Two respirators of the weir prepared to enter the building after the first review of the situation by Hans Kammerer.
After a short time, it was possible to find the first person and guide him into the open.
The second person had to be worn.
This wasn’t so easy, as there was also a tight staircase to overcome.
The building, a workshop with an integrated stable for two horses, was not easy to secure.
It stored a lot of wood and also straw bales in it.
In addition, there were electrical machines for woodworking.
The first extinguishing attack took place via the tank in the vehicle.
A further line was carried out via an overfloating hydrant at a distance of about 100 meters.
Three extinguishing attacks could now be designed.
A hollow-jet pipe was also used in the building.
Hans Kammerer was also about pointing out what can be done with little available personnel.
In an emergency, support is provided via the day-to-day operations group Königsfeld.
The commander was satisfied with the course of the exercise.
Networking of universities and companies important
On his visit to the district of Breisgau-Hochschwarzwald, state parliament president Guido Wolf was also informed in Titisee-Neustadt about where the workforce of tomorrow comes from.
In the Förderzentrum Hochschwarzwald, together with the managing directors of the Vocational Schools as well as the District Gymnasium and the Chairman of the Parents’ Advisory Board, he explored this important question.
"Education is an important location factor," emphasized Claudia Stehle, director of the Hans-Thoma-Schule, who presented the networked school project Bildungszentrum Hochschwarzwald.
Wolf was impressed by the model projects for training.
Since 2011, there has been a successful cooperation between Förderzentrum, Kreisgymnasium and Vocational School Centre in the Waldstadt.
He also praised family friendliness in the county.
"It is important not only to keep an eye on the family phase, but also because of the demographic change to take care of the care of relatives," Wolf explained.
In addition, he cited that more and more employees took care of the care and care of their relatives.
"Without the support of employers, the resulting burden on employees can lead to a termination of the employment relationship," Wolf said.
But none can afford to lose skilled workers, he said.
Another, particularly important factor is the networking of universities and companies.
"Because those who have already learned about the advantages of the region during their studies are often preserved with their acquired knowledge and skills of the regional economy, which contributes to the strengthening of the region," the state parliament president was convinced.
Because only if sufficient training places are offered, the need for skilled workers can also be covered.
In his opinion, the location advantage of the district is its advantageous location in the south-west of Germany, in the immediate vicinity of France and Switzerland.
In addition, on his train journey from Titisee-Neustadt to Freiburg, he witnessed the efforts that have already been made for the expansion of public transport.
"Remain stubborn and make sure that public transport gets better," he explained to the address of the local politicians present.
Experience an entertaining afternoon with music
For 15 years, the Kaul family has been inviting Dietingen seniors for coffee, cake and then a Vesper.
In the past, the older citizens were served in the rest area.
Meanwhile, this afternoon takes place in the nursing home St. Josef.
The residents were looking forward to the delicious cakes and cakes.
Later there was sausage salad.
Silvia Kimmich-Bantle and her father Karl Kimmich entertained with popular melodies.
The more than 100-year-old natural stone wall as a front demarcation of the old schoolyard to Kirchstraße before the renovation is to be used again on the renovation cost plan of the city of Bräunlingen.
In the days before the Kilbig, in the first works of the renovation of the front schoolyard, a small angular wall was installeded for stabilization and static relief of the more than one hundred-year-old natural stone wall with balustrade.
According to Alexander Misok of the Bräunlinger Stadtbauamt, the further procedure envisages involving the state monument office in the plans and creating a renovation concept for the old wall.
Subsequently, a cost calculation is to be carried out by experts, which should then be placed on the table for the municipal council to decide and determine the further procedure.
A decision on this is expected to be made as early as next year.
After the renovation, paving and tree planting of the old school courtyard within the two side wings of the school built in 1912 in the foreseeable future, the boundary wall in need of renovation has to be refurbished from scratch and placed on a new substructure in the foreseeable future.
The historic, one hundred-year-old demarcation wall of the old schoolyard to Kirchstraße, is mainly due to water and frost damage, since there is no drainage, in need of refurbishment and has an arch towards the front.
Deep cracks in individual stones testify to weather damage, but the wall is currently not at risk of collapse.
A stable wall is a prerequisite for one of pupils used schoolyards, which has been done by the current fastening work.
Originally, the schoolyard renovation was already planned in 2008/2009, but high unplanned expenses brought a postponement.
An important word in the historic school wall renovation, which has even already been used for film recordings, will be voiced by the state monument office.
Should specifications come from this side, then the city of Bräunlingen hopes for grants from the preservationists.
The pupils of the Bräunlingen elementary school, who are now taught again in a well-renovated old school building with a new auditorium, will soon be able to use the old schoolyard.
Özdemir wants to receive jazz training in Stuttgart
Cem Özdemir, Federal Chairman of the Greens, has now also intervened in the ongoing discussions about the future of music academies in Baden-Württemberg.
"I think it's wrong to give up the holistic education at the Musikhochschule Stuttgart," Özdemir told the Stuttgarter Nachrichten.
Jazz and classical music belong together at the jazz location in Stuttgart.
With this, Özdemir, who is considered the favorite in Stuttgart for a direct mandate in the federal election on September 22, contradicts the Green-Red state government.
Based on an expert study, this promotes a reorganization of the five music academies in Baden-Württemberg.
Subsequently, among other things, the Jazz and Pop degree program is to be relocated from the Musikhochschule Stuttgart to the Musikhochschule Mannheim.
U.S. border: 'super-tunnel' for drug smuggling discovered
On the Mexico-U.S. border, investigators have discovered and decommissioned a "super-tunnel" for drug smuggling.
U.S. customs officials said the more than 500-meter-long tube between Tijuana and San Diego was equipped with electricity, train tracks and a ventilation system.
Three suspects were arrested, eight tons of marijuana and almost 150 kilograms of cocaine seized.
The tunnel had not yet been put into use.
"These cartels are stupid if they think they can burrow under the radar," U.S. District Attorney General Laura Duffy said at a news conference outside a warehouse in San Diego, where one end of the tunnel had been discovered.
Addressing the drug ganges, Duffy said: "If you continue to build such tunnels and try to use them, we are determined to make it a huge waste of your dirty money."
Both U.S. authorities and Mexican security forces are undergoing a permanent fight against the drug cartels.
Since 2006, more than 77,000 people have been killed in Mexico in connection with drug crime.
Designed by experts
Due to the elaborate equipment of the tunnel, the investigators assume that it was designed by architects and engineers and that the construction took around a year.
The track system was equipped in such a way that electrically operated wagons could be used there.
On the Mexican side is the access in a building that is 80 meters from the border.
According to the authorities, a ladder leads 20 meters into the depth to the actual tunnel entrance.
The tunnel has a cross-section of 1.20 meters in height and 90 centimeters wide.
He would also have been suitable to promote illegal immigration towards the USA.
Two of the suspects were arrested in connection with the cocaine find.
The thirder, a Mexican, was arrested because of the confiscated marijuana.
All three threaten life deprivation as a maximum sentence, officials said.
Since 2006, eight such tunnels have been discovered, according to the press conference in San Diego.
However, this is the first time that cocaine has been found in such a tunnel construction.
Usually, cocaine is smuggled in smaller quantities rather than tunnels.
This shows the “desperation” of the drug gangs, whose traditional routes are meanwhile cut off, said Bill Sherman of the drug manhunt DEA in San Diego.
They would do anything to get to the U.S.
No details of those arrested were disclosed, at least one was Mexican, it said.
You have to expect imprisonmental sentences of up to ten years.
2014 becomes Geisinger Festjahr
The anniversary of the city of Geisingen on the occasion of the 1250.
The return of the first documentary mention is approaching.
When the year comes to an end in a good eight weeks, the anniversary year is coming up.
Geisingen and Kirchen-Hausen were mentioned together for the first time in 794.
In churches, a document was produced in which both places are mentioned.
On 15 March, there will be an official kick-off on the anniversary year, and since Geisingen currently has no hall, this kick-off event will take place in the place where both were first mentioned 1250 years ago, in Kirchen-Hausen.
As part of the anniversary, a number of events are planned both in Geisingen and Kirchen-Hausen.
Kirchen-Hausen will become 1250 in July on a festive weekend from 18 to 20 July.
Recurrence celebrations.
On Friday with a ceremony, on Saturday and Sunday with a festival around the Kirchtalhalle.
On this festive weekend is also the Kirchen-Hausener Kirchenfest.
On the occasion of the jubilee, Rudolf Barth City Music Director Rudolf Barth has written a commissioned composition, which will be performed on three dates.
On 21 June together with the solstice celebration of the Black Forest Association, on 12 July in the arena with fireworks and on 13 December as the first concert of city music in the new Festhalle.
This will be completed and inaugurated in the anniversary year, the official date is on the last weekend of September, 27 and 28 September.
On the occasion of the Geisingen city anniversary, a new chronicle will also be published.
"However, it should become a chronicle not only by Geisingen itself, but a printed work about today's Geisingen, which consists precisely of Geisingen, Kirchen-Hausen, Leipferdingen, Aulfingen and Gutmadingen," says Mayor Walter Hengstler.
And today’s Geisingen also celebrates its 2014 birthday, in 1974 today’s space with the incorporations was completed.
The new chronicle will then be presented on 21 or 22 November in the new Festhalle in Geisingen.
However, 2014 is another year with many anniversaries.
Strohglonki fool's guild begins on February 8 with an evening of customs on the occasion of its 60th anniversary, Harmonie Gutmadingen will be 90 years old and will have the festive banquet on 29 March and then celebrate the district music festival from 1 to 4 May.
The Altenwerk Leipferdingen turns 40 years old, the Geisinger School has been at the new location for 50 years and celebrates this on May 10, the Musikverein Polyhymnia Leipferdingen will be 150 years old and celebrates this as part of the Brunnenfest from 4 to 7 July.
The town of Geisingen was first mentioned in 764 in a donation certificate, which is owned by the monastery of St. Gallen.
The certificate was made in churches (houses), which at that time was a court place.
Both municipalities are therefore mentioned in the same document.
Reason to celebrate this 1250-year-old accordingly in 2014, in addition the municipal reform is 40 years ago.
Council targets track facilities
Should the "Am Hirschen" level crossing be extensively rebuilt in order to increase road safety?
Yes, the local councils have decided.
Schiltach has to take 220 000 euros in hand for this.
Deutsche Bahn intends to improve the Kinzigtal railway line next year.
In particular, the safety at level crossings is to be increased - regular collisions of trains with other vehicles occur nationwide.
If the municipalities agree to the conversion measures, they have to contribute a third of the costs, the rest are shared by the railway and the federal government.
Schiltach's city builder Roland Grießhaber explained to the local council the peculiarities of the level crossing "Am Hirschen".
There, the city plans to build a bulge at the level of the local tunnel mouth west of the railway tracks.
There, larger vehicles, for example those that transport longwood, could also stand in oncoming traffic without having to block the tracks.
Grießhaber therefore proposed constructing a necessary retaining wall in such a way that the city could widen the adjoining road in due course in order to ensure a smooth flow of traffic.
This was controversially debated in the local council, as some councils questioned the need for a road distribution: Thomas Kipp put the mood of the critics in a nutshell with his statement: "Do we have to take so much money in hand because of so few vehicles?"
Mayor Thomas Haas disagreed: The level crossing "Hirschen" is regularly used for longwood transports.
Even wood from the "Kuhbach" area is partially transported over this route, because the vehicles, which are up to 20 meters long, could not use the route at the Häberles Bridge, because they could not turn into the main road there.
In addition, the route serves as a diversion for those vehicles that are not allowed to use the tunnel if the main road is closed, Haas argued.
Since it should be foreseen that Kirchberg and Schlossberg tunnels would have to be rebuilt and refurbished, as is currently the case in Wolfach, the level crossing "Am "Hirschen" could also have an important traffic significance for a longer period of time.
The councils agreed to investigate what costs the envisaged widening of the road would cause.
Despite this, the conversion of the level crossing was approved by the majority with the proviso that the necessary retaining wall will be built in such a way that the city could expand the subsequent road in the direction of "Bahnhofstraße" for encounter traffic to around 5.5 meters if necessary.
The conversion of the level crossing at the Trautwein tannery, on the other hand, could lead to a completely different problem that affects the access road to the strongly rising "Geroltzhäuser Weg".
The administration had examined the conversion plans of the railway by the engineering office Breinlinger, since it had doubts about whether the planned connection of the Geroltzhäuser Weg was then easily possible.
The investigation concluded that it could not be ruled out to rule out that the inclination ratios of the road could lead to longer vehicles.
In turn, the city bears the responsibility for this as a road construction load carrier.
The conversion could therefore lead to liability disputes.
Since both the wood removal and the turning worked in the current state of expansion, one should not agree to the conversion of the level crossing, Haas argued.
At this point, too, the risk of a collision of the train with a vehicle standing on the transition is significantly lower than at the other level crossings, because directly next to it is located the stop point center, to which there is a good visual connection.
Even trains from the main station would have to be slowed down at this point to such an extent that no collision threatens.
The majority of the conversion of this level crossing was refused approval because the city of Schiltach "would have to bathe the problem" with the problems ocupying vehicles, according to the councils, since the railway could withdraw from responsibility after the completion of the construction work.
The panel considers the conversions at the level crossings "Vor Heubach" and "Vor Kuhbach", for which crossing agreements have already been made with the railway.
Esa decides on major space missions: missions for billions
Distant planets, gravitational waves or black holes - experts from the European space agency must now agree on two large-scale projects they want to launch over the next few years.
There were 30 proposals to choose from, five are still in the race.
distant worlds.
More than 1000 planets at other stars have already been found by astronomers.
Whether there is at least on some of these lives, no one knows.
It’s difficult to choose from: should we look for gravitational waves?
Or rather explore the origin of the cosmos?
Or search for Earth-like planets and perhaps discover alien life?
These questions arise precisely those responsible for the European space agency Esa.
For their "Cosmic Vision" program, they are looking for ideas for two large-scale space projects designed to decisively advance research.
The budget for such "L-missions" is in each case about one billion euros.
The launch is expected to be 2028 and 2034.
In early September, the elite of European space exploration met in Paris to discuss proposals for such missions.
30 ideas were debated, five of which have now been shortlisted.
Numerous committees examine the proposals and check with the researchers.
In the next few days, a final decision by the scientific program committee is to be made.
Esa has selected a total of four major questions to which the missions of the "Cosmic Vision" program, which was initiated in 2007, should find answers.
What are the conditions for the formation of planets and lives?
The exact events in the formation of stars from large gas clouds - and thus also the formation of planets orbiting these stars - are still in the proverbial darkness.
A large infrared telescope in space could permeate this darkness.
And if a star owns planets, under what circumstances can live occur there?
In order to investigate this question, the space telescope "Cheops" is to be launched in 2017, with which 500 already known planetary systems will be examined in more detail in our immediate vicinity.
However, the possibilities are limited, Cheops is a small mission with a budget of 150 million euros.
Using larger instruments, astronomers could depict Earth-like planets and even analyse the composition of their atmospheres and thus search for "biomarkers": gases considered an indication of biological activity.
How does the solar system work?
This question, too, is ultimately intended to provide information on the prerequisites for the emergence of life.
An important role is played by the magnetic activity of a star and the interaction of its magnetic field as well as the particle radiation emitted by it with the planetary system.
In our solar system these processes can be investigated by way of example.
With the "Solar Orbiter," ESA has already approved an M-Class mission (where the budget is around half a billion euros) for this question.
The probe is scheduled to launch in 2017 and closely observe the surface and activity of the sun.
Of great interest to the researchers are also Jupiter and its moons.
They want to find out what role the giant planet played in developing the solar system.
For this purpose, ESA envisages the first of a total of three L missions of the "Cosmic Vision" program.
The "Juice" (Jupiter Icy Moons Explorer) probably begins its journey in 2022.
Eight years later, it reaches Jupiter and is to study the planet's atmosphere as well as the icy moons Europa, Kallisto and Ganymede.
What are the basic physical laws of the universe?
The universe offers many opportunities to study matter under extreme conditions and thus to investigate whether the laws of nature known to us are still valid there.
There may also be deviations that could show physicists the way towards a new theory under whose roof all known laws of nature can be combined.
How did the universe originate and what does it consist of?
13.7 billion years ago, our cosmos arose due to the Big Bang.
In addition to the matter familiar to us, from which stars, planets, and living things like us consist, there is dark matter whose gravity holds galaxies and galaxy clusters together, and dark energy, through whose action the expansion of the universe accelerates.
Until now, the researchers don’t know which physical laws were validated in the first fractions of the Big Bang, nor what dark matter and dark energy consist.
With "Euclid", Esa has already approved an M mission here, the launch of which is scheduled for 2020.
With a special telescope, the probe is to investigate the distribution of matter in the universe for the first time in the entire sky and thus enable conclusions about the nature of dark matter and dark energy.
who could make the race
Of the 30 proposals, only two will be left at the end of the selection process.
According to many experts, "E-Lisa", a sophisticated concept for detecting gravitational waves, has the best chances of approval as an L-mission.
By this, physicists mean changes in the structure of space-time that Albert Einstein predicted almost 100 years ago.
To this day, they have not been proven.
Elisa could do that, the scientists behind the project hope.
The mission envisages a detector consisting of two or three probes that is stationed in space.
In contrast to plants on Earth, it could also detect gravitational waves that arose at the Big Bang and thus provide new insights into the formation of the cosmos.
Four other proposals are still competing for the second L mission: a large X-ray telescope called "Athena," which, among other things, is to explore black holes, "Icy Planets," another mission to the solar system's outer planets, "Prism," a mission to measure cosmic background radiation, and the "Exoplanet Finder," which is supposed to detect and study Earth-like planets.
Among other things, proposals for sample return transport from Mars, exploration of the moon, Venus and asteroids as well as solar physics have already been eliminated.
Snowden ready to 'cooperate' with Germany regarding U.S. surveillance
Edward Snowden, the whistleblower of the U.S. intelligence agency, has stated that he is willing to travel to Berlin and testify before the German Bundestag if the U.S. National Security Agency and its director Keith Alexander do not provide answers about their activities.
German MP Hans-Christian Ströbele met Snowden on Thursday in Russia, where he received asylum to discuss with him the statement in Germany.
In a letter from Snowden, which was presented by Ströbele to the media in Berlin on Friday, he writes: “Even if the result of my efforts was demonstrably positive, my government continues to treat a dissenting opinion as a breach of allegiation and tries to criminalize political expression with the threat of punishment, in which no defense is possible.”
However, telling the truth is not a crime.
In the letter, Snowden writes that he is convinced that supporting the international community could convince the American government to drop the charges against him.
The indictment filed by the U.S. Department of Justice includes espionage and theft of state property.
German Interior Minister Hans-Peter Friedrich told Zeit Online: “If Mr. Snowden is ready to talk to German representatives, we will find ways to make this possible.”
U.S.-Germany relations are strained by claims that the NSA has wiretapped Chancellor Angela Merkel's mobile phone.
Thomas Oppermann, the deputy who chairs the parliamentary committee responsible for the secret service, said it should take the opportunity to listen to Snowden as a witness, if possible, "without jeopardizing it and completely ruining relations with the United States."
Ströbele, a member of the Greens, posted a picture of himself and Snowden in his Twitter feed.
During his visit to Russia, he was accompanied by two German journalists.
Ströbele explained that according to the lawyer of the former NSA employee Snowden could not return to Russia if he left the country.
If Snowden testifies in Germany, then he needs an assurance that he is “safe” there, according to the MP.
Snowden declares in his letter that he would be exposed to a “serious and ongoing” campaign of persecution that drove him out of his homeland.
However, he was touched by the worldwide reaction to “my act of political expression.”
Citizens around the world, as well as high-ranking politicians – including in the United States – have described the revelations about an uncontrolled system of comprehensive surveillance as a merit to the general public.
In his letter, Snowden offers the German authorities an offer “when the difficulties surrounding the humanitarian situation have been resolved.”
Frontier Airlines charges fees for carry-on baggage
Frontier Airlines plans to charge up to $100 from passengers wishing to stow carry-on luggage on their flight.
Frontier Airlines intends to charge up to $100 for $100 for a piece of carry-on and $2 for coffee or lemonade, even if it was stated in Wednesday's announcement that passengers were allowed to keep the whole can.
The new fee applies to carry-on luggage in the luggage compartment above the seat, small bags under the seat are still free.
Frontier said it calculates $25 if the fee is paid in advance and $100 for travelers who only paid at the gate.
Frontier spokeswoman Kate O'Malley said the $100 fee was aimed at moving travellers to take care of the payment in advance.
“We don’t want to calculate this amount,” she said.
Airlines began charging fees for the first and second checked baggage in 2008.
Passengers who tried to circumvent them have therefore stuffed as much as possible into carry-on luggage, which means that the luggage compartments above the seat are often crowded.
Fees are a way to persuade passengers to take less on board.
O'Malley said the new fee isn't really about making money.
Frontier’s most loyal customers, who have clearly expressed that it is becoming increasingly difficult to find space in the compartment above the seat.
Passengers who purchase your tickets on the airline's website do not have to pay.
This means that a passenger may be allowed to take a piece of luggage free of charge at Frontier’s gate, but the next person may have to pay $100 for a comparable piece of luggage.
O'Malley said Frontier's website and check-in procedures would be changed, allowing passengers to be notified of the fee before they came to the gate.
Frontier's new carry-on luggage fee won't come into effect until the summer, an exact date doesn't yet.
Passengers often scold about baggage surcharges and other fees, but airlines like to resort to it.
They argue that transporting the luggage costs money and passengers should pay for that service if they desired it.
Many on Wall Street view the new baggage fees as a sign that airlines are taking enough money to cover the cost of air travel after years of losses.
Most, however so far, leave the fingers of fees for carry-on luggage.
Spirit Airlines Inc. demanded fees for carry-on baggage three years ago as the first, followed by Allegiant Air, also a low-cost airline.
The only other airline with such a fee is the Hungarian Wizz Air, said airline consultant Jay Sorensen, who is closely following the additional fees.
He estimated in a December 2011 report that the carry-on baggage fee brings in Spirit $50 million annually.
Sorensen, who formerly held a leadership position at Midwest Airlines, has recently flowned with Spirit and was curious about what would await him at the gear if passengers were confronted with the company's unusual carry-on luggage fee.
“The boarding process was one of the smoothest I’ve experienced in my aviation career,” he said.
I was expecting to see gnashing of teeth and palpable discussions on the gate.
"The plane was full," he explained, "and the boarding went like lubricated."
Frontier also follows Spirit at the £2 charged for coffee, tea, lemonade or juice.
The company explained that passengers who bought lemonade or juice were allowed to keep the whole tin and coffee would be refilled for free.
Water continues to be free.
U.S. Airways briefly attempted to demand money for soft drinks in 2008, but reversed this seven months later after passengers complained and no other large company followed suit.
Frontier's move to levies on carry-on baggage if passengers don't buy directly from the airline is the latest initiative designed to steer customers onto their own website.
Airlines pay online travel agencies such as €10 to £25 per ticket sold.
This provides all companies with an incentive to persuade passengers to buy directly from them instead of via an online travel agency.
Frontier, however, has gone the furthest in this area.
In September, the company began awarding only half as many frequent-flyer miles when booking through an online travel agency.
On Wednesday, the miles were cut once again to 25 percent of the flight route.
An air trip booked by a third-party provider with Frontier over 1000 miles therefore only earns customers 250 miles.
Passengers can also only reserve seats when purchasing directly on the Frontier website.
Frontier owns a loyal customer base at its headquarters in Denver, but the business is shrinking and the company is losing money.
Revenue declined 9 percent and flight capacity shrank nearly 13 percent in the first-quarter, according to the financial report released on Wednesday by its parent company, Republic Airways Holdings Inc.
Republic has been trying to get to get to grip Frontier's finances as part of the company's sale.
Proverbs come from the Bible
More than 40 women attended this year’s last women’s breakfast in the Evangelical parish of Bisingen.
Topic of the day in the parish hall was "Proverbs from the Bible" and "idioms from the Middle Ages."
Zita Köhler, the chairwoman of the parish council, addressed the biblical proverbs in a lecture after breakfast.
"Proverbs contain ways of life, regulations or warnings," she explained.
She compared several proverbs to the corresponding Bible passages and explained the meaning.
She called proverbits such as: Wood-eyed is vigilant as scales fall from the eyes, cast an eye on someone, the gentleman in his sleep, washing his hands in innocence.
On several occasions, the speaker also let her listeners guess at which saying it was.
Christel Dehner took on the idioms from the Middle Ages: all good things are three, making blue, somewhat spankened.
She explained the significance and origin of the idioms, which she called "bridges to the past."
Finally, a tombola was raffled.
The prizes were covered on a table and were humorously circumscribed before being handed over to the winners.
Mayoral candidate Roman Waizenegger also mixed among the visitors to the breakfast round.
Learning instead of unemployed: pilot project for unskilled in tourism
A pilot project for the tourism industry is starting at the Mecklenburg Lake District today.
Under the leadership of the Federal Employment Agency, 49 women and men who already work as unskilled persons in the industry are to be qualified as specialists for hotels and restaurants over three winters.
"What's new is that the six-month courses end with degrees," said Carmen Wiechert of the Neubrandenburg employment agency.
The benefits are varied: the participants would not become unemployed, the companies would get trained specialists, who were increasingly lacking in hotels and restaurants, and the agency did not have to finance unemployment.
The Chamber of Industry and Commerce in Neubrandenburg and the German Hotel and Restaurant Association (Dehoga) Mecklenburg-Vorpommern are also involved in the project.
NSA declares an "internal mistake" and not hackers are to blame for the website's crash
The shadowy National Security Agency stated late Friday that an error had paralyzed its public website for a few hours rather than hackers, as was claimed online.
“NSA.gov was unavailable for a few hours tonight because an internal error occurred during a planned update,” the spy agency explained in an e-mail message.
The problem will be fixed this evening.
The allegation that the failure was caused by a distributed denial of service (DDoS) was not true.
In the early evening hours, online server trackers noticed that the NSA website was unavailable for at least six hours and this is still not for some users.
Previously, a spokesman for the NSA told ABC News that the agency’s sensitive internal network was “in no way” compromised.
Secretial information is not in danger, according to the spokesman.
At least one group of hacktivists claimed to have paralyzed the NSA website with a DDoS attack.
With such attacks, the target site is flooded with page views until the servers are overloaded and the site collapses.
Cybertactics are relatively simple and the attacks do not serve to penetrate the internal network of the target system.
The formerly super-secret NSA, whose nickname was once No Such Agency (No such authority), now finds itself in the bright light of the public and faces sharp criticism after the revelations that have become known in recent months about their extensive surveillance program at home and abroad – a result of the secret NSA data that was stolen and published by the disillusioned former NSA employee Edward Snowden.
The growing controversy surrounding the secret service fueled initial speculations that the incident today was the result of a targeted cyper operation.
The number of people without a job has fallen slightly in October by 22 to 1,307.
At 3.1 percent, the ratio is better than last year and also better than in September, "we would have hoped for more," says Monika Felder-Bauer, deputy head of the employment agency in Sonthofen.
Because numerous companies have reacted so far cautiously to attitudes.
One reason for this, according to Felder-Bauer is: "We have hardly any skilled workers in craftsmanship, healthcare and elderly care".
Due to seasons, vacancies for staff in the hotel and restaurant industry have also been available since September.
Mid-December, the winter season begins there.
You can find the full background report in the Allgäu display sheet from 31.10.2013 (page 33).
Bombardier's loss of profits after decline in delivery figures and orders
Canadian aircraft and railmaker Bombardier Inc reported a 15 percent drop in net profit on Thursday after coming under pressure from declining orders and deliveries on third-quarter planes as well as contract problems in the railway division.
Bombardier, based in Montreal, also did not release flight test dates for its brand new CSeries aircraft and did not provide any new information on whether the aircraft could go into commercial use next September according to the ambitious schedule.
So far, only three more take-offs have followed the maiden flight of the test aircraft one and a half months agove, which makes it seem questionable whether the test phase will proceed as planned.
The results fell short of forecasts, leading to an over-eight percent price crash on the Toronto Stock Exchange.
Cameron Doerksen, an analyst at National Bank Financial on Thursday lowered his rating from “outperform” to “sector perform” in view that the stock has limited price potential over the next one to two quarters.
“While weaker delivery figures for aircraft were largely expected, we are clearly disappointed by the development of margins in the transport sector,” said Doerksen in a customer letter.
We believe Bombardier will receive new orders for the CSeries as the testing program advances for the aircraft.
However, if no new orders are announced in the coming months, we expect the market to become more skeptical of the program.
Bombardier hopes the CSeries aircraft series can catapult the company into the lower market segment, which is currently dominated by Boeing and Airbus.
The first test aircraft was unveiled to the public in March and flew for the first time in September after months of delays.
However, so far, binding orders for the CSeries are moderate with 177, as potential buyers are waiting for the results of the flight tests and want to see if the company's claims regarding fuel efficiency and cost-saving potential of the new jet aircraft are accurate.
There are currently a total of 403 orders and commitments from 15 customers and companies.
Pierre Beaudoin is confident that Bombardier will achieve its target of 300 binding orders at the beginning of commercial use of the jet.
On Thursday, the company’s management also assured analysts and media that the program was in the schedule.
“The test aircraft didn’t stay on the ground any longer than expected,” Beaudoin said on a conference call, adding that during the plane’s deployment, ground tests and software updates had been scheduled.
Each manufacturer plans differently.
We have decided to make a first flight and then attach a revision period, which we have done.
This happens during the entire flight programme.
The second of five test aircraft is expected to start in the next few weeks, the rest shortly thereafter.
Nevertheless, analysts remain sceptical about whether twelve months after the maiden flight, the first customer can commission a CSeries aircraft.
Bombardier said it is reviewinging planning for commissioning (EIS) and will update it over the next few months.
“The slow advance of the flight test – even if they obviously correspond to Bombardier’s internal planning – reinforces our view that commissioning will be postponed to the first quarter of 2015,” Doerksen said.
In the third-quarter at the end of September 30, Bombardier's net profit fell to $147 million, or 8 cents per share from $172 million, or 9 cents a year earlier.
Adjusted earnings per share are unchanged 9 cents.
Sales declined marginally and fell from $4.2 billion to $4.1 billion.
Analysts had expected earnings of 10 cents per share and $4.56 billion in revenue, according to Thomson Reuters I/B/E/S.
The world's fourth-largest aircraft manufacturer says it delivered 45 aircraft this quarter, a decline of 57 last year.
Net orders fell from 83 aircraft to 26.
The order backlog in the aerospace division was $32.9 billion on September 30, unchanged from December 31.
“In the aerospace sector, the results were within the scope of our specifications, but the low order intake and the overall market conditions were disappointing,” Beaudoin said.
Revenue in the aerospace division fell 13 percent to two billion dollars.
Bombardier, the world's largest railcraft manufacturer, announced that sales in this division have risen by almost 11 percent to $2.1 billion.
The order backlog in the transport division amounted to $32.6 billion on September 30, slightly increased compared to December 31.
The profit margin in the transport sector has been affected by settlement problems for some major contracts.
According to the company management, there will be new guidelines for the fourth quarter.
In afternoon trading on Thursday, Bombardier shares fell 8.5 percent and stood at $4.83 after it was also announced that Google Chief Financial Officer Patrick Pichette would move to the board.
Brazil's Embraer SA, the world's third-largest commercial aircraft maker and Bombardier's narrowest rival, reported a ten percent drop in quarterly profit on Thursday.
U.S.: Shots fired at Los Angeles airport
An unidentiary fired shots at LAX airport on Friday morning (local time).
While the airport management said on Twitter that the shooter was in custody, the Los Angeles Times reported that the man was dead.
A security guard was also killed.
Media also reported several people injured.
U.S. President Barack Obama is constantly informed about the situation, White House spokesman Jay Carney said.
The incident took place in Terminal 3. Eyewitnesses claimed to have seen a gunman with a rifle in one of the departure halls, according to several media outlets.
One traveler told CNN that many people had sought shelter in a panic.
Travellers would have screamed and children were crying.
Panic had broken out among those waiting, many had hidden behind chairs out of fear.
He has heard two shots, says a traveler to the Los Angeles Times.
An employee of the TSA has suffered a gunshot wound to the foot.
The scene was "like in a movie."
After that, he was taken to a security area himself, the man reported.
The shots are said to have fallen near a security control area.
Airport building evacuated
Televice pictures showed numerous ambulances and police vans driving in front of a terminal.
Hundreds of people were taken to safety from the building.
Users on Twitter posted photos of a special operations squad and a rifle lying on the ground.
As the airport management announced via Twitter, the incident began at 9.30 in the morning (local time).
The access roads were closed, resulting in long backwaters, according to CNN.
With the exception of landings, the air traffic had been temporarily stopped, it said.
However, on CNN, several planes could also be observed at launch.
Terminal 3 is mainly served by smaller U.S. airlines.
LAX's Los Angeles Airport is one of the U.S.'s largest airport.
Cocaine-addicted lawyer who warned drug lord of police investigation must go to prison
Basharat Ditta, 42, leaked information to underworld great Neil Scarborough
The lawyer was terrified his secret drug addiction might come to light
He was sentenced to three years in prison by Liverpool's Crown Court
A well-known criminal defense lawyer who warned a drug lord about a major police investigation because he was afraid his secret drug addiction could be uncovered has been sentenceded to three years in prison.
Basharat Ditta, 42, provided underworld great Neil Scarborough with sensitive information about investigations into his activities in drug trafficking after being compromised by his own cocaine addiction.
The lawyer nicknamed “Bash” by criminalists was arrested at his home in 2011 after police oversee Scarborough, whom he had represented in a previous drug trial.
Officers watched as Scarborough (32) delivered three bags of cocaine at the lawyer's house in Blackburn, Lancashire, while the latter was with colleagues at a dinner by the bar.
Investigations revealed that Ditta regularly consumed Class A drugs after traces of cocaine had been founded in his hairs, on his wallet and on credit cards.
Over an eight-month period between January and August 2011, he attempted unauthorised to procure information about the arrest of two men on behalf of Scarborough and one of his partners.
All four suspects were supervised by police at the time as part of a major investigative into heroin and cocaine trafficking in Lancashire, Cumbria, Merseyside, Berkshire and West Yorkshire.
Together with 32 other people, they were later arrested after police secured heroin and cocaine worth £1.5 million worth £1.5 million as well as £200,000 in cash during a series of house searches.
Ditta, 42, leaked information to the criminals because he feared his drug addiction could become public.
Today, Ditta, who works for Forbes Solicitor's law firm in Blackburn, was found guilty and convicted at Liverpool's Crown Court following a three-week trial on two counts of obstructing justice.
He admitted cocaine possession during a previous survey.
The lawyer was convicted after the police found out in their investigations against Scarborough that he had been in regular telephone contact with Ditta in February 2011.
Two investigating officers followed the suspect and saw him go to Ditta's house and put the drugs with a purity of 60 per cent in a black golf glove under the lawyer's rubbish bin.
Shortly after delivering the drugs, Scarborough regularly phoned Ditta, who was on dinner at the Blackburn Rovers football stadium in Ewood Park.
The lawyer returned home, retrieved the drugs and then nine notices were exchanged between the two.
In court, it was revealed that Ditta was a "regular consumer" of cocaine after Class A drugs had been discovered in his hairs, his wallet and credit cards.
Ditta was later arresteded, but denied taking cocaine and said he had spoken to the alleged dealer because of being his client, claiming that their discussion was subject to “lawyer secrets.”
During his arrest, Ditta took his wallet and tried to remove a number of credit cards, but they were secured and a hair sample was taken.
During police interrogation, he stated that he was running an office both at his private address and at the workplace and clients would call him at home for legal matters.
In court, it was revealed that he had invoked key drug trafficking actors, some of whom he had previously represented, after momentous arrests and informed of what investigators knew about them.
Prosecutor Anne Whyte said, "A criminal lawyer should know better than everyone else that you don't break the law."
Ditta is indicted abusing his position as a criminal defense attorney, as he has engaged too much with certain clients.
The relationship in question is not simply that of a drug trafficker, but of a drug trafficker who provides his lawyer with drugs.
A part of his communications was undoubtedly legitimate, because he was the lawyer of these individuals.
Yet that went far beyond the boundaries of a normal relationship between lawyer and client.
He counteracted the police investigation as much as possible so that they could continue their criminal activities.
Mr Ditta thus discredited his profession.
He was too close to certain clients, in particular Scarborough, and he allowed his independence to be compromised.
Ditta dismissed any misconduct, claiming, "If I were a corrupt lawyer, which I'm not, and wanted to share information with Scarborough, I wouldn't wait 15 hours, I'd do so immediately."
However, after the hearing, Superintendent Lee Halstead of the Lancashire police said: "Mr. Ditta took the move from a criminal defense lawyer to a criminal at the moment he started acquiring drugs from organized criminals."
His cocaine addiction led to a hopeless compromisation and made him receptive to the motives of leading members of organized crime who instructed him to gather valuable information about police investigations.
Lawyers must maintain the highest standards of integrity and ensure trust and confidence in the public.
Ditta betrayed that trust and tried to entrench himself behind the façade of his profession.
Lancashire's Serious and Organised Crime Unit spearheaded investigations against Ditta, which also resulted in conviction in three counts of possession of cocaine and impedimentiary, underlining our determination to bring criminals to justice.
This case should be a warning to criminals and shows that no one can escape the arm of law.
We'll find you and bring you to court.
Scarborough himself was sentenceded to 14 years in prison after pleading guilty to conspiracy of heroin, cocaine and cannabis trafficking.
Thirty-five other people involved in drug trafficking have been sentenced to a total sentence of 153 years for drug offences.
On his website, Ditta published an area with questions and answers about himself, in which he stated that his dream job was to responsibate as a lawyer client on death row in America, his ultimate guest at dinner was Mohammed Ali and inequality was the motivation for his work.
Revolutionary work of church music
At the end of the festivities on the occasion of its 160th anniversary, the Liederkranz Dunningen will frame two services with the "German Mass" by Franz Schubert.
After the great success of the musical "Rock my Life", which thrilled the audience in the spring of this year, at the end of the jubilee year, will enrich two services with the performance of Franz Schubert's Deutsche Messe.
On Saturday, October 26, this extraordinary Mess will be heard from 19 during the Evening Mess at St. Martin's Church Dunningen and on Sunday, October 27, from 10.15 a.m. at the service at St. John's Baptist Church in Lackendorf.
The so-called Deutsche Messe with the original title "Chants for the Celebration of the Holy Sacrifice of the Mass", D 872, is a sacred musical work by the composer Franz Schubert from 1826 and was considered almost revolutionary at the time of its creation.
The Mass will be listed as part of the divine services, as the client Johann Philipp Neumann had in mind.
Under the direction of Hermann Schneider, the choir is accompanied by the brass group of the Musikverein Frohsinn Tennenbronn and Noemi Lokodi on the organ.
Haslach: Skepticism softens confidence
With the elections to the parish council in 2015, for example, in the pastoral unit Haslach (SE), fundamentals will change.
There is then only one common parish council for all six parishes.
In a joint meeting on Saturday, the committees from Haslach, Hofstetten, Mühlenbach, Fischerbach, Steinach and Welschensteinach prepared for the changes.
After all, there is nothing to be changed about the fundamental decisions of the diocese and it is up to the parish councils to implement them as satisfactorily as possible.
In the course of the day it became particularly clear: the skepticism towards the new hesitantly but increasingly gives way to confidence.
From the ideas and expectations of the innovations in 2015 to concrete proposals about the future composition and the size of the parish council, the extremely constructive discussions of the six committees with the main officials of the pastoral care team ranged.
On the basis of a presentation, Regional Dean Georg Schmitt explained the revised guidelines for pastoral units in the Archdiocese of Freiburg.
Subsequently, SE Haslach, as a church congregation, will in future form a body under public law, which enters into the rights and obligations of the six individual parishes.
There will be only one common parish council and one board of trustees, in which the pastor Kraft Amtes is represented.
At least two elected members from each parish are to be represented, and so-called community teams are formed on site in the six parishes.
Your task in the future will be to promote church life in the place.
In the question of the finances of the individual parish, there was a considerable need for discussion.
The innovations stipulate that accumulated credits in the parishes will be preserved earmarked.
Liabilities, on the other hand, are transferred to the entire community.
Especially with regard to the large planned investments, the commitment of which all parishes then meet, there were great concerns.
"Even in the past, investments were only invested in projects that could be mastered by the individual parishes on their own," Hanslach's parish council chairman Bruno Prinzbach said.
Practical experience with a joint committee for five parishes was described by Barbar Ritter, the chairwoman of the committee in the Schutterwald-Neuried pastoral unit.
They had set themselves the task in 2006, "Become one, stay five" and had to realize that this is not so easy.
The committees of the Haslach pastoral unit then set out to answer the questions about the future composition and size of the joint parish council as well as the practical work of the parish teams.
The elaborations will be clarified in further meetings and decided at a further joint meeting of all six committees next spring.
In the end, parish councillor Michael Schöner from Steinach presented the new logo of the pastoral unit, which was approved by a clear majority.
In Pfullendorf, the electricity becomes more expensive
The citizens of Pfullendorf have to prepare for higher electricity costs.
As the managing director of Stadtwerke, Jörg-Arne Bias, confirmed to the SÜDKURIER, a four-member Pfullendorf family will pay 70 to 90 euros additional costs per year.
The main reason for the upcoming price increase is the increase of the so-called EEG surcharge from 5.277 to 6.3 cents per kilowatt hour.
This surcharge is re-established every year in October by the four operators of the major power lines.
These quantify the additional costs incurred by them from the energy transition, which they can levy through a levy above the price of electricity.
Stadtwerke Pfullendorf forms the last link in this chain.
The supervisory board of the energy company had even assumed a higher surcharge in its forecasts, as Jörg-Arne Bias reports.
Now the specific figures would have to be calculated before municipal utilities could inform their customers about the exact price increase next week.
"We expect an increase of 1 cent plus x," says Bias, although the figure will be rather close to 2 cents.
Traffic lights on permanent red: Pay attention to waiting time
Sometimes a traffic light just doesn't turn green.
The contact loop in the road does not react, or the light sign is defective.
Then what to done - drive at red?
Has waited long enough at a red traffic light and the intersection is free, he can continue driving, explains the Stuttgart lawyer Ralf Becker in the magazine "Motorrad".
However, the waiting time must be "appropriate", which could be interpreted differently from case to case.
Becker advises to endure at least five minutes before a malfunction of the traffic light is assumed.
However, anyone who continues the journey despite red lights must be aware of the fact that they are fully responsible for an accident caused thereby.
If you wait less than five minutes, you may not wait long enough, warns Becker and refers to a decision of the Higher Regional Court of Hamm.
In the negotiated case, a driver had ignored the traffic light after about three minutes of continuous red and had to answer for a negligent red light violation.
The traffic light was not defective, but jumped to green a little later.
A driving ban was spared him because of the special situation - but not the fine.
local council is pleased with solid budget
Whenever municipal council meetings are about the budget, Mayor Ralph Gerster laughs well.
The Gemeindesäckl in Herdwangen-Schönach is well filled and the administration has not known debt since 2005.
In this sense, the financial year 2012 can also be recorded as a complete success.
This became clear at the presentation of the annual accounts for 2012.
As Andrea Rothmund explained, the administrative budget was around €7.6 million higher than one million euros than planned.
The surplus of the administrative budget is also around one million euros higher at around 1.8 million euros.
Thanks to the surplus is mainly higher business tax revenues and the municipal share of the income tax.
The wealth budget was again well above the planned values.
Instead of the €1.5 million planned, it amounted to €2.5 million last year.
"This is mainly due to the higher funding from the administrative budget," Rothmund said.
Since fewer investments were made than planned in the financial year 2012, the allocation of reserves was also higher.
In total, the general reserve was increased by around €2.1 million to now a total of €4.9 million.
Mayor Ralph Gerster thanked Rothmund and her team for their good work.
"You don't just shake a work like that out of your sleeve," Gerster praised with a view to the annual accounts.
He and the local councillors were visibly pleased with the good numbers.
It may sound paradoxical, but it is precisely these good numbers that the citizens could soon come to stand dearly.
Due to the good financial situation, the municipality was able to do without high lifting rates in the past.
As a result, according to Gerster, however, grants have often been reduced by the country.
There are prerequisites for these grants from various compensatory sticks.
Among other things, the cities and municipalities that apply for grants must have certain lifting rates.
"We have to look at the issue of lifting rates, as there will be investments in the coming years, for which we would like to have grants," Gerster announced in this context.
Nagold: Hugo Wermter in the choir for 60 years
At the age of 60 as a singer, Hubert Wermter’s history at the Men’s Singing Club Cäcilia is almost twice as long as that of the autumn festival, during which he was solemnly honoured.
Hubert Wermter stood as usual in the colors of his male singing club on stage.
Even after 60 years he still enjoys singing with the choir, which is now probably more than beloved.
Wermter was also active in the association, among others for 28 years as a member of the committee.
Jörg Kohr, pastoral officer on behalf of the Cäcilienverband Rottenburg-Stuttgart, handed him - since the choir is also a church choir - a certificate from the association and an honorary letter signed by Bishop Gebhard Fürst.
His fellow singers presented him with the certificate of the German choir association as well as an engraved tin plate.
In addition to the honor, the 31st autumn festival ran in the usual ways: With the MGV Betra, the Liederkranz Salzstetten, the singers' department Baisingen and the local Musikverein, the Vollmaringer had invited a colorful mixture of different choirs and singing styles, which entertained the approximately 400 visitors.
The MGV Vollmaringen opened with atmospheric songs such as "Im Weinparadies" and "Lustig, her brothers".
They prepared a small serenade for their honorary singer Hubert Wermter - Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart's "Bundeslied".
The men's singing club Betra also sang classical choral songs such as "Jägerwerben" by Julius Wengert, "Weit, far away" by Hubert Goisern as well as the folk song "Wann du durch durch durchs Tal".
The Liederkranz Salzstetten provided with its mixed choir and its women's group - the "Impulschor" - for the women's quota of the festival.
The mixed chorus kidnapped "Santa Lucia" and "Eviva Espana" by Antos Gus in foreign countries with the Neapolitan folk style, while the "Impulschor" also resorted to foreign languages: "Liberatio" and "Hey Jude" belonged to her program.
The singing department of the Baisinger Sportverein came back with "Was ische der Schwob?" by Hans Süssmuth and Robert Pappert's "Bierlied" back to local fields, where the Vollmaringer Musikverein offered the crowning musical conclusion.
For the children there was a small side program - "Grandma Hanne" aka Hannelore Stollsteimer performed a Kasperltheater and read a few stories, which kept the children busy at noon.
Children's dreams come true
The kindergartens and day-care centers in Zollernalbkreis are getting mail these days.
In cooperation with Bauhaus and Sparkasse Zollernalb, the Black Forest messenger wants to support the facilities in the realization of their construction and beautification wishes.
Does kindergarten need a new sandbox?
All kindergartens, kindergartens and crèches from the entire Zollernalb district can participate in the campaign.
If you have a construction or beautification request for your facility, you can apply to the Black Forest messenger.
Be creative with your children and surprise the jury of the aktion Kindergarten-Baustelle.
A jury consisting of representatives of Schwarzwälder Bote, Bauhaus and Sparkasse Zollernalb selects three wishes from all submissions.
What are the prerequisites for participation?
The desire should be realized with building material worth a maximum of 2000 euros and on an (action) day.
In addition, the winner himself must provide for many hard-working construction workers.
What's with the day of action and the helpers?
With the support of Sparkasse Zollernalb, Bauhaus provides the material.
It will be delivered to the kindergarten on the agreed day and should then be processed directly by the helpers.
Helpers can be parents, grandparents, friends, clubs and, of course, the educators and children themselves.
Kenyan press outraged over controversial media law
“We are in a frightening position and one may ask oneself: What is stopping Parliament from simply abolishing judicial independence tomorrow?” was read in the newspaper, which considers the bill not constitutional.
“The law is draconian and very punitive and we reject it,” said Cyrus Kamau, managing director of Capital Group, which also includes CapitalFM, one of Kenya’s most respected independent radio stations and news websites.
He said the new media tribunal "will always be biased, because it's an extended arm of the government," and restrictions on content and advertising would damage Kenya's position in the global economy.
"I hope the president listens to us and we are appealing to him to reject this bill and send it back to Parliament," he said.
According to The Star newspaper, through the new law, the government is “effectively targeting the media, while the Standard writes that democracy and freedom of speech in Kenya have suffered “a serious blow” and titled the draft “draconian.”
The adoption of the law is among a series of measures designed to strengthen national security in the aftermath of the attacks by armed Islamists on the Westgate shopping centre in September.
The Kenyan media attracted the wrath of the authorities because they leaked footage from security cameras showing the units dispatched to the crime scene allegedly robbed the luxurious shopping centre.
Police Chief David Kimaiyo responded by summoning two journalists and a leading media representative to interrogation, even if the subpoena was withdrawn following an outcry from the media.
Under the new law, media houses can be fined up to 20 million Kenyan shillings and individual journalists with up to one million as well as the further risk of losing their professional recognition or being excluded from official press accreditation.
The tribunal also has the right to confiscate the property of those affected if a penalty is not paid.
According to the Daily Nation, “already a single penalty can push most radio stations into bankruptcy.”
In addition, the newspaper considers that these measures could have a damning effect on Kenya’s “livable blogosphere”.
If they silence the media, politicians could do whatever they want with impunity.
“No one will ever experience it,” wrote Mutuma Mathiu, a journalist with the nation who describes the Kenyan media as a pivotal control factor in public life.
“Leaving politicians to themselves, they lead the country into bankruptcy and us going back to the time of hunter-gatherers,” he said.
Kenyan lawmakers have been attracting public anger in the past.
In May, they agreed to reverse the cuts ordered by the National Compensation Commission and re-enacted their hefty salaries of about 532,000 shillings tax-free per mon-month - they are among the world's tallest.
Bad-Dürrheim: A dream comes true for FC
Despite the cold, the official inauguration ceremony for the artificial turf sports field did not lose the importance for the city and the football club.
Everyone had probably wished for a more pleasant celebration last Saturday, but Peter did not want to play along.
So all those who came without an umbrella or couldn’t hold one for any reason.
This happened to the musicians who framed the ceremony, just as many a speaker.
That’s why FC chairman Albrecht Schlenker, who spoke about the fulfillment of a dream, got wet.
Above all, his thanks went to Mayor Walter Klumpp, who was strongly committed to the implementation of the project, the municipal council, the participating companies and members of the association who contributed.
Lothar Held, Paul Weizenegger, Heiner Gail and Peter Graf in particular here.
Thefty pensioners laid 400 squares of paving stones for the new paths.
This remarkable voluntary commitment was also honoured by Karl Rombach member of the state parliament.
Further greetings spoke for the municipal sports committee Hubert Baier, the district chairman of the South Baden Football Association, Kuno Kayan, and Friedrich Knorr from the planning office, who announced a donation for the youth department of the club.
Mayor Klumpp recalled that recurring weather-related influences in spring and autumn added to the original turf pitch in such a way that it could not be used.
Already 15 years ago there were the first considerations to solve the problem, when the construction of a third place at the grounds of the football club or at the Realschule am Salinensee was thought.
Both were discarded again.
Six years agove, thoughts arose about the creation of an artificial turf sports field, which was generally regarded as the ultimate solution.
Concrete plans were started two years ago, when the FC took over the Schabelstube in lease, pledged a cost contribution of 100 000 euros for the construction of the square and the state approved subsidies of 104 000 euros.
In October 2012, the municipal council decided to cover the remaining 356 000 euros at the total cost of €560 000.
The start of construction of the 68 by 108-meter artificial turf pitch took place on June 4.
During the three-month construction phase, 3000 cubic meters of earth were removed, half of which was used for the construction of a dirt bike route.
Union and SPD have resumed their coalition negotiations on the issues of home affairs and the judiciary.
In addition, the responsible working group came together in Berlin in the morning.
Among other things, it is about direct democracy, deputies bribery and the federal police.
In the morning, the Migration and Integration working group also wanted to continue its deliberations.
Among other things, it is likely to be about the issue of dual citizenship, in which both sides have contradictory ideas.
The SPD wants to end the compulsion for immigratory children born in Germany to opt for one - the union rejects a double passport.
On the subject of transport, which is not on the agenda this Friday, the SPD underpinned its no to a car toll demanded by the CSU.
A vignette is a "flat rate for frequent driving and thus ecologically counterproductive," explained the SPD negotiator for the subject of transport, Florian Pronold, in a letter to his group.
There was a risk of evasive traffic on rural roads, which were already accident focal points.
The introduction of a toll for all passenger cars would also only be a first step from Pronold’s point of view.
"There is a risk that at a later date the compensation for Germans will be canceled," he said.
