Motorist seriously injured in accident
In an accident on Thursday evening, a 37-year-old car driver from Aachen was seriously injured.
According to the police, a 41-year-old from Müsch was at about 21.15 o'clock with her car on the Kempen outer ring in the direction of Grefrath.
When she wanted to turn left onto St. Töniser Straße, she overlooked the 37-year-old's oncoming carriage.
The vehicles collided.
The Aachen man suffered serious injuries and had to be taken to the hospital for treatment.
Constance: Closure after accident at the Schänzlekreisel
According to police, the accident occurred when a 51-year-old driver of a Swiss Seat Ibiza drove in the left lane of the two-lane Reichenaustraße out of town on Thursday evening, around 8 p.m.
Shortly before the roundabout at Schänzle, he noticed that he was in the left turn lane in the direction of Stromeyersdorfstraße and switched to the right-hand lane.
This resulted in a collision with a 23-year-old Constancer driving BMW.
A 19-year-old's VW Passat behind the BMW was also damaged.
In total, about 15000 property damage was incurred, the police said.
The roadway had to be closed during the accident, it said.
Vienna airport abolishes passenger calls
Mr. Max Maier, please come to Gate 24.
In the future, there will no longer be announcements like these at Vienna-Schwechat Airport.
The majority of loudspeaker announcements are thus omitted - the noise level is to be significantly lowered.
Creating a more relaxed atmosphere is the goal of this innovation.
Airport spokesman Peter Kleemann told Radio Wien, Vienna Airport is following an international trend with the discontinuation of announcements affecting individuals.
For example, the airports in Copenhagen, Frankfurt or Dubai are already abandoning the noisy appeals.
Due to the elimination of these announcements - on average, around 200 personal calls through the loudspeakers in one day - it should be quieter at the airport in the future.
"It has also been shown in experience that these individual announcements were perceived relatively little, but at the same time all the other information about changed flyings or departure times a bit are lost here," Kleemann said in an interview with the radio station.
Highlander-Games at the Kaltenhof
In addition to pulling brake cars with tractors, there is also a second competition at the agricultural autumn festival at the Kaltenhof from 6 to 8 September.
On Saturday, September 7th, the Highlander Games will start at 1.30 p.m.
Although they have a Scottish tradition, they are Swabian on the Kaltenhof.
Teams and lone fighters compete in the disciplines of tree trunk throwing, horseshoe throwing and water bucket carrying.
The tree trunk is four and a half meters long and weighs 25 to 30 kilograms.
"It doesn't come down to the distance," explains organizer Peter Saile.
The trunk must be thrown off vertically, is supposed to flip over once and then just come to rest.
That's rated with the most points.
When throwing horseshoes, it depends on the target accuracy.
Throwing at a stake from eight yards away.
Skill and strength must be demonstrated by the participants when carrying water buckets over 50 meters.
Time and amount of water in a vat are measured at the end.
From each "clan" or team, two may then participate in the individual competition.
The winners of the team and individual competition will receive prizes.
Training can be done as early as 10 a.m.
A suitable clothing is desired, for example, the competitors can compete in the Scots skirt.
Swabian costume is also allowed.
For the "Outfit," the jury awards a few more bonus points.
You can register up to an hour before the start of the competition.
With the Scottish-Swabian Highlander games and the tractor-pulling, which starts half an hour earlier, there is already a lot of action on the festival grounds on Saturday.
In the evening, the wind music lovers get their money's worth.
At 7 p.m., the birabohemian brass music from Schömberg designs the big entertainment evening.
The chapel plays brisk polkas and marches.
Her repertoire also includes soulful waltzes and a rich big band sound.
Heinz Koch from Weilen, known as a singing innkeeper and Schwarzwurst baron, will be present.
In Dornhan he has already performed at the Christmas market and the fool's guild.
On Sunday, the Musikverein Leinstetten entertains for Frühschoppen, from 14 o'clock the Bauernkapelle Böffingen is a guest, and the "Oldies" then end the festival.
Kick-off on Friday is the Stoppelacker party with DJ Ralf.
On Saturday and Sunday, other items are the classic car and smuggler meeting, exhibitions of companies on the subject of agriculture and forestry.
Other attractions on Sunday are the maize maze, the craftsman's market and sheaving.
Especially for children there is a petting zoo, straw bouncy castle, tractor driving (under instructions) and tractor surfing.
Organizers are the Förderverein Mehrzweckhalle, the ski club and the tractor friends "Zündkäpsele".
Overview of milk and egg alternatives
Vegan diet requires plant-based alternatives to eggs, milk and dairy products.
Purened tofu with plant cream, for example, is a quark substitute.
The Vegetarians’ Confederation for World Vegan Day on 1 November also mentions others.
On the occasion of World Vegan Day on 1 November, the Vegetarians’ Association Germany proposes a number of vegan alternatives:
Pure plant margarine is a good alternative to butter, yogurt can be replaced by soy yoghurt.
Soy, oat, almond or rice milk can be used instead of milk.
Applicable plant cream replaces conventional whipped cream.
There are plant-based alternatives to eggs as well.
For example, a half crushed, ripe banana can replace an egg as a binder in a cake.
50 grams of applesauce or a tablespoon of ground flaxseed plus three tablespoons of water are also suitable.
For example, doughs become loose instead of eggs with a mixture of a teaspoon of baking soda, a tablespoon of starch and three tablespoons of mineral water.
But 50 grams of soy yoghurt or silk tofu can also fulfill this purpose.
According to Vebu, around 800 000 people are currently eating vegan in Germany, i.e. purely plant-based.
Sleepless in New York
On the way to their host families in Weymouth, Massachusetts, the students of the Black Forest Gymnasium got to know the American city of New York.
After eight hours of flight, we headed straight to the Brooklyn Bridge, which reached the heart of the city on foot.
There was already a fascinating view of the world-famous skyline, which is a special attraction in the evening light.
Due to the time difference, the students were on their feet for almost 24 hours, but true to the motto "The city doesn't sleep," the day in Times Square was decided.
Despite shutdown, on the second day, the Statue of Liberty could be reached with a ferry and climbed on foot.
Subsequently, the financial center was about Wall Street to the memorial of 9/11.
On the third day, students were able to gain an overview of the fascinatingly large city from the 373-metre-high viewing platform of the Empire-State-Building and enjoying the view.
Afterwards it was through the hectic hustle and bustle of New York’s streets across Rockefeller Central Park, which provided an ideal opportunity for a short linger in the autumn sun.
After a short flight from New York to Boston, the students have now arrived in their host families and experience everyday school life at Weymouth High School.
This year, too, a Hubertus Mass will be celebrated on the name day of St. Hubertus in the parish church of St. Mary on Hülsbergstraße.
According to legend, St. Hubertus was a rather ruthless hunter, who was converted by a vision and later appointed bishop of Liège.
The hunting horn brass group of the Jagdreiter Westfalen under the direction of Brigitte Kluth makes music on parforce horns (natural horns in the mood Es) according to old, originally French melodies.
The winders wear riding clothes because they mainly accompany riders, horses and dog crowds on tow hunts musically.
The solemn Mass will take place on Sunday, November 3, at 11.30 a.m.
The redesigned Opschlag shines with a new cafe.
One that invites you to linger and return.
one that wants to make the name a program.
Déjà Vu is, according to lexicon, a psychological phenomenon that manifests itself in the feeling of having experienced or seen a new situation before.
In the case of a visit to the café of the same nam Opschlag in the district town of Kleve within sight of the Rhein-Waal University of Applied Sciences, it does not have to have to stay with the pure Déjà Vu feeling.
Finally, the café with the beautiful name has exactly that as its goal: not only mentally, but to make tangible again and again.
Because it's fun and it's just beautiful.
A look into the inner life at Opschlag 8 gives the viewer a good feeling right away.
The state-of-the-art, but non-cool café ambience looks like a place to feel good.
Here, the team with the founders Mirjam van der Stelt and Daniel Büttner at the helm spoils the guest with various coffee or cocktail variants.
In addition, there are delicious cakes or individually topped fresh baguettes.
