The MGV "Friedrich-Wilhelm" meets for its regular choir rehearsals every Tuesday at 19.45 o'clock in the club premises.
"We are always warmly welcome to singing," says Schlotmann.
UN announces new poverty reduction targets
The United Nations is now starting to develop a number of new goals to replace the Millennium Development Goals announced twelve years ago on poverty reduction.
In the run-up to the U.N. summit on sustainable development that began in Rio de Janeiro this evening, Australian diplomats played a key role in the push for “sustainable development goals” designed to replace the Millennium Goals that expire in 2015.
They have been included in the final draft of the document, which will be adopted by world political leaders, including Ms Gillard, at the summit.
U.N. Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon said in the evening at the summit that the time had come to “think beyond national interests.”
“I am pleased that the member states have agreed to tackle a process with which universal, sustainable development goals are to be agreed,” he said.
“These goals will build on our progress from the Millennium Development Goals and form an integral part of the post-2015 development framework.
I will spare no effort to implement this mandate given to me by the member states in order to realize our vision of the Sustainable Development Goals based on the success of the Millennium Development Goals.”
Even if the SG Achim/Baden has been without a victory for four match days, there is still a good mood in the handball league.
Coach Tomasz Malmon leaves no doubt about that at all.
Everyone continues to be fully motivated in the
Nevertheless, I hope that we finally win again.
"Because I don't even know what such a victory tastes anymore," Malmon expects a sense of success from his team at VfL Fredenbeck II.
Even if the third-league reserve from Fredenbeck has not yet ripped out any trees, Malmon raises his forefinger in advance.
I trained a lot of players myself in the A-Youth.
That’s why they’re going to be particularly motivated against their ex-coach.
"In addition, it is important to wait and see whether there will still be reinforcements from the first," explains the SG coach.
To finally get back on track for success, the Malmon team must definitely improve compared to previous performances.
Especially in the coverage work and in the switching game, it last lashed enormously.
Exploiting the chances must also definitely get better.
Therefore, I hope that my players have finally cleared their heads during the short break.
Mozamby security concerns are growing in the face of clashes between influential personalities
Thousands of people gathered in central Maputo under the watchful eyes of the statue of Samora Machel, the state founder and first president of Mozambique, to voice calls for peace at a rare public demonstration.
"We want peace again, we want stability," said Vanessa de Sousa, managing director of an investment company.
Fearing for the country's future, she traded her business clothes for a T-shirt with the inscription "We demand security" in Portuguese and joined the crowd on the capital's Independence Square on Thursday.
There have been almost daily reports of clashes between government forces and Renamo for two weeks, which are among the worst skirmishes since the peace agreement over 20 years ago.
Renamo was once a notorious rebel movement, initially backed by White-ruled Rhodesia and later South Africa's apartheid government in an effort to destabilize the country's independent government.
Under the 1992 peace treaty, she became an opposition party.
Analysts consider it unlikely that the country will fall back into a full-blown conflict, but recent events have worried foreign investors and locals.
For the fast-growing economy, there is a lot at stake, because the discovery of huge gas deposits off the coast and coal warehouses in the northwest could bring investments of over $50 billion from firms such as Rio Tinto, Vale from Brazil, Eni from Italy and Anadarko from the U.S. into the country over the next few years.
The ruling Frelimo party, the dominant force since 1975, and Renamo blame each other for the tensions.
Renamo claims the government provoked the latest clashes by attacking its members in Sofala province, a traditional stronghold of Renamos, on October 17.
The attacks on the former rebels escalated as government forces attacked Renamo bases and tried to kill Afonso Dhlakama, the group's leader, Renamo's spokesperson explained to the Financial Times.
The government blames Renamo for the clashes and accuses the organisation of assaulting soldiers.
President Armando Guebuza has trieded to downplay concerns about instability.
Speaking to AFP, the French news agency, he said Wednesday that Dhlakama considers himself a "loser" who "wants to use all his remaining forces to prove he can impose his decisions on the government."
Both Frelimo and Renamo stress that they want to avoid a war.
Yet concerns have increased since Mazanga was quoted as saying that Renamo is giving up the 1992 peace agreement.
He told the FT that by which he meanted Frelimo no longer respected the agreement.
“Our vision is to resume negotiations and seriously,” Mazanga said.
Previous talks between the parties have contributed little to settling the tensions fueled by a series of clashes this year.
"We have two grown men here (Guebuza and Dhlakama) who collist their heads," says Joseph Hanlon, a lecturer at Open University and Mozambique expert.
None of them are good at negotiating and none is willing to make the necessary concessions.
Renamo, pushing for electoral reforms, has already said she will boycott local elections in November.
Presidential and parliamentary elections are scheduled for next year.
Some commentators have interpreted the use of force as an attempt by an afflicting movement to obtain concessions and financial perks from the government.
Renamo's electoral successes have been shrinking since 1992, while a newer party, Movimento Democrático de Moçambique (MDM) founded by a former Renamo member, is likely to improve in elections.
Mazanga maintains that Guebuza – whose resignations when his second term ends next year – wants to destroy the country’s democracy.
“He doesn’t want multi-party democracy, he doesn’t want transparent elections, he doesn’t want peace because he doesn’t want to give uprision,” Mazanga said.
It is unclear what capacity Renamo has, but it has carried out unrested attacks on police stations and vehicles on an important north-south connection.
Most battles took place in Sofala province, located a few hundred kilometers north of Maputo, but in which Beira is located, the port that mining companies such as Rio Tinto and Vale use to export coal.
In June, Rio interrupted the use of the railroad for a month after Renamo announced an attack on the track.
Mazanga responded cautiously when asked if Renamo would repeat this threat.
Renamo wanted "to warn the international community that things don't stand well in Mozambique," Mazanga said.
Instability has heightened frustration over the government, says Fernando Lima, head of Mediacoop, an independent media company, because many people were already worried about corruption, sluggish progress in development and a recent wave of kidnapping.
“People think that the government and the president are responsible for the future of the country and he should be the one who finds solutions to the problems,” he explains.
Omar Sultane, a protester, said people simply wanted stability.
“None care about Renamo and Frelimo, they just want peace and usable roads again,” he explained.
Children should be taught myths and legends as “role models for life”, says one author.
Thor's stories showed that "brutal power doesn't have a chance against clever tricks," and the Artus legend demonstrates the importance of having a dream.
However, many of the myths are “far too wild, too offensive and in some cases too dirty to be taught in schools”, which is why Crossley-Holland pleaded for a “careful selection” of age-appropriate works.
“I think it’s wonderful that myths and folklore are already part of education in America,” he said.
I've proposed this as a plan for twenty years.
He added that for writers and teachers who are “excessively didactic” children would “completely switch off”, because in entertaining stories the messages would be conveyed “subliminally”.
Crossley-Holland, who translated Beowulf from Anglo-Saxon and is the author of the Penguin Book of Norse Myth and the British Folk Tales, explained: "You can pursue certain intentions, but you should keep them in the background as possible."
Perhaps the biggest difference between an adult writer who writes for adults and an author who writes for children lies in the need to impart hope.
Not everything needs to be simplifiered or lead to a happy ending, but there is an immanent sense of good and evil.
This must be subliminally portrayed, revealed through history and not explained.
That's the old story of pointing rather than telling stories.
Hansjakob pulls once again into Freihof
100 years ago, Heinrich Hansjakob moved into his retirement residence in Haslach, on Sunday his last years came alive in the "Freihof".
The many spectators were thrilled by the extremely entertaining scene play.
From the arrival to the granting of honorary citizenship to his death, screenwriter and Hansjakob actor Alois Kraftzcyk had drafted an entertaining piece that received much applause under the direction of Cornelia Volk.
Marcus Zagermann took the audience as a speaker through the ten different scenes, explaining correlations and bridging leaps of time from youth to approaching retirement.
On October 22, 1913 Hansjakob comes home to his children's sky, in his Freihof.
And then the scene game began, in which Alois Krafczyk shined once again in his parade role as the city's great son.
He proceeded in style with the two-horse and "Schwarzwälder Füchsen" by Erich Becherer from Mühlenbach.
Not only he was received by the audience with much applause, his sister Philippine also welcomed him.
Billy Sum-Hermann knew it incredibly well to put herself into the sister's role and gave her tremendous life in facial expressions and gesturology.
Between the individual playing scenes, the Mühlenbacher Bauernkapelle played suitably selected pieces of music and rounded off the performance.
Haslach’s mayor Heinz Winkler gave himself the honor to the award of honorary citizenship, who, in representation of the then incumbent Hättich, together with a part of the city council made his appearance.
Their wait was then made by the three-king singers, who prompted Hansjakob to the exclamation: "Oh how beautiful, youth memoriations of my own Three Kings come alive again".
With their song "O Jesulein" they delighted the audience in the Freihof as well as the stork day children with their noisy "out, out".
Hansjakob had voiced his criticism of the official church at the time as well as his concerns about the excesses in agriculture, the consequences of industrialization or the effects of war.
In the native earth, behind his burial chapel on the fire near Hofstetten, he found his resting place.
"A restless spirit had finally found its calm and had gone home forever," it said at the end of the scene play.
A lot of applause and many words of praise were given by the audience for the performance.
Basketball: The hope of the Neckar giants grows
The chances of the league stay of the sportingly relegated Ludwigsburg Bundesliga basketball players have increased.
Because if there is a wildcard procedure, then there is now one competitor less for the Neckar giants.
