Snowden could also testify in Russia
Former U.S. intelligence expert Edward Snowden could also make statements in the U.S. spying affair in his Russian asylum.
Representatives of the Federal Prosecutor General's Office could either ask questions in writing or meet the 30-year-old in person in Russia.
This was reported by the Interfax agency, citing unspecified circles that were familiar with the situation.
Such a question could be clarified intergovernmentally, it said.
Snowden's departure from Russia is virtually impossible.
"In this case, he loses his refugee status," the agency quoted its source as saying.
Because Germany is an ally of the USA, Snowden threatens extradition there, it said
Long breath pays off
Six men's teams competed for the winning title at the tennis tournament of the clubs.
In the endance, the endurance of Team Maier/Bronner decided the last match.
A dream weather had received the tennis departmen of Sportfreunde Rohrdorf for the tournament of the local clubs and friends.
The fun of playing tennis was in the foreground of the tournament.
There were five games per team on the schedule, with everyone playing against everyone.
It was played in double mode with a long set up to nine points or a maximum of 45 minutes duration.
Anja Schlichter leads the tournament
The tournament management was in the proven hands of Anja Schlichter, supported by Carmen Müller and Inga Kronemeyer.
After the first three games and a lunch break with pasta, the tournament went into the decisive phase.
Favourites Andre Maier/Matthias Bronner were level on points with Michael Klippel and Sadmin Osmicic (both teams from SG Rohrweiler), so the last two matches had to bring the decision.
At the award ceremony, Carmen Müller was finally able to hand over the hiking trophy to Andre Maier/Matthias Bronner, who had the longest breath on the finish line.
The second place was occupied by the double Michael Klippel/Sadmin Osmicic.
Rainer and Bernd Maier of the Asphaltriecher team reached the bronze podium.
With late afternoon sun and coffee and cake, the successful conclusion of the tournament took place.
On the last weekend in August, the Schwarzwälder open-air museum Vogtsbauernhof in Gutach near Hornberg is once again in children’s hands.
Traditionally, the museum welcomes visitors to a colourful children's and family festival with numerous hands-on activities and events on Saturday and Sunday 24 and 25 August as part of the summer holiday programme.
"Large and small can learn different old crafts on both days," the press release said.
Whether making soaps, turning candles, felting or making ropes, there is something suitable for all ages.
Regional craftsmen from 11 a.m. to 5 p.m. are assisting the children.
Work is also carried out in the museum workshop.
In the construction of cuckoo whistles, water wheels or gyros, the young guests can prove their artisanal skills.
The many old games, such as wheelbarrow races or stilt running, also promise a lot of fun.
Speed and coordination are required here.
For all small detectives, the open-air museum offers a puzzle tour of the museum on Saturday and Sunday, at 12 and 14 o'clock.
On a terrain tour, the children have to guess which stories correspond to the truth or are a lie.
In addition, visitors have the special opportunity to get to know the open-air museum during a carriage ride with Black Forest foxes.
Not only horses, but also many other animals such as sheep, goats, cows and chickens can be found and admired on the grounds.
In addition, all children can look forward to the Clown Otsch on Sunday, who drives his Schabernack from 11 o'clock with the museum guests.
Hermann Büttner, meanwhile, is cosy and fairy-tale-like.
The storyteller takes the little guests into the wonderful world of fairy tales at 11, 13 and 15 o'clock.
In addition, the children's costume dance group from Gutach performs traditional dances at 11.30 a.m.
In addition, numerous craftsmen such as the broom tie, the seamstress, the spinner, the weaver and the baker can be looked over their shoulders on both event days from 11 a.m. to 5 p.m. in their traditional work.
Visitors to the open-air museum can expect a varied holiday programme until the end of the summer holidays in Baden-Württemberg.
In a small New Zealand town, Burt Muro is considered a lovable owl because he planned to take part in a race in America with his motorcycle, built in 1920.
Burt can't dissuade from his dream despite a heart defect.
With his savings, a few donations and a mortgage on his old workshop shed, he transferred by ship to Los Angeles.
After the movie there's coffee, tea and cake.
Shrill beeping disturbs residents
Rhenus Midgard has also invested heavily on land.
They distribute the coal coming from the ships to the heap.
The company has several irons in the fire at the site.
In addition to coal imports, Rhenus Midgard relies on wind turbine logistics.
Black shimmers the hard coal on the storage site in front of the dike.
Two huge blue stockpiling and back-chargers distribute or load the coal here as needed.
With every movement of the transshipment devices, a shrill warning noise sounds over the terrain.
"The signals are intended for safety and are required by law," says Matthias Schrell, managing director of Rhenus Midgard in Wilhelmshaven.
With unfavorable winds, this can unfortunately be heard widely - and so there have already been some complaints from people from the city north.
He takes the complaints very seriously and has therefore contacted those affected, says the 40-year-old.
In close consultation with the authorities, it is now to keep the warning signs quieter with technical measures.
In the future, he will also focus on open dialogue with his neighbors.
Matthias Schrell still has a lot to do at the location.
With the expansion of the Lower Saxony Bridge, Rhenus Midgard continues to relies on coal imports for power plants and is one of the largest in Europe in this sector.
Thanks to the sunbathing trough in front of the terminal, which has been deepened at 18.5 metres, capesize bulk carriers can also be handled here.
"This year, we want to break the 3-million-tonne mark when it comes to the envelope," says Schrell.
In addition to the Eon power plant on site, import coal goes to power plants in the inland.
If the GDF-Suez power plant goes online, a good 5 million tons of coal import per year would be realistic.
The three ship unloaders on the bridge as well as the second conveyor belt could create up to 10 million.
Bayern's basketballer optimistic despite first defeat
Even the first defeat in the Euroleague could not diminish the huge optimism among the basketball players of FC Bayern Munich.
"No one can forbad us to believe in winning against the best team in Europe in the past two years," Bayern coach Svetislav Pesic accounted after the unfortunate 83:88 (39:47) on Thursday night at defending champion Olympiakos Piraeus.
We sent the message: Basketball also exists in Munich!
With their only third appearance in the European premier class, Munich sold very well for a long time, but were simply not clarified enough in the decisive phases.
The guests initially managed the feat of converting a 15-point residue 13 minutes before Piraeus gave the game a turn.
"We fought back incredibly against the two-time Euroleague champion, but then made reckless mistakes again," Bayern pro Yassin Idbihi said.
We're proud of our performance but we want to win every game.
Malcolm Delaney and Nihad Djedovic were the most successful throwers of Munich, who had celebrated significant victories in the first two games against the Italian series champion Montepaschi Siena and the Polish title holder Zielona Góra.
Ben Greenman: Ten Years of New York Comedy Festival: The New Yorker
One could say that New York City is the birthplace of stand-up comedy in America: almost a hundred years ago, variant artist Frank Fay, who worked as a conférencier at the Palace Theatre on Broadway, started telling the crowd directly jokes in the form of a conversation.
Over the years, Fay's innovation has been expanded, as recently as the New York Comedy Festival.
Launched and directed by Caroline Hirsch, the founder of Caroline's stand-up institution, the festival celebrates its tenth anniversary this year with over sixty shows in small clubs and big theatres.
“Most of these headliners started at Carolines and then became increasingly successful until they were too well-known to perform at a club,” Hirsch said.
We designed this festival so we can keep working with them.
At this year's event there will be performances by Wanda Sykes, Kathy Griffin and Bill Maher as well as "Stand Up for Heroes," an annual music and comedy benefit event for army veterans at Madison Square Garden, where Bruce Springsteen, Jon Stewart, Roger Waters and Bill Cosby, among others.
With the festival, the world of comedy has also grown.
A number of comedians taking part in the festival this year have become known through rather unconventional ways, such as broadcasts on smaller television channels such as Comedy Central, FX and Spike.
Nick Kroll became known with a sitcom on cable television (the ironical-slippery fantasy-football series “The League” on FXX) and now has his own sketch show on Comedy Central.
Jenny Slate was part of the line-up on both “Saturday Night Live” and “Parks and Recreation”, although she is best known for her viral video series “Marcel the Shell with Shoes On”.
Both Kroll and other young comedians with characteristic voices (the surreally pessimistic Anthony Jeselnik, the ironic W. Kaumau Bell-focused on racial themes) are products of the decentralized world of American comedy.
One of the festival's biggest visitor magnets is an interview: David Steinberg talks to Larry David.
Steinberg started as a stand-up comedian, but has evolved into a respected television and film director as well as an unofficial comedy historian.
From 2005 to 2007 he hosted a television show titled "Sit Down Comedy with David Steinberg" on TV Land.
The meeting takes place at Town Hall in the middle of Manhattan.
“The city is definitely in the comedy DNA of Larry’s entire work,” Steinberg said.
He told me that when he's here, he sometimes walks through the alleyway between two buildings and thinks to himself: Hey, if I lose all my money, maybe I'll live here.
Iran satisfied ways with trial of nuclear negotiations
Iran is pleased with the negotiation process a week before the next nuclear meeting with the five U.N. veto powers and Germany.
"After years, we now have an agreement with the International Atomic Energy Agency to resolve the differences in recent years," Foreign Minister Mohammed Javad Zarif wrote on his Facebook page.
Although it would still be a long way to go, the nuclear chief negotiator is satisfied with the negotiation process and also optimistic that in the end both sides will come to a solution.
Drama in Uruguay: Boys kill playmates with machete
Two children have confessed the murder of an 11-year-old in Uruguay.
The two 14 and 12-year-old boys had invited their victim to go bird hunting with them.
Armed with machetes and knives, they stabbed the 11-year-old on Tuesday and threw the body into a well, the El País newspaper reported on Thursday.
Subsequently, they would have played football in front of their flats in a working-class neighborhood.
The corpse was found the following night in the village of Cerro Pelado, 15 kilometers north of the exclusive beach of Punta del Este.
As an alibi, a five-year-old half-sister of the younger, who had taken her, was supposed to testify she had been sexually assaulted by the boy.
However, when the girl described before the police without mentioning an attack against them, the two boys admitted the intentional killing of the 11-year-old.
Among other reasons, the elder cited, among other things, a rivalry in the football game.
He was provisionally admitted to a psychiatric youth institution.
Pimp must go to prison
Because of exploitative human trafficking, pimping, threats, insulting and dangerous bodily harm, the Regional Court of Konstanz has sentenced a 33-year-old man from St. Georgen to three and a half years in prison.
The former Bundeswehr soldier, who worked as a bouncer after two deployments in Afghanistan, made a confession as part of a litigation.
Accordingly, in five cases, he met women via internet or telephone contacts, which he sent after a few weeks against their will.
Resist he used to break with violence and threats.
He retained the revenues of the women, some of whom bought for him for years.
By confiscating mobile phones and SIM cards, he prevented women’s contacts with the outside world.
Together with a colleague who conveyed the women, he took them to various brothels in the southwest.
There he had them monitored in part to control their revenues.
In court, the defendant initially claimed he was suffering from severe trauma after colleagues in Afghanistan died next to him in an explosion.
After a psychiatric experience that a psychiatric expert had strong doubts about the psychological restriction and thus a limited culpability, the 33-year-old made a comprehensive confession.
The court did not have to hear any more of the aggrieved
According to a detective, at least four other cases of exploitative human trafficking could be determined after an initial display by telephone surveillance measures.
The wiretapped talks were marked by high levels of aggression.
In later interrogations, the injured parties would have confirmed their martyrdom.
Another witness, in which one of the women had discovered bruises all over her body, had not made a statement to date.
“It’s a completely broken personality” who sees violence and exploitation in this profession as normal.
The defendant deplored his conduct yesterday.
"He broke away from these circles and wanted to live a normal life after detention," he said.
Oil price continues to drop to $96 per barrel
The price of oil also fell further on Friday after worries about a high supply overshadowed a report that China's energy-hungry manufacturing sector is growing.
The referencial raw oil variety for December's deliveries had fallen by 14 cents to £96.24 per barrel in e-commerce in Europe on the New York Mercantile Exchange.
The contract felled 39 cents on Thursday, meaning a 5.8 per cent drop in October.
The large supply of crude oil has weighed on prices in recent weeks.
The U.S. Department of Energy said on Wednesday that the U.S. supply increased by 4.1 million barrels last week.
Over the course of five weeks, the supply had risen by 25 million barrels.
However, on Friday there was evidence of stronger demand through two reports of an increase in activity in China's manufacturing.
This indicates a continued recovery in China's economy, whose growth had risen to 7.8 percent in the third-quarter, having reached its lowest level in two decades in the previous quarter.
Crude oil from the Brent variety, a reference value for international crude that is also used by U.S. refineries, fell 26 cents to $108.58 per barrel at the ICE Exchange in London.
Court blocks decision on NYPD's stop-and-frisk directive
A federal appointment court in the U.S. on Thursday blocked the order of a female judge who had called for changes tover the New York Police Department's stop-and-frisk program ("Hitch and Search"), and untied the judge from the case.
The 2nd U.S. Court of Appeals said Judge Shira Scheindlin's decisions would be suspended until an appeal by the city was decided.
The judge had ruled in August that the city violated the constitution with the way the program for stopping and questioning persons would be implemented.
The city appealed against its assessment and remediated measures, including the decision to set up a monitoring body to assist the police in amending its policy and the appropriate training programme.
The appeals court on Tuesday listened to the arguments on the required suspension.
The appeal report stated that the judge must be relieved of the case she had violated the code of conduct for U.S. judge by determining that a judge should avoid the appearance of partisanship; this was partly based on a series of media interviews and public statements in which she had publicly responded to the court's criticism.
The judge had ruled that police officers violated the civil rights of tens of thousands of people because they were unreasonably targetingly targeting black and Latin American men with the stop-and-frisk program.
It appointed an external body to monitor the implementation of material changes, including reforms to policies, training and service supervision, and ordered a pilot program with body-carried cameras in neighbourhoods where most of the checks were carried out.
In August, the city of New York agreed to end the practice of storing names and addresses of people who were dismissed again following a check by the police.
An oral hearing on the city's appointment is scheduled after March 14, 2014.
The stop-and-frisk tactic has been criticised by a number of civil rights advocates.
Controls of this kind have been in various forms for decades, but the number of recorded checks during the tenure of non-party mayor Michael Bloomberg rose to an all-time high of 684,330 in 2011, most of them black and Latin American men.
In 2004, a lawsuit was fileded by four men who belonged to all the minorities, which developed into a class action lawsuit.
Advocates of changes to the NYPD's stop-and-frisk program say the changes will end unfair practices and create a more trustworthy and effective police, as well as the way other police presidiums use the scheme.
Opponents are of the opinion that the changes would dampen the morale of the police, but not crime; also, money would be wasted and the broader problem of a police would not be solved, which is under pressure after the elimination of thousands of jobs in the last decade.
The judge pointed out that it did not end the stop-and-frisk practice, which was constitutional, but only reforming the nature of implementation of the controls by the NYPD.
Of the happiness of dreaming camels
Happiness is an oasis that manages to reach only dreaming camels
With this Bedouin wisdom, the first chairman Wolfgang Henne described the history and fascination of the association "Helfing Hands".
On Saturday afternoon, the club's tenth birthday was celebrated, which can already show considerable success.
The first chairman Henne reported extensively on the work of the association.
He himself was for the first time in Mauritania at Cheijk-Zajed Hospital in Nouakchott in 2004.
Subsequently, a further cooperation developed, and the gynaecologist Henne carried out numerous operations on several trips.
During the speeches, pictures of the stakes were shown on a big screen, so that the guests of the anniversary event could get a picture of the mentioned actions.
The club's desert vehicle has also been shown.
It is a former federal border protection vehicle.
Rainer Prewo, the former OB of Nagold, had made the proposal to equip this vehicle with a photovoltaic system and now, according to Henne, plans to act in an advisory capacity.
In 2008, the Schleeh carpentry company from Baiersbronn created the infirmary in Socogim, a misery district on the outskirts of the capital during the record time of just under a week - on a voluntary basis.
Hen also mentioned a new mother-to-child hospital in Nouakchott
Particularly interesting in times when the maternity clinic in Nagold is to be rationalized away.
One learns a lot in Africa as a doctor, according to Henne, for example deceleration against burn-out.
Due to the political unrest in the country, it was not possible to undertake as many trips as planned, but many physicians came to Germany to work here in various areas.
In addition, containers of material are regularly sent to Africa, such as medical devices, medications, bandage materials, hospital beds and glasses in March this year.
Co-initiator Hans-Joachim Fuchtel himself provided plenty of local color by telling from Mauritania about Mercedes vehicles converted into a mobile goat stall and describing the experiences of the Stammheim musicians, who traditionally support the association, during their Mauritania trip.
As his personal motives, he cited his work in the field of auditing as a young member of parliament.
When it became clear to him how much money was lost along the way, he decided to help the Africans in the control of a budget by the people.
Fuchtel also emphasized that the global economy also demands global human love.
It should by no means be forgotten that Africa is closer than some think.
It’s not far from Gran Canaria to Africa.
Coulson used phone hacking to review a tip
Former News of the Word editor Andy Coulson allegedly used "phone hacking, surveillance and confrontation" in an attempt to confirm a false hint about an affeat by then-Home Secretary Charles Clarke.
Prosecutor Andrew Edi's QC told the Old Bailey court that News of the World heard a false rumor in May 2005 that Clarke had a relationship with his "attractive special adviser" Hannah Pawlby.
The newspaper instructed private detective Glenn Mulcaire to hack Pawlby's voicemails and chased them, but Coulson also called them, leaving messages as the court learned.
“According to the prosecutor’s office, Coulson, who is now editor of NotW, is not someone standing in front of people’s homes and hopes to catch them on fresh deed, but one who likes to confront people with a story to see their reaction,” Edis explained.
He said NotW uses three procedures to research stories investigatively: phone hacking, surveillance and confrontation.
The editor is personally involved in the third method.
Obviously he knows about the second, the surveillance, it can't be otherwise.
What's it like with the first one?
Does he know about phone hacking?
He denies this, but we say, “And if he knew about it.”
Rumours of Clarke's affair were first picked up by NotW's feature editorial board when a source sexually interested in Pawlby was told: "Don't waste your time, she's with Charles."
A band with voicemails from her phone of at least three occasions was secured at Mulcaire's apartment in August 2006.
Investigators also founded listings on the detective's computer where Pawlby and her sister were stored as "projects."
By the time when the searches were ongoing, Pawlby's grandparents received anonymous calls asking for information about them, Edis explained.
Meanwhile, former chief reporter Neville Thurlbeck and former reporter James Weatherup overseed Pawlby's movements overseeing.
On June 18, 2005, Coulson left her a voicemail saying, “I’ve got a story we’re going to bring out tomorrow that I’d like to talk to Charles about.”
Edis explained Coulson's activities in this story followed the same pattern as with other important personalities, such as former Interior Minister David Blunkett.
Jurors heared Thursday that Coulson confronted Blunkett about an affair with a married woman when he himself was dating co-defendant Rebekah Brooks, who had been married at the time.
Coulson and Brooks deny having conspired with others from hacking phones between October 3, 20000 and August 9, 2006.
Mulcaire, Thurlbeck and Weatherup have admitted phone hacking.
In protest against the planned wealth tax, French football will actually go through the first strike since 1972.
A conciliation meeting comes to an end inconclusively.
In the conflict over the wealth tax, the government and professional football have maneuvered into an impasse in France after a failed conciliation meeting.
President François Hollande received club and association representatives on Thursday at the Élysée-Palast in Paris and also listened to their complaints.
However, he refused to spare the football from the planned 75 percent levy on income of more than one million euros per year, which all companies in the country are supposed to pay for two years from 2014.
The opposing side also remained tough.
The strike, announced at the end of November, will now also be carried out, said Jean-Pierre Louvel.
Ligue 1 and Ligue 2, scheduled between 29 November and 2 December, are therefore cancelled.
Gechingen: No absolute protection possible
"We want to involve the residents early in the planning," Mayor Jens Häußler opened the citizens' information event on the flood protection concept in Gechingen.
Around 80 participants had come to the community hall to explain the planned measures, ask questions, express concerns and contribute ideas.
Häußler clarified: "The ultimate decisions are made by the members of the municipal council".
According to Häußler, the floods that Gechingen hit unprepared on May 15, 2009, was a drastic event that raised the question of protective measures for the future.
A river area investigation had been commissioned with the neighbouring municipality of Aidlingen, which is now the basis for the flood protection concept in Gechingen.
"The protection of as many built-up plots as possible is the goal," says Häußler.
According to the German weather service, it is based on the planning of the protection against floods to be statistically expected every 100 years.
The climate change is taken into account by a 15 percent surcharge.
In professional circles, this is referred to as "HQ 100aKlima".
The water masses, which had led to considerable damage in Gechingen in 2009, corresponded to a 1000-year-old flood.
"There is no absolute protection, but we can achieve a relative improvement," says Häußler.
In order to achieve the protection HQ 100aKlima, around four million euros have to be invested in Gechingen, whereby the municipality can expect state grants of around 70 percent.
However, Häußler pointed out that funding only exists if an overall concept is implemented.
For this purpose, the municipality is dependent on the participation of the citizens.
A complex planning and approval procedure had to be completed.
In the best case, implementation could begin in 2016.
Among other things, the protection concept, which was already presented in the municipal council last week (we reported) provides for local measures in the inflow of the river Irm from the Stammheim valley.
Following the presentation of the flood protection concept, the participants took the opportunity to contribute their concerns.
Among other things, it was questioned why more dams and retention basins were not planned to protect the place.
Gregor Kühn, specialist planner of the commissioned engineering office Wald and Corbe from Hügelsheim, who presented the concept, emphasized that it needed the interaction of all presented measures in order to achieve the desired goal.
In addition, the most economical solution should be found, according to Joachim Wald von Wald and Corbe.
Displeasure reigned among residents of the old town, who suffer from an overloading of the canal network even during normal thunderstorms.
The problem is known in the administration and initial measures have already been taken.
However, Häußler pointed out that the guidelines for sewer systems were different than for flood protection and that this should not be mixed.
The municipality is obliged to interpret the canal network in such a way that two to three-year rain events could be tolerated.
Sizing the sewers larger would devour millions.
Seriously injured after clash
Early on Friday afternoon, two motorists were seriously injured in a head-on collision on Landesstraße 44 near Revensdorf.
The police immediately closed the road completely, but could initially say little about the accident.
However, there are various testimonies that should first be evaluated.
What is certain is that the 19-year-old driver of a VW Golf drove in the direction of Revensdorf and the 38-year-old man from Gettorf approached her with his Hyundai.
Both vehicles collided, with the woman so severely pinched that the fire brigade needed just under half an hour to free her from the vehicle wreckage.
The actual cause of the accident remained unexplained at first.
Both victims came to nearby hospitals.
The track remained fully closed for about two hours.
You could call him Dostoevsky of the United States.
Philip K. Dick was a seeker of God, a metaphysician, his novels and narratives also have something serial.
Surprisingling the kinship in the exterior: the pungent eyes, the beard, the high forehead.
And just as Fyodor M. Dostoyevsky is to be understood with his massive oeuvre as the founder and apologist of the Russian soul, one reads Philip K. Dick as an American prophet, who is quite true in his own country.
Some titles have been published by Heyne Verlag, the Haffmans cassette with the 118 narratives distributes two-thousand-one.
Dick explains America - the realm of breathtaking technical progress, coupled with paranoia, security delusions and the belief of being chosen.
Thick had experiences of God, his late books read like a mixture of John's revelation and a computer manual.
Maybe he was crazy, had drugs added to his brains.
He understands, early on, that the computers are divinities.
He wrote a theology of the computer and asked what distinguishes a man from a machine, the Creature.
That's the "Blade Runner" problem.
The killing.
a master of science fiction.
It's a fantastic writer.
His stories have inspired film directors like Ridley Scott, Paul Verhoeven and Steven Spielberg.
This story is particularly significant now.
The "Minorities-Report" dates from 1956, it exudes the smell of the Cold War and the McCarthy tribunals.
The principle of "pre-crime," "pre-crime," i.e. what is now more or less official doctrine of the White House is developed here.
In Dick's world, which is terrifyingly, mutants are looking to the future - and the police are reachingly.
"We capture individuals who have not violated any law," says the head of the authority.
“We’ll grab them even before they can commit a violent crime.”
And: "In our society there are no serious criminals, but we have a penal camp full of pesudover criminals."
They assume that bad things happen, and prevent the criminal thought from being put into action.
Dick's narrative seems like the blueprint for anti-terrorism laws and the NSA's wiretapping frenzy.
Anticipation of the attack: This is how Obama’s drone philosophy and practice also works.
But Dick's story isn't over.
The police chief laments that there is no longer the separation of powers, the military controls everyday life - and what is left of the state.
Then the supervisor himself will be monitored and convicted of a future crime.
The system's freaking out.
It's perfect, but it's lying.
It invents its own prerequisites.
Not only accepts errors and sacrifices, but builds on them.
The system creates the danger it fights.
Reading Philip K. Dick means getting closer to the truth.
Spoking affair around Merkel in Russia testifying
Former U.S. intelligence expert Edward Snowden could also make statements in his Russian asylum in the U.S. spying affair around German Chancellor Angela Merkel.
Representatives of Germany's Federal Prosecutor's Office could either ask questions in writing or meet the 30-year-old in person in Russia, the Interfax agency reported.
Hans-Christian Ströbele was the first German politician Snowden in Moscow to talk to him about the affair.
NSA revelations intensify company paranoia over government surveillance
On a mild day at the end of August, a German police helicopter flew deep over the U.S. consulate in Frankfurt am Main, Germany's financial capital.
At the instruction of the Federal Office for the Protection of Terrorism and Counter-Terrorirism (BVT), the German internal intelligence service, he should photograph the roof of the U.S. outpost, which is located less than 5 km from the European Central Bank and the Bundesbank.
According to German media, the BVT wanted to prove the presence of surveillance antennas and the action led to an exchange of words between the USA and the German Foreign Ministry in Berlin.
James Clapper, U.S. Director of National Intelligence, insisted again in September that the U.S. did not use foreign espionage “to steal the trade secrets of foreign companies on behalf of U.S. corporations so that they could expand their international competitiveness or profits.”
However, since Edward Snowden, who has become a whistleblower, has begun publishing his cornucopia on U.S. surveillance secrets, European governments and corporate chief executives are no longer sure whether they can take the director at their word.
Reports that the U.S. National Security Agency spied on Brazilian oil company Petrobas and procured data from American cloud providers, including Google and Yaahoo, have brought company paranoia to a new high because of government surveillance.
The barrel to overflow brought the revelation that chancellor Angela Merkel's phone had been bugged, possibly for ten years.
If the most powerful person in Europe can be targeted, then company managements are certainly also potential targets.
Snowden has made intensive cooperation between U.S. intelligence agencies and companies transparent.
I believe it is conceivable that this data will be used for mutual benefit.
“Germany needs to wake up,” explains Oliver Grün, president of BITMi, which represents small and medium-sized IT companies in Germany.
German companies believe the U.S. now posed an almost as big a risk as China when it comes to industrial espionage and data thefting, according to a survey published in July by consulting firm EY.
So far, however, there has been no evidence in the documents leaked by Snowden that the U.S. has leaked the trade secrecies of foreign companies to its own companies.
Politicians have expressed concern that the EU lacked certain IT and Internet opportunities and that they should reduce their dependence on the US.
The companies are sceptical about this.
In the Bundestag it was suggested that we should build a German Google.
I can only close my eyes and slowly reopen them...
"That's not how it works," says Hasso Plattner, chairman of the German software manufacturer SAP.
If you had wanted a strong European IT industry, you shouldn't have let it die out 20 years ago.
Everything in Germany is subsidized, from coal to cars to agriculture.
Everything except for the IT industry.
Nevertheless, range and technical sophistication of the U.S. spy regulators uncovered by the Snowden revelations have shocked companies that had previously seen the biggest espionage risk in China.
In cloud computing, a major change is taking place, because European business leaders are now more aware that data stored in the United States is subject to local case law and is therefore potentially at risk.
According to a survey conducted by the Cloud Security Alliance, a trading institution, after the revelations about U.S. data mining activities with Prism, about ten percent of non-U.S.-based members rejected plans to use an American provider.
Jim Snabe, deputy managing director of SAP, says: “We see a new question from customers that didn’t exist a year agove – it’s: ‘Where will my day store my data and can you guarantee that it will physically remain in that jurisdiction?”
Many German company managers argue that recent reports simply confirm what they already knew anyway, namely that powerful states want to steal their most valuable secrets and must therefore be guarded at all costs.
Economic espionage takes place is no surprise.
It's always been.
“It has been an issue for many years and has not fundamentally changed as a result of the current discussion,” says Kurt Bock, chairman of the chemical company BASF.
Americans are spying on us on a commercial and industrial level, just as we do, because it's in the national interest to defend our companies.
Corporate governance doesn't usually boast about the countermeasures you've taken, because that gives attackers benefits.
In large companies, employees have long been taught that taking a free USB stick at a trade fair or the unguarded lying of a laptop in the hotel room is not advisable, to say the least.
Ulrich Hackenberk, a board member at automaker Audi, says that it has been standard practice for years to collect the mobile phones before a board meeting so that they cannot be used as wiretapping devices.
Germany's BVT advises executives to use simple prepaid phones when going on trips abroad, because smartphones can potentially be compromised.
The prepaid phones are thrown away afterwards.
However, there is concern that small and medium-sized enterprises will remain vulnerable to hacking and surveillance.
In Germany, many of these companies are the global market leaders in the respective niche.
“Small and medium-sized enterprises often lack the experience, personnel and financial resources to effectively protect trade secrets against unauthorized access,” the BVT warns in a report.
The U.S. warns its own companies about economic espionage by other countries.
According to the Washington Post, in the U.S. National Intelligence Estimate in February, France, Russia and Israel were ranked second behind China in second place among the countries that used to obtain economic data from hacking.
A board member of a German blue-chip company agreed that when it comes to economic espionage, “the French are the worst.”
Bernard Squarcini, former head of France's internal intelligence service DCRL, was quoted as saying in an interview this month: "The intelligence services know very well that all countries, even if they work together in the fight against terror, are spying on their allies."
