Parents of Georgia teenager who died in tragic accident
The teenager's parents, whose corpse was found in a rolled-up wrestling mat in the gym of his high school, are convinced their son was murdered, the family's lawyer explained on Thursday.
Kendrick Johnson from Valdosta in the U.S. state of Georgia was found on January 11 in an uprisoned mat that stood behind the grandstand in the school gym.
Lowndes County sheriff's investigators concluded that Johnson died in a tragic accident, which the 17-year-old's family doubted.
“They’re convinced that their son has been murdered,” Benjamin Crump, Kenneth and Jacquelyn Johnson’s lawyer, told FoxNews.com.
They never thought he died as the sheriff concluded.
“They believe that without any logic that it violates the laws of physics as well as common sense,” Crump said.
Rather, they are convinced that the truth will be covered up in order to protect the person or persons responsible for the death of their son.
“Your son went to school to study and came backing back in the mortuary,” he comments.
U.S. Prosecutor Michael Moore said on Thursday there will be a formal investigation into Johnson's death as a number of important issues remain unresolved.
What was the cause of death?
Is his death due to a crime?
Moore said this at a press conference on Thursday afternoon.
I'm following the facts in whatever direction they're leading.
My aim is to find out the truth.
"I take the view that there is a sufficient basis for a formal investigation," he said.
Moore told reporters that the initial autopsy indicated that Johnson had died as a result of “position-related suffocation.”
However, a second autopsy resulted in a different cause of death, according to Moore.
"There are a number of questions that need to be answered or confirmed," he said.
Moore added that if there is sufficient evidence for a criminal or civil investigation into Johnson's death, he will instruct the FBI to conduct the investigation.
A representative of the County Sheriff's office was not immediately ready for comment at our request on Thursday.
A court in southern Georgia ordered on Wednesday that the authorities must release all the surveillance footage that investigators reviewed.
The teenager's parents said they hoped the video recordings contained clues as to how he died.
CDC release allergy guidelines for schools
On Wednesday, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) released a set of guidelines for dealing with food allergies of children in schools.
These are the first such guidelines issued by the U.S. government as the number of school-aged children with food allergies is increasing.
One out of 20 children in the United States now has a food allergy.
The proliferation of food allergies among children has increased by 18 percent between 1997 and 2007, according to CDC.
The guidelines provide information for schools designed to raise awareness of food allergies among the faculty and staff and how to deal with children showing allergic reactions.
It is also recommended that schools have epinephrine in stock – the car injection of the EpiPen brand is most commonly used – to counter a potentially deadly hypersensitivity reaction.
Legislatures in the states have recently changed regulations so that schools can keep epinephrine in stock more easily.
The report also includes a list of typical symptoms mentioned by children who have an allergic reaction.
Children may say, “It feels like something presses on my tongue”, “My tongue feels as if a hair lies on it” or “My tongue tingles.”
Parents of intersex children can choose ‘gender indefinitely’
Germany is the first European nation in which a third gender is recognized for children who were born with inconclusive genitals.
Newbornays are no longer rigidly assigned male or
Under the new law, parents no longer have to declare gender for such children, but can specify “indefinitely” or “non-determined” on the birth certificate.
The aim of the law is to take the pressure off parents so that they don't have to make hasty decisions regarding surgery for a sex assignment in newborns, and fighting discrimination against intersex people.
An intersex person said years later, “I am neither a man nor a woman.”
I remain the patchwork created by doctors, mutilated and full of scars.
It is estimated that one in 2,000 children are not born each year either as a boy or as a girl.
They belong to the intersex people, a group that falls under the diagnosis “sex-differentiation disorder” (DSD), a collective term for people with atypical chromosomes, germ glands (ovaries or testicles) or unusually developed genitals.
Wallis Simpson may have been intersex.
The understanding of gender identification is still not very well developed, but most experts in the U.S. advise to use the best information available for gender allocation if a provision is not possible and not wait for the psychological and physical development of the child before deciding – if at all – for surgery.
New York psychiatrist Dr Jack Drescher, who specializes in the issue of gender identification, said the new German law "sounds like a good thing."
Intersex children constitute an ethical dilemma.
"Some people have life-threatening diseases that require surgery, but this does not apply to most children," he said.
One can make a gender assignment without surgery and then wait to see how the identity develops.
The scientific data on how a child develops their gender identity in the first place are not very accurate.
No one knows why it's happening.
It’s like the secret why people are homosexual.
In a 2011 report to the European Commission, intersex people should be differentiated from transsexual or transgender persons, because their status is not gender-related, but due to its biological constitution, which is neither exclusively male nor female, but typically defines both at the same time or not clearly as one of them.
These traits were manifested in subordinated sexual characteristics such as muscle mass, hairing, boosts and stature, priority sexual characteristics such as reproductive organs and genitals, or in chromosome structures and hormones.
The report also provides an overview of the discrimination faced by intersex and transgender individuals in professional life, as well as the extent of harassment, violence and prejudgment crime.
Boys who do not agree with the gender image now have a special camp.
It is now possible for adults in Australia and Nepal to indicate male, female or “third gender” on official documents.
In June, Australian Norrie May-Welby (52) was recognised as the world’s first “genderless” person after a court granted lifelong retention of “undetermined” gender status.
In addition to M and W, German identity cards will have a third assignment in the future, X for intersex, according to the Interior Ministry.
In neighbouring France, gender issues are still controversial, according to a news news on France 24.
In 2011, dozens of French deputies from the strictly Catholic country signed a petition that the “gender theory” should be removed from textbooks.
The U.S. website Catholic Online has also spoken out against the German law, saying: “If the world is dragged into a new state, where gender is a decision, but sexual activity does not, then we twist two further pillars of civilization.”
The mother of a newborn from Maryland told Baby Zone that she would find it better if babies were assigned a gender at birth.
“Parental existence is stressful enough even without further restrictions, especially if you don’t know the gender of your own child,” she said.
Children need stability and security.
Historically, children born with both male and female genitals were referred to as hermaphrodites, after the attractive Greek god who had both sexes.
Ten years ago, medicine regarded gender as something that could be erased and then rebuilt.
But in the meantime, the ethical foundations of an operation are questioned after it is now known how complex the sexual identity is, and that doctors can also lie wrong, because they do not know how the child feels when growing up with the gender assigned by them.
“In the middle of the 20th century it was called a ‘psychiatric emergency’,” Drescher said.
If such a child was born, they did not call the psychiatrist, but the surgeon.
The prevailing theory about the treatment of children with inconclusive genitals goes back to Dr. John Money of Johns Hopkins University, who argued that gender was malleable.
He coined the term “gender identity” and argued that social and environmental impetus – how parents raise a child – interacted with a child’s genes and hormones and thus shaped whether a person considers himself male or female.
However, in a case from 1966 under “John/Joan”, his theories became controversial.
He advised the parents of a boy whose penis had been severed during a botched circumcision to neuterate the child completely and also to remove his testicles and then raise him as a girl.
“Money presented this case as a successful example of a transformation, but it wasn’t,” Drescher said.
When the boy was about 15, he switched back to the male sex and married a woman.
At 38, however, he committed suicide.
According to Drescher, there are still doctors who would practice this model.
Yet in the 1990s, with the entry of the Internet, survivors of these gender surgeries who are “not happy with the result.”
Jim Bruce, a 36-year-old writer from Montana who was born with XY male chromosomes, but ambiguous genitals.
Doctors weren't clear about whether he had a large clitoris or a small penis and were convinced he could never live a "satisfactory life" as a man.
So his external organ and testicles were surgically removed shortly after giving birth in 1976 and he was raised as a girl.
At the age of 12, he received female hormones.
"I knew I wasn't a girl," he told ABCNews.com
I was unhappy, but it was really hard to ask questions.
At the age of 18 he should be given a vaginal plastic.
However, since he was depressed and knew something was wrong, he demanded his medical records.
What he figured out was shocking.
I was sterilized at my birth – and nobody ever told me that.
Bruce was born with a DSD through which his body couldn't produce enough testosterone to develop the genitals.
After learning the truth, he switched back to male identity, received testosterone injections and had his boobs removed.
As a result of the operation, he has become infertile.
Today he fights for others in an organization called Interface Project that intersex people are perceived as normal.
However, Anne Tamar-Mattis, chief executive of California-based Advocates for Informed Choice, is worried that German law could “encourage stamping and stigmatizing.”
“Many activists are concerned that the German decision could encourage parents to make quick decisions and assign a ‘not fixed’ to the child,” she explained.
We are afraid this favors interventions.
We think it would be better to assign the male or female gender and then wait.
However, we do not yet know how the law will affect it, so we can only speculate.
Tamar-Mattis said her organization supports Australian law because “it allows adults to be recognized as a third gender.”
“Adults should be able to make their own decisions about their legal gender,” she explained.
The German law is allocated at birth.
This isn't a struggle to force young children at this time.
When they are adults, they can decide on their own body.
However, Dr. Arlene Baratz, a breast radiologist from Pittsburg who has a daughter with a sexual differentiation disorder and supports hundreds of others in a self-help group, says German law “authorizes” both parents and children.
Katie, Baratz's daughter, was born with male chromosomes, but has a DSD called complete androgen resistance.
Due to the receptor defect of the target cells for testosterone, Katie developed female characteristics.
She has a vagina, but no uterus and no ovaries.
Today Katie is 29 and married; she works at the University of Pennsylvania as an employee in child psychiatry.
Although she is infertile, he hopes to be able to have a child through adoption or a surrogate mother.
“The law gives parents space, so they don’t have to make decisions hastily,” Baratz said.
It gives them the time to do different tests and clarify things and thus give them time before they write “male” or “female”.
In this way, everything is fine – you raise the child and love it.
You have a wonderful baby and enjoy parental happiness.
And you don't have to hastily perform an operation that can't be reversed.
“This includes the children in the decision and eliminates the fear that the parents around, because they think they don’t do the right thing,” she explained.
Ultimately, the child decides the gender that suits him better – and that’s wonderful.
This enables children to make the decision themselves.
