Living with future prospects
Waltraud Ries actually lives fantastically.
Your house is located in a quiet residential area of Stuttgart with a lot of greenery, an old tree population, nice neighbors and also not too much traffic.
It is only a few minutes to the city by public transport.
"Hope you'ren't afraid of spiders?" Waltraud Ries asks, pointing to a fat spider on the door frame.
I've been looking for a new apartment for my husband and myself for a while now.
But you know how difficult this is in Stuttgart.
However, the reason is not a spider phobia, but the worry of not being able to cope with the many stairs to the house and in the apartment in old age, she explains.
In addition: Since a meniscus operation, the mid-fifties has had to experience what it means to move from floor to floor with handicap.
Recently, a book from her on the topic 'Happy living in old age - Which form of living is the best for me?'.
'Our apartment is the best example of what doesn't work in old age,' she begins to tell.
When she moved with her husband to the duplex apartment more than 20 years ago, it was simply a beautiful apartment in the middle of the countryside.
Today at mid-fifties, Waltraud Ries thinks about it differently.
However, she also knows that she is one of the few people who even deals with the issue of 'living in old age'.
Most are displacing it as long as possible.
Only the others always get old,' she says chuckling.
It is only when the pressure of suffering is really great that you worry.
'Then it may already be too late," says Ries.
In her book, the interior designer introduces 17 residential models for self-determined living in old age.
"What type of living you choose, however, is always an individual decision," she explains.
D i e solution does not exist for old age.
Even those who can afford the retirement home in Tuscany or Brittany should always consider that they can also get sick.
And: 'Without a good knowledge of the national language, you can also work together in the Dolce Vita," says the specialist book-seekers.
Among their models are classic care models, alternative forms of housing or the often cited pensioner flatshare.
In old age, everyone has their ticks and quirks
"For me, however, such a residential community would be nothing," she says with a wink.
Certainly not like in a student apartment with a shared bathroom and a kitchen.
"In old age, you just need your retreat spaces," says Ries.
Nevertheless, the book author does not want to rule out the fact that a senior living community can still work.
However, this must be different than in the classic student shared flat.
And sometimes you just want your rest," she believes.
With this opinion, at least in the state capital, she is not alone.
"Most old people in Stuttgart want to stay in their own apartment as long as possible," is also the experience of Theresa Rütten, the head of Citizens' Service Life at the Age of the state capital.
Your department advises people on the subject of 'getting older'.
Therefore, especially older people would often accept inconveniences and restrictions, only to be able to stay in their usual environment, this is also the experience of Stiftung Warentest.
Today, there are many ways to make the house or the apartment barrier-free.
Ries recommends all those who deal with the age-appropriate conversion of their house or apartment to inform themselves thoroughly and not to hire the first craftsman.
Today there are also specialists for age-appropriate conversion.
Even in the rental apartment, one or the other can be realized, and if it is only the toilet seat increase, the interior designer enlightens.
However, the existing apartment cannot always be redesigned according to age and therefore barrier-free, even if it is still beautiful.
Waltraud Ries had to have this experience.
Although you could install a stairlift inside the apartment, the path paved with stairs to the apartment door would be the much bigger obstacle.
She'll keep looking until she's found the ideal object for herself and her husband.
Before the relocation, she grieves more than in front of the spider at her apartment door.
U.S. seeks to prosecute Snowden
The U.S. has not changed its stance to former U.S. intelligence official Edward Snowden.
He is still accused of illegally leaking secret information.
Therefore, he had to face criminal proceedings in the U.S., U.S. State Department spokeswoman Jennifer Psaki said in Washington.
Snowden's recent comments would not change that either.
Previously, after a meeting with Snowden in Moscow, the German politician Hans-Christian Ströbele had appealed to the U.S. not to threaten him further with punishment.
Psaki emphasized that Ströbele's remarks were those of a parliamentarian and not those of a German member of the government.
Intelligence revealer Edward Snowden is basically ready for a conversation with German authorities.
First, however, he wants to know his situation clarified.
This was made clear by the US-sought former NSA employee in an unaddressed letter published by the Green politician Hans-Christian Ströbele on Friday after a meeting with Snowden and forwarded it to the federal government, the Bundestag and the attorney general.
According to a translation disseminated by Ströbele, the lettering has the following:
To those responsible
I've been asked to write to you regarding your mass surveillance investigation.
My nam is Edward Joseph Snowden and was formerly contracted or employed through a direct position as a technical expert at the National Security Agency (NSA), the Central Intelligence Agency (CIA) and the Defense Intelligence Agency (DIA).
In the course of my employment in these institutions, I witnessed systematic violations of the law by my government, which prompted me to act out of moral duty.
As a result of the publication of these concerns, I found myself exposed to a serious and persistent incitement that forced me to leave my family and home.
I am currently living in exile and enjoying temporary asylum, which the Russian Federation grants me under international law.
I am encouraged by the response to my political actions, both in the US and elsewhere.
Citizens around the world, as well as high-ranking officials - including the United States - have judged the revelations about a system of all-encompassing surveillance accountable to anyone as a service to the public.
These spying revelations attracted many proposals on new laws and directives aimed at the previously covert abuse of public trust.
The benefits for society from these findings are becoming increasingly clear; at the same time, the risks that have been accepted have been visibly diminished.
Though the outcome of my efforts was demonstrably positive, my government continues to treat dissent as a breach of allegiation and strives to criminalize and impeach political expression.
Nevertheless, speaking the truth is not a crime.
I am confident that that the United States government, with the support of the international community, will adopt this detrimental stance.
I hope that, if the difficulties of this humanitarian situation are addressed, I will be able to participate appropriately and legally in clarifying the facts of the matters made in the media, in particular with regard to the truth and authenticity of the reports.
I look forward to a conversation with you in your country as soon as the situation is resolved and thank you for your efforts to uphold the international law that protects us all.
Edward Snowden testified by Hans-Christian Ströbele
kindergarten has found a private buyer
"The building is in good hands," says Winterlingen's mayor Michael Maier.
The former kindergarten in Gartenstraße was sold by the municipality to a private person.
The sale was approved by the local council in its most recent meeting in the non-public part.
The building changed its owner at an "appropriate" price.
"The community is satisfied," Maier points out.
In the budget, €100 000 was budgeted.
It is not yet clear how the new owner intends to use the former kindergarten.
The mayor’s right that Winterlingen has handed over responsibility for the building to someone else before winter and thus no longer has to pay for the maintenance of the house, how to heat, look after the garden and clear the sidewalk.
"This effort is now gone," Maier rejoices.
