About 50 listeners enjoy the sounds of the Hammrich musicians.
Chairman Manuela Honeck explains the pieces of music.
Honeck thanked all those who had helped to successfully manage the first hock of musicians in this context.
Report: Obama campaign considered replacing Biden with Hillary Clinton
President Barack Obama's closest advisers have secretly considered replacing Vice President Joe Biden with Hillary Clinton in the 2012 election campaign, according to the New York Times.
The revelation is the most notable surprise of the long-awaited work by Mark Halperin and John Heilemann about the 2012 election campaign, “Double Down: Game Change 2012”.
The Times has a copy of the soon-to-be-release book and it reported on Thursday night that the president's top advisers had conducted "extensive group meetings and polls at the end of 2011" to find out if it looked better for Obama's dwindling hope for re-election if he dropped Biden.
According to Jonathan Martin, the Times national policy correspondent, the book thoroughly traces the history of efforts by leading employees of the campaign and the White House, namely former White House Chief of Staff Bill Daley, to measure the impact it has on the pollicity of exchanging former Secretary of State Clinton for the vice-president.
The potential shift was a tight-kept secret within campaign infrastructure in Chicago and the Oval Office.
Only half a dozen of the president's closest advisers -- including Daley's former campaign leader, Obama's former campaign director, Jim Messina, and former White House senior advisers David Axelrod and David Plouffe -- knew about the envisaged change.
“Double Down” claims Daley was at the forefront of efforts to exchange Biden despite her “close, personal relationship” before finally deciding against such a move after the data showed that Clinton’s candidacy “wouldn’t significantly improve Obama’s chances.”
In an interview with Martin, Daley confirmed that the administration had actually considered replacing Biden with Clinton.
“I’ve expressed a whole bunch of things to consider, and that’s one of them,” Daley explained.
One has to remember that the president had terrible poll ratings at the time, so we thought, “For God’s sake, what do we do?”
While Daley described the investigations as “committed diligence,” Martin told CNN’s Anderson Cooper that significant effort had been made during the re-election campaign to find out if such a move was paying off with regard to the ballot boxes.
“In campaigns, there isn’t as much money spent on polls and focus groups if you don’t think seriously about something,” Martin said.
However, it is not clear if Obama knew that his team was thinking about an exchange.
Martin told CNN he asked Daley if his then-boss would have known about the potential change.
Although Daley declared that he did not believe the president was “aware” of the possible change, the former chief of staff admitted it was “possible” that Obama knew about it.
Martin added that "Double Down" did not provide a definitive answer to the question of whether the political exploratories had reached Obama's desk.
Cooper asked Martin if he was seriously thinking Obama didn't knew of the investigations into whether to drop Biden in the elections.
“Possible,” Martin replied.
What happens on the way with my e-mail?
E-mailing means electronic letter.
Was e-mail technically implemented letter secrecy in the design of the Internet service, similar to the conventional postal service?
When designing the early Internet services, the focus was on making communication possible.
Therefore, the basic technical protocols did not take care to create a stable foundation for a hearing-proof exchange of letters.
Therefore, a conventional e-mail corresponds more to an open postcard rather than a sealed letter.
If user "Anna" sends an e-mail to "Benni", are the computers of the two directly connected?
Anna's e-mail program or browser sends the e-mail to her service provider's mail server in the first step.
For example, if she has a GMail account, that would be Google.
This mail server sends the content to the provider Benni uses.
On the route, the e-mail can run through several other servers on the Internet.
Bennie can then call the content at his provider.
Are conventional e-mails read on their way through the net?
Most e-mails are read multiple times by software robots on the go.
With the provider of the sender, it is usually checked whether the e-mail carries a malicious program as an attachment.
A virus check is also carried out on the recipient systems.
In addition, the providers check whether it is an annoying and unwanted spam mail, which is immediately deleted or at least sorted away into a spam folder.
Do the robots have another task?
For systems like GMail, the robot also determines the information Google needs to place context advertising.
If Anna and Benni communicate via GMail about their upcoming holiday trip, Google can display corresponding links to holiday offers.
Do unknown people also read the e-mails?
The probability that unauthorized persons will read an e-mail is extremely low.
Theoretically, however, this is possible.
In companies, administrators often have the opportunity to read electronic mail.
Law enforcement and intelligence agencies also have legal ways to intercept or take note of e-mails.
In addition, there are possible illegal spying actions.
Is it possible to protect e-mails against reading?
With encryption according to the OpenPGP standard, an e-mail can be effectively encrypted against reading.
With high technical effort, it is also possible to disguise the metadata of an e-mail communication, so that it is not even possible to easily identify who has communicated with whom.
How important are Yahoo and Google in the e-mail market in Germany?
According to Convio’s Consulting, Yahoo and Google play only a minor role in the mailbooxes mainly used privately.
While Yahoo-Mail's shares had recently fallen, GMail was able to increase significantly from Google.
Mother buried in the woods and pension conceded
When his elderly mother died, a man from Wolfsburg forged a plan.
He buried the dead in a forest - and continued to concede their pension and care allowance.
In order to continue to collect pensions and nursing money, a 67-year-old Wolfsburger buried his dead mother in the forest, according to police investigations.
At first, he had covered up the death of the 89-year-old, then he collected the money for more than a year and a half.
Now, the 67-year-old must answer for fraud, as a spokesman said on Friday.
Accordingly, the man hided the woman in a forest near Helmstedt.
The man was already suspected in May: At that time, it was noticed that no more medication was needed for the mother in need of care.
Investigators said the pensioner that his mother had traveled to Spain.
Because this seemed strange to the officers, they continued to investigate.
It turned out that the two had lived in a shared apartment for more than 15 years and he had most recently cared for them.
The 67-year-old finally admitted the death of his mother.
An autopsy yielded no evidence of a capital crime, according to police.
Fancy an ice cream that glows in the dark?
A British entrepreneur has developed the first ice glowing in the dark – using jellyfish.
Charlie Francis took advantage of the fluorescent properties of marine animals and created a luminous treat from it.
The idea came to him after reading a research paper on jellyfish and was able to convince Chinese scientists to artificially produce the glowing protein.
The ice cream reacts with the tongue when consumed, which raises the pH value in the protein and triggers the glow.
Chris explains that the ice cream reacts to the warmth in the mouth and glows – so the more you lick, the brighter it becomes.
Charlie, founder of ice-cream company "Lick Me I'm Delicious," says: "The product is incredible, but at a very early stage of production, and so you get two grams of it for £200."
The protein used in the ice cream reacts with the tongue at a neutral pH.
When the mouth heats the protein, the pH rises and the ice lights up.
We’ve tested it over the last few months and it seemed ideal to bring it to market on Halloween because it’s having this wonderful luminous effect.
It's probably the most expensive ice cream I've ever made, because the jellyfish fluorescent is four times more expensive than gold.
Every bullet costs me about £140.
But it tastes delicious for that.
Charlie's Bristol-based experimental company is famous for its unusual flavors, which include beer, cheese, beef and gold leaf.
However, his latest creation is to become even more ambitious.
“I’d like to develop an invisible ice cream.”
This is impossible in and of itself due to the refractivation caused by the ice crystles that make up ice cream; but I suspect we find a way to make it.
The ice cream utilizes the fluorescent properties of a jellyfish artificially produced by Chinese scientists
The TSV Morsum is still without any point in the handball association league after six matchdays.
Right now it's time for Ingo Ehlers' team to the derby against TSV Daverden, who celebrated their first victory last weekend.
With this game, the season begins anew for us.
"I hope that we finally have our minds clear," Ehlers says cautiously optimistically.
With the exception of the permanently injured Hendrik Blohme, he finally has his entire squad available.
Daverden, like us, relies on tempo play.
Therefore, we have to keep our error rate low.
First and foremost, however, it is important to increase in coverage, which has recently left anything but a certain impression.
Daverden's coach Thomas Panitz is much more relaxed after the first win of the season against Nordhorn.
The game, of course, was a house number.
But that's why we'ren't the favorite, because anything can always happen in the Derby.
Nevertheless, Panitz now wants to follow up in Morsum to start a series.
Jan-Malte Jodat is missing.
For this, the A-youth Joost Windßuß, who is active with the A-youth-Bundesligisten HC Bremen, celebrates his season debut.
If you’ren’t hot in a game like this, you’re supposed to stay at home.
Halloween 2013: In numbers
When I was little, Halloween had something magical.
My sister and I were allowed to eat sweets, stay up late and dress up for the neighborhood.
Today, I've become more of a Halloween muffle.
In the last two years, I haven't agreed to distribute sweets from my apartment, and I probably won't do it this year either.
But according to statistics, I'm a black sheep when it comes to Halloween.
The majority of Americans – 158 million, to be precise – celebrate Halloween this year, spending a total of $6.9 billion on sweets, costumes and decorations, according to the U.S. Association of Retailers (NRF).
One thing I look forward to every Halloween is the trend values.
The costumes will account for about 1.2 billion of the $6.9 billion spent, according to the NRF.
This year, sexy inanimate items are the last cry.
Women no longer have to wrap themselves in sexy clothes, they can now also embody seductive food such as pizza, hamburgers or carrots.
As for men, I'm expecting loads of zombies thanks to The Walking Dead, and I bet the daft punk spacemen will make it into the Instagram feeds this year.
According to Google, the most searched costumes are zombies, Batman, pirates and witches.
I'm guessing there's nothing wrong with tradition.
We dressed up our dogs last year and weren't alone with it to my astonishment.
In fact, Americans spend $330 million this year on costumes of their pets, according to the NRF.
That’s a lot of “hot dogs.”
As for candy, we'ren't squeamish.
Americans will spend $1.9 billion for it this year, according to The Nielsen Company.
That's about 270 million kilograms of chocolate bars, lollipops, candies and gummy bears.
So good news for, according to the U.S. Department of Commerce, 41 million people pulling through the neighborhoods with the saying “sweet or sour.”
In fact, we will buy over 40 million kilograms of chocolate for Halloween and – don’t let’s fool ourselves.
The only thing we don't want to consume is sugar-sweet Candy Corn; nevertheless, 15 million kilograms of it are sold around Halloween, according to the National Association of Confectionery Manufacturers.
That's about nine billion corn grains.
It's a mystery I haven't been able to solve.
Nothing belongs more to Halloween thanks to houses it haunted.
They have great names like “Terror Behind the Walls” (which, by the way, stands in a real prison), “Howl-O-Scream” and “The House of Shock”.
There are actually 1,200 officially-approved haunted houses in the United States that generate £500 million in proceeds, according to America Haunts, and that includes the wonderful photos in which you’re peeing in your pants and then putting your friends on Facebook, where you won’t get rid of them, leaving a comment like “nice facial expression.”
But let's finally get to the pumpkins.
Charlie Brown introduced us to the Great Pumpkin when we were kids, and carving a pumpkin lantern is like decorating the Christmas tree – something we did from an early age.
Luckily, the “baby in the pumpkin” trend didn’t start until last year thanks to Pinterest, so most of us grew up by carving the pumpkin and not sitting in it.
This year, Americans will spend about $1060 million on pumpkins, according to the U.S. Census Bureau.
The pumpkin lantern, which slowly rots in the front garden, presumably comes from Illinois, where 245 million kilograms of pumpkins were harvested this year.
If you want to score correctly, then call Tim and Susan Mathisdon in Napa, California and carve something pretty out of their 921-kilo pumpkin.
Residential fire in Helmbrechts: Rescue workers recover charred corpse
The firefighting firefight had to disembark in Helmbrechts late Thursday night.
A three-family house in the Hof district catches fire in the night to Friday.
The rescue workers recover a charred body from the house.
Rescue workers have found a charred body in a three-family house during a fire in Helmbrechts.
Whether it was a resident is still unclear, as a spokesman for the police in Bayreuth said.
Late on Thursday evening, it had burned on the ground floor of the house.
When firefighters arrived, the flames were already pounding out of a window.
The emergency services went into the apartment and found the body in a room.
This and another room burned out completely.
The entire ground floor was damaged.
All other residents were not at home during the fire, the police spokesman said.
For the time being, the building is no longer habitable.
The exact cause of the fire was not clear at first.
Germany's largest regional church has embarked on an offensive to promote the profession of pastor.
Background is a possible shortage of pastors: The Evangelical Lutheran State Church Hannover expects that the number of its currently around 1,800 pastors will halve by 2030 according to the current situation.
For young people of today, the profession is very attractive and offers good prospects for the future, said Pastor Mathis Burfien (43) in conversation with the Evangelical Press Service.
It is attractive to be able to determine the workflow and daily routine yourself.
With burfias, the state church has commissioned a pastor on a full place to inspire young people to study theology.
Currently, fewer and fewer young people decide to study theology after graduating from high school.
Burfien attributes this to the process of de-ecclinicalization: "God's voice is quiet, the world is noisy".
The profession is characterized by great freedom and diversity.
I am master of my schedule and can set my priorities where it is important to me.
Pastors could be very close to people as pastors.
They earned as much as teachers and could live well from it.
Certainly more is paid in the free economy, but theologians have a safe employer for this.
This is true of today’s young people, who are not only about careers, but about a meaningful job.
According to the regional church, which covers three-quarters of Lower Saxony, around 60 pastors are currently retiring each year.
At the same time, about 40 graduates of a theology degree begin their vicariate.
In sparsely populated peripheral areas such as the Harz, Emsland or Wendland, it could be difficult to fill positions in the future.
Among other things, Burfien wants to organize study days for young people and also invite celebrities who have studied theology.
The study of theology offers a broad education.
You can even become federal president with it.
Land Rover Rally Series announced
In the interior you will find racing seats and six-point belts as well as a hands-free system.
Options range frequent from reinforced brakes, a service package with support from bowler-works mechanics and logistical support to vehicle storage between events.
Drew Bowler, Managing Director of Bowler Motorsport, explains: “The rally customers who come to bowlers have changed.”
They are not all experienced racers, but people who are looking for excitement and adventure as well as for an achievable path to world-class events.
We are delighted to open this path in partnership with Land Rover and the MSA and believe that the format opens up new opportunities to experience the various rally disciplines in the UK and abroad and prepare newcomers for the rigors and reality of Rally Raid.
We really enjoyed developing the Defender Challenge car – this is going to be an entertaining championship.
In addition, there will be a training and testing day at the Defender Challenge in February as well as the option to participate in desert races in North Africa and the Middle East.
Firefighters called to rescue a pooch stuck 15 metres above the ground on a dangerous rocky outcrop in a quarrry
Cockerspaniel bitch Ruby had run away after a minor traffic accident
Three days later she was spotted by a walker in the quarry in her predicament
Firefighters roped themselves off on rock face and rescued the dog from certain death
The fire brigade managed to bring a pooch to safety, which was stuck on a 15-meter-high protrusion in a rock wall.
The nine-month-old Cockerspaniel bitch Ruby had run away after a traffic accident on Sunday afternoon and survived three days alone before she was rescued from a quarry on Wednesday.
Scott Alderson, 25, and his girlfriend Becky Hall, 20 to whom the dog belongs, came to the Flappit quarry in Denholme, West Yorkshire, to receive Ruby and thanking the firefighters at West Yorkshire Fire and Rescue Service.
They had desperately searched for their missing bitch and posted calls for help on social networks when she escaped into the quarry after the minor traffic accident.
Around 2:15 p.m. on Wednesday, a walker carrying out his dog saw the stranded Ruby on the 15-metre-high heel in the quarry.
Cleckheaton Fire Station's technical rescue team rushed to the spot and abseiled to Ruby's rescue, which was then transported upstairs in a dog box.
Andy Clayton, the technical rescue team's specialist, said: "She was in a dangerous situation.
It was located in the middle of the rock wall – 15 meters from above and the ground.
She didn’t move any muscle during her rescue – she was frozen.
But she's fine now.
She then eat a couple of dog cakes.
This was a rather unusual deployment.
The fact that the dog has been discovered is incredible.
Peter Lau, specialist of the technical rescue team, said: “Ruby was very lucky.”
There was a possibility that she was very badly injured or worse.
Ruby was taken to the veterinarian for examination, but did not notice anything except exhaustion and dehydration.
Becky Hall from Halifax, West Yorkshire, said, “Witnessing the rescue was scary.”
I couldn't believe she was really up there.
It was wonderful to hold her in her arms again.
The veterinarian explained that if the exhaustion had become too great and it had collapsed, it would presumably have crashed.
The firefighters have done great things.
It was really brave what they've done.
We're just so grateful and each of them has been absolutely unbelievable.
Scott Alderson from Keighley added: "We were afraid she was going to fall down, but she stayed on top."
The firefighters were great.
I can't believe where she was.
Mick Jagger explains that he never turned Katy Perry on when she was 18.
Speaking an interview with an Australian radio station this week, the pop star said she had collaborated as a background singer on Jagger's song "Old Habit's Hard" from 2004.
Perry explained she had dinner with the alt-rocker, and that "he dredged me when I was 18."
She added: "That was a long time ago and he was very nice to me."
In a statement, Jagger's representative, 70, said Thursday that he "categorically denies that he's ever made rapprochement attempts at Katy Perry."
The representative added, “Maybeve she confused him with someone else.”
Perry was one of the singers who made a guest appearance on this year's Rolling Stones tour.
Their new album “Prism” landed immediately at #1 this week.
George Kerevan: European unbundling gives Scots the choice
A new day, a new scare storie for independence.
This time we are warned that an independent Scotland is needed to join the Schengen area as a condition for EU membership.
This provides the keyword for stories about passport controls in Berwick and a barbed-wired border along Hadrianswall.
In fact, the Strathclyde template pointed out the possible economic benefits that more freedom of travel in the rest of Europe would bring, but – as expected – this did not matter in the headlines.
Also, no one mentioned that EU member states often bend their strict rules if it is cheaper for them.
Since Scotland does not currently belong to the Schengen area, the continued non-compliancence for Brussels would only be a small concession in return for what one really wants from the Scots.
So actually not a story.
And one that has already come in such a way that it already resembles a fossil: For the first time I heard the “independencence means passport control” duck at least 40 years ago.
An interesting point is lost during the constant repetition of this ancient history.
What one should expect an independent Scotland to dance after the European whistle?
Why trade the London yoke for that of Brussels, especially now?
This is the real European news: the great post-war plan for the unification of Europe has stalled.
With the euro crisis, the Europe project officially died.
All over the EU, parties are gaininging ground that are hostile to the EU or want to throw the euro overbord as a single currency.
Even in Germany, the Eurosceptical party Alternative for Germany – founded only this year – was able to win almost five million votes in the federal election in September and thus effectively expel the Free Democrats (the equivalent to the British Liberal Democrats) from the Bundestag.
There has always been domestic resistance to the plan to create a federalist Europe.
However, the current economic crisis turns out to be a turning point.
Due to the strict austerity policies imposed by Berlin and the European Central Bank in connection with the straitjacket, into which the respective national economy is compelled by sticking to the single currency, many people are of the opinion that the Europe project has gone too far.
The crisis of the euro has little to do with national governments causing excessive budget deficiences – this was only the case in Greece.
Instead, the Eurosystem has nailed its members to exchange rates that are beneficial for German exporters – and German politicians would like to do so.
Without the possibility of a devaluation of the local currency, southern Europe finds itself with a built-in productivity disadvantage against Germany.
The only option is to cut wages and public spending – spurred on by Berlin.
Behind the current budget and currency problems lies a deeper European productivity problem.
As a result of “green” energy directives prescribed by Brussels – a code for subsidizing French and German energy companies at the expense of consumers – European industry pays twice as much for electricity and quadruple for gas compared to the United States.
That's a crippling cost disadvantage, as we've already seen in Grangemouth.
All of the world's wage fixings do not protect the European petrochemical industry from being cornered by cheap American shale gas.
As a result, a revolt is picking up, especially in France, the EU's one-time cheerleader.
After the war, France's political elite saw the EU as a vehicle that could keep Germany at bay and raise Paris on an equal footing with Washington.
But Berlin no longer needs Paris as a gateway to political legitimacy and has imprinted Europe's own economic policy, which is what the ailing French economy is struggling with.
The result: Marine Le Pen's right-wing, anti-EU Front National has just won a decisive by-election, thus relegating the ruling Socialists in third place.
The Front National is now the most popular party in France with 24 per cent of the electoral votes - a timely warning for Britain's Labour party that it can't assume a split in the right-wing spectrum would automatically mean benefits for the left.
What does Le Pen do with her newfound popularity among working-class white French?
She wants to use next year's EU elections to forge an anti-EU, anti-common currency blond in the European Parliament.
If the anti-EU parties do well in these elections – and this is very possible – then for the first time such a bloc could dominate the European Parliament.
This is the point I want to go beyond: in the not too distant future, the growing resentment against the EU and the single currency will come together to abolish the euro.
The EU will not disappear, but it will rather become something like the “Europe of (sovereign) nations” that General de Gaulle favored.
Germany and a few of its satellite economies may stick to the euro, but France and southern Europe will return to their own currencies.
I expect Britain to distance itself from this project in the hopes of flattering itself with the United States.
However, Washington's growing interest in the Pacific is suggesting that Britain will stand alone in Atlantic rain.
Where's Scotland with all of this?
We can decide to be a region of (in the last consequence) Little England.
Or we can defend our own economic interests – which includes telling Berlin and Brussels what they can stuck to where.
I suspect Scotland could drive quite well in a looser European network, provided we keep our own currency.
Cooperating with other similar-thinking countries is easier in a non-federated Europe of nations.
Otherwise, we should consider doing the same to Norway and retaining our economic independence.
The SNP government in Scotland is – remarkably – the most successful political movement against austerity in Europe and won a spectacular majority in 2011 due to the rejection of the proposed (and made) cuts that Labour Finance Minister Alistair Darling and subsequent Tory-Lib Dem coalition were responsible.
It would be ridiculous for Scotland to opt for independence now, only to then be imposed by Berlin and Brussels an austerity.
Early puberty: growing up faster
Afro-American and Hispanic girls tend to reach puberty rather than their white peers, as studies show.
Physical changes don't directly mean puberty
There's no evidence that hormones or other chemicals are responsible
Experts believe the epidemic obesity is a trigger of early puberty
The trend towards early puberty is less pronounced among boys
Former CNN correspondent Pat Etheridige is a journalist specializing in children's health and family issues.
Should a mother be worried if her daughter's breasts and pubic hair are already growing at the age of seven or eight?
At this week's American Academy of Pediatrics Association's annual conference in Orlando, Florida, pediatric endocrinologist Dr. Paul Kaplowitz stated that these early bodical changes were relatively normal among American girls and represented a new norm.
“I spend a lot of time calming parents – usually that’s no indication that puberty is now fast,” Kaplowitz explained.
Obvious indications of the development, such as growing breasts, pubic and armpit hair as well as body odor, are evident in girls earlier.
Nevertheless, there was only a slight shift in the age of the menarch (occurrence of the first menstrual period) in the last 40 years.
In the United States, the average age is 12.5 years, slightly less than 12.75 in 1970.
"If the development of the breasts begins, it will take at least another two to three years for menarches," said Kaplowitz, also author of the book "Early puberty in girls: The essential guide to dealing with this frequently occurring problem."
Time is the most accurate test of how puberty progresses.
It’s controversial, which is actually the onset of puberty, but it’s “early ready” if breast augmentation is accompanied by a growth spurt before the age of eight.
In most cases, the process slows or stagnates – something a pediatrician can closely monitor.
With faster progress, tests by an endicronologist could be indicated so that serious problems such as tumors or cysts can be ruled out.
There are treatment methods to delay early menstruation and the defense of another consequence: premature aging of the bones, which can ultimately lead to growth inhibition and thus small body growth in adults.
The recommendations for drug or hormonal therapy are based on the age of the age of the child, the speed of development, the growth rate and the emotional maturity.
Psycho-social aspects are also important.
Kaplowitz is reticent when it comes to medication, but he admits: “Suppressing puberty can mitigate behavioral problems and the feelings of girls other than being their peers.”
The other big problem is understandable: parents simply don't want their very young daughters to have the period.
“They’re worried about the risk of pregnanciation or how they handle the hygienic side,” Kaplowitz said.
“It was a shock,” recalls a woman whose daughter got her days at ten.
Even if there were first signs and we had talked about menstruation, she was emotionally unprepared.
She came terrified and agitated home from school because she was the first in her circle of friends.
There are many fully published theories about the causes of early puberty.
Yet there is no conclusive evidence that hormones in milk or other foods, chemicals in the environment, or sexual messages in the media would be causative.
Boyns, much like girls used to enter puberty.
Kaplowitz believes that the most convincing premise is the epidemic of obesity.
He supported the implementation of a study in 2001 on girls aged six to nine, which establishes a link between body fat and entry into puberty.
Other investigations support this conclusion, but there are many other factors that played a role.
In this country, African-American and Hispanic girls tend to reach puberty rather than their white comrades.
There are different explanations for this.
Global, early puberty seems to be influenced by all sorts of things, from economic conditions to the climate to the genes.
Another mystery: Although boys’ facial and pubic hair is growing earlier, the trend towards full previous puberty is not as pronounced as in girls.
Other doctors at the AAP conference underlined the complexity of the issue.
The appearance of acne and pubic hair is widespread even among babies and toddlers.
"We have to be careful how to identify the true beginning of puberty," said Dr. Lawrence Silverman, a pediatric endocrinologist at Goryeb Children's Hospital in Morristown, New Jersey.
Parents shouldn't hesitate to seek advice from their pediatrician on how to talk to their child.
“This can mean that you have to have a conversation sooner than expected,” Kaplowitz said.
If you stay calm, the child usually reacts well.
Girls who blossom early need the assurance that the process is a normal part of life, even if it enters earlier than thought.
Jumbo manufacturers argue over seating width in face of big orders
There has been an altercation between leading aircraft manufacturers over the width of seats in the tourist class on long-haul flights, indicating the tone for a bitter clash at the Dubai Airshow this month.
The dispute revolves around the width of the seats offered to passengers on long flights in the tourist class – not always those airlines strive for most, but this designated space is the key to the efficiency assertions of the latest Airbus SAS and Boeing Co. aircraft.
Airbus this week called for an industry standard that prescribes a seat width of at least 46 cm (18 inches) in the tourist class, but U.S. arch-rival Boeing says airlines should decide that.
The altercation comes at a time as aircraft manufacturers are vying to sell even larger versions of their twin-engine long-haul aircraft, expecting record orders at the November 17-21 event.
How the rear part of the aircraft is designed – in particular whether there are rows with nine or ten seats – is crucial for the stated economic results that the new “mini-jumbo” design is intended to deliver.
Boeing claims its modernized “777X” accommodates 406 people at tourist-class seats that are over 17 inches wide, allowing ten per row.
Airbus declares that the competing version of the A350 transports 350 people in 18-inch-wide seats in the tourist class, with there being nine per row.
Airplane giants often distribute blows about technical details via advertising in the trade press.
Airbus is now appealing directly to the public ahead of the Dubai Airshow, where the 777X is expected to make the race with over 100 orders.
Recently, the company unveiled an ad that could be the start of a new ad war and showed donors a picture in which three people squeezed together in a restaurant, plus the title: “Would you accept that?”
“Boeing proposes long-haul flights in seats that are tighter than regional turbo-props,” says John Leahy.
Faced with changing dietary habits, people are getting fatter, but plane seats haven't radically changed.
Between the early 1970s, when the Boeing 747 defined modern long-haul travel, and at the end of the century, the weight of male average-American between 40 and 49 years has increased by 10 percent, according to data from the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services.
The waist circumference of male average American in the 21st century is 100.8 cm, according to US health statistics.
Airbus says its rival adheres to a seating concept from the 19500s, when the average body circumference of the freshly christened "Jet Set" was narrower.
According to Airbus, investigations have been carried out, which have shown that an additional tariff (2.54 cm) per seat increased the sleep quality by 53 percent.
Boeing denies Airbus' figures on seat dimensions, saying it's not at the discretion of manufacturers to decide how airlines shape the balance between air fares and facilities.
The company also states that, according to investigations, the experience in the aircraft depends on more than the seat width.
“It’s really boiling down to giving airlines flexibility so they can do what they think they need to be successful,” Boeing’s Kent Craver said in a statement.
They don't want us to dictate to them what makes them profitable.
They know their business better than anyone.
For passengers, it's about more elbow clearance, but for manufacturers it's increasingly becoming an issue that can have an impact on sales.
Behind the dispute is a race for aircraft orders with an estimated volume of at least $700 billion by list prices in the coming decades, which could have a lasting impact on U.S. or Europe's export balance.
As Reuters first reported in July, the seating layout is the driving force behind the clash over the new jets.
Both Airbus and Boeing claim 20 percent more efficiency per seat at their latest twin-engine long-haul designs than the market leader in this segment, the 365-seat Boeing 77-300ER.
Boeing's statements on the results depend in part on comparing the 777X with its ten seats per series with the original design of nine seats in the 777.
The increased costs per unit are still lower than currently used machines with ten seats per row.
“Boeing therefore squeezes more seats into its aircraft to make it more competitive with our products,” says Kevin Keniston, head of passenger comfort at European Airbus.
On the other hand, analysts say full cabins with ten-seats per-row at existing 7777s suggested that many passengers opted for the tighter arrangement, which could be accompanied by cheaper airfares.
“Eighteen-inch seat width would be great for passengers, but the reality is: from an economic point of view, the Airbus offer is steered by the 777 threat,” says Mary Kirby, expert in cabin set-up, founder and editor of Runway Girl Network.
Airbus and Boeing do not offer seats, but a catalogue of suppliers from which the airlines can choose.
The world-traveling jet sellers are even carrying tape size with them in order to measure the set-up in the competition.
Even if they advertise the comfort, all manufacturers still offer jets with a high-density design for low-cost airlines and regional flights.
Airbus offers an A350 with ten-seater rows, but hasn't sold one, according to its own statement.
Until recently, Airbus emphasized the importance of more adaptability of the cabins and offered wide seats at the aisles for some jets.
Without the support of the only other manufacturer of major modern jets, experts say the call for a new industry standard would presumably fizzle out, but could distract from the wave of 777X sales.
New anti-nicotine vaccine could eliminate the pleasure of smoking
Scientists have developed an anti-nicotine vaccine that could spoil the fun of smoking.
A single dose of the vaccine could protect mice from nicotine addiction for a lifetime.
Before human tests, more attempts are needed, which may take a few more years, but Professor Ronald Crystal of Weill Cornell Medical College in New York said the first results were good.
“We are very hopeful that this type of vaccination strategy can once help millions of smokers who have attempted to stop and tried all the methods available on the market, but have had to experience that their nicotine addiction is stronger than these approaches.”
The new vaccine contains a harmless virus that has been altered to include the genetic information of nicotine antibodies.
The virus selectively infects liver cells, which then produce a constant stream of antibodies.
The antibodies chase the nicotine molecules in the blood and neutralize them before they reach the brain, as a result of which a smoker does not experience nicotine high.
In trials, vaccinated mice given nicotine maintained their normal level of activity.
Mice without vaccination, on the other hand, had taken a “time-out”, explained the researchers, a sign that the nicotine had reached their brains.
The experiments are described in the journal Science Translational Medicine.
Former tobacco vaccines failed because they contained antibodies.
Vaccinations had to be given so often to maintain antibody levels that they were expensive and impractical.
However, the cost of the new vaccine is likely to be significantly lower because it transforms liver cells into antibody factories.
As Professor Crystal said, a future vaccine for humans, if it is completely safe, could be given children before they were tempted to try a cigarette and thus prevent nicotine addiction.
However, it is more likely that smokers would use it to quit.
“Then they know that when they start smoking again, they don’t feel pleasure anymore because of the nicotinine vaccine, and that helps them to put off the habit,” he explains.
British scientists said the findings were interesting, but at the same time warned that further research was still needed.
Tripodi denies having been influenced by Obeid
Former minister Joe Tripodi from New South Wales will launch an investigation by state corruption oversight.
The former NSW minister denies having changed regulations on maritime leasing at the request of his political mentor Eddie Obeid, who owned covert involvement in three properties on state-controlled land.
The Independent Commission Against Corruption (ICAC) on Friday expanded its investigation into whether Obeid influenced various ministers of state to extend leases at Circular Quay, where the Obeids operated two restaurants and a cafe, without an invitation to tender after their expiration.
Tripodi is now being investigated that Tripodi had known about Obeid's undercover interest in the properties after Tripodi's former Deputy Chief of Staff Lynne Ashpole testified accordingly on Thursday.
In years of talks that began in 2005, the government had pushed for public tenders for the leases.
The tenants were against it and also wanted longer runtimes.
In 2009, the lease agreements were extended without a public tender at Circular Quay, which earned the Obeids €2.5 million annually.
Tripodi, who was port minister from February 2006 to November 2009, had originally advocated for public tenders.
However, he denied having made the changes at the request of Obeid, which, Tripodi confirmed, had urged a reshaping of the government's leasing regulations.
A telephone transcript submitted to the ICAC showed conversations from August and September 2007 between Obeid, Tripodi and Steve Dunn, a senior official who had moved to the port ministry after working under Obeid at the Fisheries Authority.
“Was the topic discussed in these phone conversations was the elaboration of the commercial leasing arrangements?” Deputy Commissioner Anthony Whealy Tripodi asked.
“No,” Tripodi replied.
I can't remember what's been discussed, but it definitely wasn't.
Definitely not between me and Mr Obeid.
Israeli warplanes attack target in Syria, according to official sources
Israeli fighter planes attacked a target in the Syrian port of Latakia on Thursday night, a senior government official confirmed to Fox News.
The officer did not explain what the target had been, but said it was at least one.
According to the Associated Press, the target was Russian SA-125 missiles.
At least twice this year, Israel has already carried out aerial strikes on missile shipments in Syria.
Foreign workers with 457 vises could be subjected to 'equitity test'
The government is considering an “authenity test” for foreign workers with 457 visas as part of expanded measures.
Should the test be introduced, it would be used via a criterion to prevent the 457 from being used to fill unqualified workplaces or as a back door for the influx of family and friends into Australia.
Today, a discussion proposal by the government was published after former Labor Party MP Maxine McKew condemned the government's comments with regard to foreign workers because they could be seen as an insult by Australia's neighbors.
“Vociferous explanations such as ‘foreigners are supposed to put themselves in the back’ and ‘Hiring Australians first’ are a very unpleasant relapse in times when unions have demanded a protected labour market,” she told the Australia India Institute today.
Historically, this meant that white workers should be protected – and if some in the region heard an echo of this historical artifact, I wouldn’t be surprised.
The discussion proposal outlines twelve measures once envisaged by former Immigration Minister Chris Bowen.
Immigration Minister Brendan O'Connor, who stayed in Sri Lanka yesterday for talks on human smuggling, has implemented five of the recommended changes, the others are still being considered.
If the “authenity criterion” is adopted, then someone applying for a visa could be reviewed whether “the application is genuine in cases in which the applicant is related or personally associated with the holder or a relevant person of the supporting entity.”
Firms could also be required to account of the number of 457 visa holders, after in the past companies that initially wanted to support only a small number of workers eventually hired hundreds.
Meanwhile, a 35-year-old Sri-Lankan asylum seeker presumably died of a heart attack after arriving with a refugee boat on Christmas Island this week.
The disturbed nine-year-old son of the man, who had come to Australia with him, has been cared for since his father's death on Wednesday by an adult cousin who was also on the boat.
Australian authorities took the man to Christmas Island Hospital where he died.
A study has been announced by the UK government to increase the benefits of the HS2 railway project for Scotland.
HS2 Ltd's work proposes to establish high-speed links between Scotland and the north of England as early as the beginning of Phase 1 in 2026.
Transport Secretary Baroness Kramer said the project "will bring Britain closer together."
Scotland's transport minister, Keith Brown, said he was very pleased to work with the British government on this plan.
Phase 1 consists of a new high-speed rail connection between London and the West Midlands.
Upon completion of Phase 2, there will be connections to Manchester and Leeds.
In June, the government corrected the estimated cost for the construction of the high-speed road between London and northern England from £32.7bn to £42.6bn.
The British government, which is in talks with Transport Scotland, has contracted HS2 Ltd to look for further route capacity and travel time improvements for northern England and Scotland.
This includes the possibility of an ultimately three-hour or less travel time from Glasgow and Edinburgh to London.
Baroness Kramer said: "Our goal for HS2 is a truly national route network that brings Britain and its cities closer together."
We're moving forward with HS2 because it brings tremendous benefits.
Without it, we are facing a capacity crisis in our rail network.
Yet it's also about the connection between 18 British cities, including Glasgow and Edinburgh, which is being improved thanks to HS2.
Scottish Secretary of State Alistair Carmichael commented: "Today's announcement is good news for Scotland."
On behalf of the Scottish Government, Keith Brown urged Carmichael "unequivocally" campaigning for Scottish integration into the HS2 network.
Brown said: "The high-speed rail link holds the potential of enormous economic benefits for Scotland, but at the same time Scotland's economic power also provides an argument for fast rail links across the UK.
That's why we are pleased to work as partners of the UK Government and explore the options for a high-speed rail link in Scotland that creates benefits for everyone and complements the Glasgow-Edinburgh connection, which the Scottish Government is already planning.
I look forward to looking forward to examining the investigation report with UK ministers next year and to decide on the next steps together.
Regulations on electronic devices on aircraft remain provisionally in force in Australia
Australian air passengers must continue to shut down tablets and smartphones on take-off and landing, despite efforts in the U.S. to loosen regulations on such devices.
The U.S. Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) allows U.S. airlines to change their guidelines so that passengers can read, watch or play e-books on their devices during critical flight phases, if they are in “flight mode”.
During the majority of the flight, passengers are already allowed this, but many people find it annoying that they aren’t allowed to read their e-books on take-off and landing.
Australian companies are observing the decision that U.S. airlines will have to handle vast amounts of work to implement, but are not pursuing immediate plans to change their regulations.
The air traffic control agency CASA also said it would look at the announcement, but stressed that the restrictions on the use of electronic devices in critical flight phases in Australia still apply.
“CASA does not currently have any specific regulations regarding the use of electronic devices on the aircraft,” it said.
The issue is subject to provisions requiring airlines to maintain safety at all times and passengers must follow the safety instructions given by cabin crew.
Virgin has already held talks with CASA on an expansion of its Wi-Fi entertainment system on flights and said it is open to changes, but these must be initiated by the legislature.
“We would welcome CASA’s approval of electronic device approval because we are convinced that this would enhance the customer’s experience after now offering (wireless entertainment) during flight,” a spokesperson said.
Qantas said it would remain with the current regulations until further notice.
“Our current guidelines stipulate that electronic devices must not be allowed to be used on take-off and landing, and we currently have no plans to change that,” the company said.
The FAA regulations apply to U.S. airlines.
However, we are always interested in developments in regulations from which passengers can benefit, and will certainly take a close look at the FAA decision and the reasons behind it.
The decision has a different effect for U.S. companies depending on the age of the fleet.
They must demonstrate that their planes can tolerate radio interference from mobile devices, changing manuals, training materials, carry-on baggage programs, as well as customer briefing.
“After an airline confirms the tolerance of its fleet, it can allow passengers to use portable, lightweight electronic devices such as tablets, e-book readers and smartphones at any height,” the FAA announced.
In rare cases in poor visibility, the crew will instruct passengers to switch off the equipment during landing.
The group also recommended storing heavier devices safely under the seat or in the compartments above the seats during take-off and landing.
Before the precinct derby against Schalke, travelling BVB supporters with light rockets had caused ugly scenes in the Veltins Arena.
Borussia Dortmund then announced harsh consequences.
Ahead of Friday's game of the Bundesliga against VfB Stuttgart, the Ultras reacted with a silence - initially.
It was an unfamiliar, almost spooky acoustics, which offered itself to the viewer until shortly before the kick-off of the Bundesliga match between the German runner-up Borussia Dortmund and the VfB Stuttgart at Signal Iduna Park.
Mostly the guest fans could be heard with their chants.
The southern grandstand, on the other hand, where the most loyal of the loyal - and loudest - BVB fans stand, initially presented itself exceptionally quietly: no chants, no battle cries.
The podium of the Capo, which otherwise coordinates the joint cheer, remained unoccupieded.
And at the very front of the grandstand a large flag with the inscription "Sektion Stadionverbot".
Only as goalkeeper Roman Weidenfeller, as always the first BVB player to enter the pitch, briefly blazed cheers.
When his team-mates followed, there was loud singing and waving scarves - but by no means everywhere.
In the central blocks 12 and 13 of the southern grandstand, nothing moved.
Even that stadium announcer Norbert Dickel once again listed the positive experiences of the last few days - derby victory, victory over Arsenal, contract extension of coach Jürgen Klopp - did not cause a celebration mood.
Only five minutes before kick-off, the Capo climbed his podium - and the tension, which had hitherto been gripped with hands, discharged itself in a loud cry, as Dickel questered the mood on the ranks in the stadium as usual.
As always, it was the turn of the southern grandstand - and finally the loudest place in the stadium was here again as usual.
And at the latest, when with kick-off from the southern grandstand came the loud invitation "Derby winner stands up", everything was again as usual.
Silence before was arguably a reaction to the events of the past few days.
Immediately before the turf derby at FC Schalke 04, some of the BVB supporters had fired light rockets into the spectator ranks and on the pitch - and in the process almost goalkeeper Roman Weidenfeller.
The game was kicked off late because of the chaotic scenes.
BVB boss Hans-Joachim Watzke had then received the tops of the Dortmund ultras in his office - and if Watzke's words are, this was a rather uncomfortable appointment for the fan representatives.
"There will be massive cuts for the entire ultra movement," the BVB boss proclaimed at a podium discussion in Frankfurt on Thursday night.
As an immediate measure, Watzke forbaded the ultras a choreography for the home game against VfB Stuttgart.
"That's what I banned," he explained.
We can't give up the zero-tolerance policy on pyrotechnics.
In addition, the ultras have been shown various possibilities of punishment - until Tuesday they now have time to decide on one.
Otherwise, Watzke said, the BVB will set a sanction by the end of next week.
"That's going to be drastic," Watzke assured.
It is interesting to see how the reactions in the southern grandstand will turn out.
Pawnshops are booming in Singapore as the crisis has arrived in the middle class
In a pawn shop at the Bendemeer shopping centre in Singapore, Janani Amirthalinga exchanges a golden bangle, a ring and a pair of earrings to pay her daughters' school fees.
“My husband and I have just bought a house, there’s all my money in it,” says Amirthalinga.
Although she earns € 3,000 (about €1,730) every month as an administrative employee and her husband also works, the monthly family income isn’t sufficient, she explains.
In fact, demand in parts of Southeast Asia – where household debt is increasing – is so great that ValueMax, where it made its exchange, became the third pawn shop listed on the Singapore Stock Exchange this week.
Pledging jewelry is not only a quick way to get money – $1,300 in the case of Janani Amirthalinga – but also almost as cheap as an unsecured bank loan.
Typically, pawnshops in Singapore demand an effective annual interest rate of 17 per cent, which is just above the 15.4 per cent the United Overseas Bank, a local lender with a branch in the same shopping centre.
Pfandhäuser offer the advantage, however, that no verification of creditworthiness or proof of income is required here, which is why loans are possible faster than with banks.
For this reason, millions of people in the region are turninging to pawn shops after families feel the pressure from rising living costs as well as increasing household and consumer debt.
After five years of robust growth since the global financial crisis and cheap loans due to a loose fiscal policy in the developed economies, families with lower and mid-income families seek to make up for the difference with a stuttering economy.
This week, rating agency Standard & Poor's growing household debt, mainly due to rising mortgages, described as a risk factor for the creditworthiness of Asian banks.
Malaysia, Thailand and Singapore have the highest household debt in Asia in relation to gross domestic product, according to the agency.
Malaysia is at the top of the list with 80 percent of GDP, an increase of 60 percent still in 2008.
Economists are also worried because of Thailand's high consumer debt, which narrowly emerged from a technical recession this week.
On Thursday, the data showed a continuing export weakness and a slowdown in consumer demand.
“In the end, this means that in view of rising costs, people in the middle to lower segment (the income scale) will try to supplement their incomes wherever possible,” says Song Seng Wun, an economist at CIMB, a Malaysian bank.
Historic highs in gold prices over the past two years have intensified the trend towards pledging personal belongings as people take the opportunity to turn the family's jewellery into cash.
In Singapore, about 70 percent of pledged items in the city-state's 200 pawn shops are gold.
People say to themselves, “The gold price looks good, let’s pledge grandmother’s gold chain and get it back next month.”
Thailand's largest pawn shop operator, EasyMoney, has witnessed a 20 per cent customer growth in its stores in recent months.
The growth in the deposit business is so strong that ValueMax, operator of the Bendemeer branch and 15 others in Singapore, wants to expand not only into neighboring Malaysia – where the company operates four houses – but also outside Asia, according to Yeah Lee Ching, managing director of ValueMax.
The company is funding that with 60 percent of the $66 million it took this week with the IPO in Singapore.
While some lenders were caught in the crossfire because of high interest rates, Yeah explains that Pfandhäuser not only offered cheaper interest rates than other lenders, but also did not directly increase debt.
“Customers borrow items they already own, and liquefying personal valuables doesn’t enlarge household schooling,” she explains.
Pfandleih’s business is increasingly socially accepted as short-term, hedged financing.
Also, not all people who use pawnbrokes are in financial trouble.
Wealthy people from Singapore also come to ValueMax's stores to pledge gold bullions and Rolex watches, for which they receive up to 60 percent of the purchase price in cash.
Customers from every social class come to us.
“This includes wealthy people borrowing something for a business or investment in the short term, or small companies that need to bridge bottlenecks in cash-flow,” says Yeah.
Sometimes they just need money very quickly.
NSNA sucks data from Google and Yahoo - Snowden wants to help
The intelligence revealer Edward Snowden has fundamental interest in helping Germany clarify the increasingly explosive NSA spy affair.
According to Hans-Christian Ströbele, his surprise meeting with Snowden in Russia was about the conditions under which the ex-intelligence employee would testify at a German public prosecutor's office or before a committee of inquiry.
Snowden had referred to his complicated legal situation, Ströbele told the ARD magazine "Panorama".
Bailout over 325 million Australian dollars for Tasmanian healthcare
The Australian government insists on the harsh conditions linked to the $325 million bailout for Tasmanic healthcare to make sure the state's government can't waste the money.
Federal Health Minister Tanya Plibersek has announced the Commonwealth will take "immediately measures" to avert a crisis caused by an aging population of the island nation, a higher quota of chronic diseases and system restrictions.
Funding for a four-year period has been decided after government deliberaties with independent Tasman MP Andrew Wilkie.
"The government has laced an emergency rescue package that, in our view, addresses the unique challenges facing the state," Plibersek said.
The $325 million package includes $31 million of emergency aid for electoral benefits.
Another 2600 operations, including orthopaedic interventions and treatments due to grey staring, are intended to eliminate residues.
In addition, there is money for day clinics in Hobart and Launceston, better follow-up care after inpatient stays, specialist training, mental health services, and the introduction of a system for electronic recording of health data in local hospitals.
“These investments are in line with the ideas that affected clinic staff have told me are the best way to improve the Tasmanic healthcare system,” said Tanya Plibersek.
The minister insisted on strict reporting and accountability from the Tasman government.
The state would have to maintain the current level of funding in order to receive money from the Commonwealth and report monthly on where the additional funds were spent.
A three-member commission will be set up to ensure that the state provides health services as effectively and efficiently as possible.
As Wilkie explained today, the $325 million would be of little use if “they aren’t accompanied by real reforms that put the public Tasmanic health system on a more sustainable course.”
Nevertheless, he praised the government for responding to his request for urgent assistance, which he first expressed to the Prime Minister in early May.
“I am confident that federal aid will make a huge contribution to taking the state’s public health system off the worry list,” Wilkie said.
According to the state government's government, these electoral services will largely make up for the recent cuts.
Yet the opposition's health policy spokesman at the federal level, Peter Dutton, believes that today's announcement is merely a "paving solution."
"We're in today's situation because the Labor state government withdrewed $430 million from the health care system," he told ABC TV.
It can't be that a state government pulls nearly half a billion dollars out of the system and the Commonwealth puts $300 million into it and then pretends that's good news.
Dutton urged Plibersek to guarantee that not a single dollar of the bailout would be spended for additional bureaucracy.
Guillaume Nicloux's adaptation of Denis Diderot's novel impresses with exceptional production equipment and contemporaneous details, but is also heavier food than necessary.
The plot, set in France of the 1760s, tells the poignant story of Suzanne, a young noblewoman sent to the monastery by her family.
As she rebels, she is confronted with the extreme cruelty of an extremely sadistic superior and becomes the object of erotic fascination of another.
The film never slides into lust or sensation – and that’s where its problem lies.
The solemn seriousness, with which the film is told, also makes it a torturous penitential shirt for the audience.
Syria has destroyed its ability to manufacture chemical weapons, according to a surveillance group
Syria has destroyed important chemical weapons manufacturing facilities and poity gas ammunition, the global chemical weapons monitoring group said on Thursday, while heavy clashes occurred in the north of the country near one of the sites where suspected toxic substances are stored.
Also on Thursday, a Syrian activist group announced that more than 120,000 people had been killed almost three years ago since the civil war began.
The announcement of the Organization for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons (OPCW) came a day before the appointment at
November 1, which the Hague-based organization Damascus had set to destroy or “inoperable” all chemical weapons manufacturing facilities, including all machines for mixing chemicals into poison gas and filling ammunition.
Completing this process, which is basically the first stage of destruction, is an important milestone in an ambitious timetable aimed at destroying Syria’s chemical weapons by mid-2014.
The destruction of the facilities means that Syria can no longer produce new chemical weapons.
Damascus, however, has yet to start destroying existing weapons and supplies.
According to assumptions, there are about 1,000 tons of chemicals and weapons in the country, including mustard gas and the nerve gas sarin.
The announcement came at a time when fighting took place in the city of Safira on Thursday, of which experts say there is a production facility for chemical weapons as well as deposits here, as the UK-based Syrian Observatory for Human Rights announced.
The activist group, which is tracking the number of dead via a network of activists in Syria, said 120,296 people had died on Thursday.
Of these, 61,067 are civilians, including 6,365 children.
On the part of the government, 29,954 of them belonged to President Bashar Assad's army, 18,678 were pro-government fighters and 187 Lebanese Hezbollah militias.
Also among the dead are 2,202 deserted army soldiers and 5,375 fighters of the opposition, many of them foreigners.
On July 25, the U.N. estimated the number of deaths in this conflict at 100,000 since March 2011.
This figure has not been updated so far.
The conflict has forced about two million people to flee the country.
Assad's troops have been battling for weeks in Safira against the rebels, many of which have links to al-Qaeda groups.
The Observatory said there had been losses on both sides on Thursday but did not give details.
The fighting underscores the danger faced by chemical weapons inspectors, because their mission to liberate Syria from its toxic arsenal of weapons is a race against time in the midst of a raging civil war.
According to the OPCW, which works closely with the United Nations, the team is “now convinced that it has seen all the important production and mixing/filling facilities mentioned by Syria and has witnessed its destruction.”
Therefore, “no further inspection activities are currently planned.”
Earlier in the week, inspectors had said they had completed the first round of checks and visited 21 out of 23 sites specified by Damascus.
Because of safety concerns, the visit of two facilities had not been possible, the inspectors said.
On Thursday, OPWC stated that the two sites had been “abandoned, according to the Syrian statement ... and the objects of the chemical weapons program existing there have been relocated to other mentioned facilities that have been inspired.”
It wasn't immediately clear if the site in Safira was one of the two establishments the OPCW inspectors couldn't visit.
Syria has put forward a plan for the complete destruction of its chemical weapons, which must be approved next month by OPWC's executive committee.
“I’m taking the hat off to the bravery and courage you’ve all shown in fulfilling the most challenging mission ever carried out by that organization,” said Ahmed Uzumcu, the Surveillance Organization’s director-general in a commentary published by OPCW.
For three years now, the mainly Sunni Muslim rebels have been fighting against Assad's government and its security forces, to which many members of the Alawites belong, a secession of Shiite Islam.
With regard to further developments, the head of the observation group, Rami Abdurrahman, said there had been a powerful explosion at an air defense facility in Syria's coastal province of Latakia on Wednesday.
The cause of the explosion is unknown, he said.
Anger over verdict for Bali bombers
Survivors and relatives of the 202 people who died in the Bali bombing in 2002 reacted angrily about the verdict for the last of the instigators to be brought to justice as Umar Patek, in her view, was to be shooked downright.
Patek, who was on the run for almost ten years as one of Southeast Asia's most wanted persons, was sentenceded to 20 years in prison yesterday for his role in the construction of explosive devices for the bomb attack.
With suspended sentence, he could be dismissed after 15 years.
The 45-year-old was found guilty of mass murder over attacking two nightclubs in Kuta's popular tourist district where 202 people died, including 88 Australians, and many more injured.
A guilty verdict was also made on a series of terrorism-related charges, including a wave of explosives attacks on churches in Indonesia on Christmas Eve in 2000.
Prosecutors had demanded a life imprisonment, even if it could have applied for the man, who is also referred to as "Demolition Man" because of his reputation as a measurable bomb maker.
The decision has evoked painful memories at June Corteen, a mother from Perth who lost her 39-year-old twin daughters Jane and Jenny in the devastation caused by Patek and his co-conspirators nearly ten years ago.
Struggling with tears, she said Patek should have been sentenced to death.
I really mean, he should follow in the footsteps of the other guys.
"He should be confronted with a firing squad," Corteen told AAP.
I have to live every day that I won't have any more grandchildren and I'll never see my daughters again.
The Sari Club was razed to the ground when, on October 12, 2002, shortly after 2 p.m., exploded in a small truck parked outside the nightclub.
Peter Hughes was at Paddy's Bar, where just 20 seconds earlier a suicide bomber exploded a backpack filled with explosives.
He fell into a coma for a month after the attack and “died” three times while hanging a life support system.
Hughes said Patek deserved the same fate as the three other members of the Jemaah Islamiah terrorist cell – Amrozi, Mukhlas and Imam Samudra – who were responsible for the carnage and were executed four years ago.
Truly, this guy was supposed to get the death penalty before anyone else.
Letting him alive – no, there’s no reason to keep him alive.
Getting 20 years after he killed 202 people and injured many hundreds isn't much.
Patek is the last of the Bali bombers to stand trial.
He evaded nearly a decade of arrest, but was eventually apprehended in the Pakistani city of Abbottabad in January 2011, where U.S. forces nearly four months later killed former al-Qaeda chief Osama bin Laden.
During the trial, an FBI agent testified that intelligence reports had yielded an indication that Patek was in Pakistan to meet bin Laden with the aim of rebuilding the connection between Southeast Asian terrorist groups and al-Qaeda.
“He didn’t turn himself in,” Corteen said.
Until recently, he has shown no remorse for how much suffering he has inflicted on other people.
The verdict came ahead of the ten-year anniversary of this year's attack, which will be celebrated with commemorations in Bali and Australia.
"There will be a lot of tears this year," Corteen said.
Patek can still appeal his verdict.
FAA: Passengers can now use electronic devices in airplanes (but don't make cell phone calls)
Air travelers can now use their electronic devices for reading, working, playing, listening to music and watching movies from departure to landing, but can't make phone calls, according to the new guidelines issued by the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) in the U.S. on Thursday.
However, passengers should not expect these changes to be implemented immediately.
How quickly they would be implemented depended on the respective airline, explained FAA employee Michael Huerta at a press conference.
The airlines must demonstrate to the FAA that aircraft comply with the new directives and that they have updated the training manuals of the flight staff as well as the arrangements for storing the equipment in accordance with the new regulations.
The FAA said it had already received plans from some airlines looking to expand the use of portable electronic devices in aircraft.
Among these airlines were Delta and JetBlue.
“Depending on the state of the plan, we could authorize the extended use of electronic devices very soon,” the FAA announced.
Currently, passengers will have to shut down smartphones, tablets and other devices when the aircraft doors are closed.
They are only allowed to take them off again when the plane has reached an altitude of three kilometers and the captain gives the green light.
Upon entry into the landing approach, passengers have to switch them off again and are not allowed to take off again before the aircraft has landed.
Under the new guidelines, airlines whose planes are secured against electronic interference could allow their passengers to use the devices even on take-off, landing and on the track, the FAA explained.
Most of the new commercial aircraft and other aviators that have been upgraded for the use of Wi-Fi at higher altitudes should meet the criteria.
Laura Glading, president of professional flight attendant association, welcomed the changes.
“If the new regulation is safely implemented – and we are working closely with the airline in this regard – both sides will benefit,” Glading commented.
We are honestly tired of having to feel like a watcher on this issue.
Nevertheless, internet connections for surfing, exchanging e-mails, text messages or downloading data under three kilometers remained prohibited, the authority explained.
Passengers are urged to put smartphones, tablets and other devices into flight mode.
So still no “Words With Friends,” the Scrabble-relating online game played by actor Alec Baldwin on his smartphone in 2011, when he had to publicly leave an American Airlines jet because he refused to shut down the device while the plane was parked at the gate.
And heavier devices such as laptops must continue to be stowed away, as there is a risk of injury if they fly through the cabin.
Mobile phone conversations during the flight will also remain prohibited.
The regulatory competence for telephone conversations lies with the Federal Communications Commission (FCC), not with the FAA.
FAA may lift ban on certain electronic devices on take-off and landing
Last month, Mark Rosenker of the traffic safety agency's National Transportation Safety Board, safety expert on national traffic issues on CBS News, said cellphones would still be considered a risk.
“Mobile phones are really a problem, not only because they could potentially cause interference with navigation devices, but because we know from the FCC that transmission from the air can also disrupt transmission masts,” Rosenker said.
An industry advisory committee launched by the FAA to investigate this issue last month recommended that the government allow a wider use of personal electronic devices.
In recent years, the pressure on the FAA to loosen restrictions on use has increased.
Critics such as Democratic Senator Claire McCaskill of Missouri argue that there are no valid security reasons for the ban.
The restrictions are also increasingly difficult to enforce because the use of the devices has become ubiquitous.
In some studies, there is talk that up to one-third of the passengers forget or ignores the shutdown of the device.
The FAA began restricting the use of electronic devices by air travelers in 1966, after reports of disturbances of navigation and communication devices by passenger-carrying FM radios became known, which at the time represented the latest technical trend.
New aircraft are far more dependent on electrical systems than previous generations of aircraft, but they are also engineered and approved by the FAA in such a way that they don’t mind.
Airlines have been providing Wi-Fi to passengers for several years when the altitude is reached.
The aircraft adapted to Wi-Fi systems are also insensitive to interference.
The vast majority of passenger aircraft should be suitable for the greater use of electronic devices according to the new guidelines, Huerta said.
Today's electronic devices generally emit considerably less radio radiation than previous generations.
For example, e-readers only emit minimal radiation when flipping around.
However, when downloading or submitting data, the transfer values are stronger.
Among the advocates of a loosening of restrictions on device use by passengers is Amazon.com.
In 2011, company employees loaded a passenger plane full of Kindle e-readers and made a test flight to identify problems, but were unable to detect any.
Members of the FAA advisory committee expressed mixed feelings about the risk of using equipment.
Douglas Kidd, of the passenger association National Association of Airline Passengers, said he was of the view that the disruption by the devices was genuine, even if the risk was minimal.
Other committee members said there were only isolated reports from pilots who speak for a disruption of aircraft systems by the devices, and most of them were very old.
However, the committee recommended the FAA to allow pilots to order the shutdown of the devices by the passengers during instrument landing under poor visibility.
A group from the travel industry welcomed the changes and described them as a sensible adjustment for travelers who would be extensively equipped with technology today.
“We are pleased that the FAA recognizes that a pleasant passenger experience isn’t at odds with safety,” said Roger Dow, CEO of the U.S. Travel Association.
Bird rescue rescued by North Sea island released again
A bird flowned to the coast by air rescue service, which had been founded exhausted on a North Sea drilling ristle, has been released back into nature.
The water ralle was flown by helicopter to Aberdeen last month, where it was puffed by the Scottish animal welfare organisation SPCA at its rescue centre in Alloa.
Colin Seddon, head of the centre, said: “This water rally was probably a wintry migratory bird from Northern Europe, stopped by the strong winds over the North Sea.”
Seemingly, the exhausted bird sought refuge on the rig.
He added: "He couldn't keep flying, which is why we were contacted asking for help."
Upon their release, the water ralle was fit and healthy again.
Is Europe's elite ready to do business with Britain?
Business for Britain was launched in April with the intention of bringing the economy to a table and defining what those in the U.K. would like to change our relations with the EU.
To this end, we have commissioned the largest and most comprehensive survey of British business leaders and asked them what they think about Britain, the economy and the EU.
YouGov surveyed over 1,000 business executives who account for roughly Britain's corporate sizes, sectors and regions.
The results of the survey will surprise many.
We have found that the vast majority of companies now export outside Europe and focus on countries that modernize and grow, while states in the EU are stagnating.
They would like new trade links with countries such as China, India and Brazil to be favoured by the government, rather thanking with the long and exhausting process of reforming the obscure EU institutions.
Asked about their view on certain legally regulated issues – from antitrust law to product legislation – the majority of business leaders are of the opinion that control over these key competences should be brought back according to Westminster.
There was general dissatisfaction about the single market, since, according to the companies, the costs of the Brussels rules would now outweigh the advantages of being part of Europe’s trading zone – even 40 percent of the big companies agreed, which are traditionally the most Europe-friendly.
Finally, and this presumably says the most, our survey of business leaders revealed that a clear majority of Britain want a course towards changing the deal and a relationship with the EU based on trade, not on politics.
This insight, which can be found among companies of all sizes and leading corporations, shows that the economy is pushing for a “meaningful change” that brings the booklet of action back to the UK.
It's about a lot - an amendment to the agreement and better conditions for Britain at 16 per cent would lead to a change of opinion in favour of remaining in the EU in a referendum.
The prime minister should have no doubts: this survey shows that the UK economy stands behind his plan for renegotiating Britain's conditions for EU membership.
It also shows that the companies expect a significant shift in power relations back to Britain from a renegotiation.
A better deal for the UK economy is possible and increasingly necessary after the eurozone is now moving towards closer economic and fiscal union.
The priority must be jobs and growth in the UK, as the result of our survey shows, which puts an increased focus on trade for businesses and a fundamental change in the Bruses approach to regulations.
Gazprom's Alexei Miller names pipeline in Bulgaria as the start of a new gas era
The start of construction of the South Stream pipeline in Bulgaria represents the launch of one of Europe's largest energy projects, the Gazprom board said.
“Today’s event is a milestone: construction work has begun on the Bulgarian section of the South Stream gas pipeline, the largest and most important project in Europe,” Gazprom’s chief executive, Alexei Miller, said in a statement on Thursday.
The project is a key element for the energy security of the entire European continent.
South Stream is intended to ensure more diversity in Russia's export routes through Europe.
A treaty dispute between Gazprom and its counterparts in Ukraine, in which the majority of Russia's gas is stored for Europe, creates an increased risk in conventional routes, as can be heard from the official side.
Miller said the direct connection to Bulgaria, a member of the European Union, meant that the geopolitical risks linked to transit countries were "forever" eliminated.
Bulgarian consumers will receive gas from South Stream at discounted tariffs when the entire project goes into operation in 2015.
According to Gazprom, the construction work is to begin in other subordinate countries on the planned route at the end of the year.
The pipeline is designed for an annual capacity of 62.3 billion cubic meters of natural gas.
For national and international investors, there is no way past the real estate location Germany.
The economic framework data is right, and the financing options are excellent.
This has been the tenor of the 9th Real Estate Day of the Börsen-Zeitung.
The German real estate markets benefit from the economic strength and the good economic conditions in the country in a European comparison.
This was pointed out by Christian Ulbrich, CEO Europe, Middle East and Africa of Jones Lang LaSalle, at the 9th real estate day of the Börsen-Zeitung.
According to Ulbrich, how sought-after German commercial real estate are.
In the first three quarters of this year, it increased by 31% compared to the prior-year segment.
In the UK, the increase was only 6%, in France it was 19%.
"Real estate investments offer an attractive interest rate," Ulbrich said.
The yield gap between real estate and federal bonds is at a historically high level.
The FAA eases restrictions on the use of electronic devices in airplanes – even if making phone calls remain prohibited.
Fighter planes are attacking a depot of Russian missiles in the port city of Latakia, it was officially announced.
