Hearing before Royal Commission: Two YMCA associates charged with sexual offences over accusations against Jonathan Lord
Two YMCA employees from New South Wales (NSW) had been charged with sexual offences in connection with children before allegations were made against child caretaker Jonathan Lord from Caringbah in 2011, according to the Royal Commission for Child Sexual Abuse.
However, at the opening statement before the Commission, the YMCA had stated that “it had never had to deal with an incident of child sexual abuse in the organization.”
Managing director Phillip Hare was questioned about a case in which a YMCA employee had been charged with child pornography offenses, and another in which a YMCA Caringbah Hall sports teacher had been convicted of the abuse of children entrusted to him.
Hare told Gail Furness, a commission's assistant lawyer, that he had known about the first case but did not know the second.
He acknowledged that the YMCA's opening statement to the Commission had also been inaccurate in claiming "there have been external checks of the YMCA in which the YMCA was recognised as at the forefront of child safety."
Evidence before the commission rather shows that the YMCA has been informed that it has received the second-lowest assessment of four possible in a quality audit by the Department of Education and Communities in August of the year.
Hare, who joined the YMCA at 21, acknowledged that the management had failed “from me downwards” in hiring Lord and failed to ensure that employees had been awared about their commitments to reporting violations of child safety guidelines.
Lord had been convicted earlier this year for sex offenses against twelve boys during his two-year work for the YMCA.
He was sentenced to at least six years in prison.
Hare denied, however, that the YMCA would have had a cultural problem that prevented employees from reporting Lord's violations regarding the safety of children.
The staff testified that they had observed violations, among other things that Lord was alone with children, looked after them privately as a babysitter, let them sit on his lap, said to one that he loved it, and let children play with his phone.
Danielle Ockwell, whose supervisor was Lord and who had asked for child protection training because she was concerned about his behavior, testified that the children's servicer Jacqui Barnat, who was the superior of Lords at YMCA Caringbah, was "very intimidating and very unapproachable most of the time."
The managing director explained that he would not accept statements from employees, according to which they had not dared to report to their superiors.
Instead, he said, their friendship with Lord has affected their judgment, which is why they did not report him.
Hare stated that he had communicated to the board of the YMCA NSW his view that the lesson that the organization had to draw from the “Jonathan Lord Incident” was “not the reporting” by employees, and the board had agreed with him.
Hare said the decision to urge employees to sign secrecy commitments shortly after the allegations appeared had been made by YMCA's managing director of YMCA's children's services, Liam Whitley.
He said this was intended to prevent contamination of the evidence, but this had been “overzealous” and poorly implemented.
The YMCA NSW had not been an organization safe for children between 2009 and 2011, when Jonathan Lord was employed there between 2009 and 2011, Professor Stephen Smallbone of Griffith University, explained to the commission.
He explained that there had been “serious problems” in hiring, reviewing, familiarization, training and supervising employees.
The hearing was adjourned until December 20.
Tony Blair declared he would seize the chance to return as a British prime minister - but acknowledged that a comeback was unlikely.
In an interview tonight on the occasion of the fifth anniversary of his departure from office, the 59-year-old proclaimed his views on various domestic issues.
Since his resignation in June 2007 after a decade as head of state, Blair has largely avoided commenting on British politics, limiting his comments mostly to foreign policy and his role as envoy to the Quartet of Peacemakers in the Middle East.
Asked if he'd like to return to the prime minister's office, Blair was quoted by the Evening Standard from London as saying: "Yeah, sure, but it's unlikely that this will happen, so ..."
When the horse show experts gathered in shares in Cardiff to choose the horse of the year, they were aware that fierce competition was imminent.
However, no one were prepared for three-year-old Fenton Kirkland.
The crunch, who isn't even in kindergarten and only took his first steps a few months ago, and his Shetland-Pony Toffee trotted loosely through the three rounds and clinched the first prize - leaving 30 adult opponents behind.
The inseparable couple, both of the same in size, have been praised for their demeanour, behaviour and style at the annual competition hosted by the Sunnybank Equestrian Centre in Rudry near Cardiff.
In his appearance in competition against men and women with elegant melons, he had set up his plate cap in a cheeky angle and led the two-year-old toffee through the ring.
Fenton was praised by the jurors for his natural abilities in dealing with the pony, which were well above his age.
And Toffee received top marks for his demeanour and personality.
Fenton had received Toffee last March as a gift for his third birthday and has been practicing with the Shetland pony every day since.
His mother, Donna, 30, said, “Fenton and Toffee are a great team.”
They had to compete against the entire field of participants and have walked off the pitch with gold cup and rosette.
It was only the second time he had competed with Toffee, and we were all thrilled when he won.
Complete strangers in the arena considered him to be so phenomenal that they wanted to be photographed.
The little boy from the village of Nantyglo near Ebbw Vale, South Wales, follows in the footsteps of his aunt Sharon Howells, who has been performing horses for over ten years.
Howells recounted, “The whole audience was electrified and everyone cheered him on and clapped.”
He ran on sand the entire length of the arena and even though he looked so tiny, he has done fantastic work.
Fenton is crazy about animals – he loves horses, tractors and farms and has two chickens to take care of.
The way he started, it won't be long before he's on the "horse of the year" show - and I'm sure he's going to do well.
A spokesperson for the annual horse show said: “Fenton is only three, but he knows how to deal with his pony.”
Together they're a great team.
The judges rated Fenton and Toffee afterwards how well they would have beaten each other and presented themselves in the demonstration ring.
They pay attention to good cooperation between pony and leader – and that’s where Fenton and Toffee were the best in the ring.
I'm sure Fenton's cute clothes did their part, which really fit the occasion perfectly.
Chinese newspaper to be 'reformed' after appeal
A Chinese newspaper that had demanded on the front page the release of a reporter who had been charged with defamation should be reformed, a press regulator said.
Guangzhou-based New Express had reprinted a rare public call for the release of journalist Chen Yongzhou.
Yet Chen later admitted on television that he had accepted bribes to fake reports of a part-state company.
Now, the New Express is undergoing “comprehensive rectification,” the regulator said.
The “rectification order” was issued by the authority for press and publications, radio, film and television in Guangdong.
Preliminary investigations revealed that the New Express, which belongs to Yangcheng Evening News Ground, between September 2012 and August 2013, released several inaccurate reports on the listed company Zoomlion.
“The editorial management of the New Express was disordered,” the regulator said in a statement.
She said she decided to "impose an administrative penalty for Chen Yongzhou and to withdraw his reporter's license."
Yangcheng Evening News Group has also been instructed to carry out a full rectification of the New Express and to immediately review the New Express’s management team.
Chen had written several articles for the New Express in which he reported on alleged financial irregularities at a manufacturer of construction equipment called Zoomlion.
After being arrested, the newspaper published two appeals for his release on the front page and stood behind his journalistic work.
But then Chen appeared on state television and admitted that he had published false reports against payment.
"In this case, I have harmed Zoomlion as well as the entire news industry and its ability to gain the trust of the public," he told CCTV.
I did it in the first place because I was looking for money and fame.
I realized that I acted incorrectly.
Following Chen's apology, the New Express issued an apology on the front page and said it had failed to review the reports correctly.
Various well-known suspects have made confessions on television in recent times.
Experts say confessions are still routinely enforced, despite a change in the law earlier this year, in which authorities were banned from forcing someone to accuse themselves.
Drug tunnel discovered with its own railroad between the US and Mexico
One of the most sophisticated tunnels for drug smuggling between the U.S. and Mexico, fully equipped with lighting, ventilation and electric rail operations, has now been discovered.
U.S. authorities described the 120 by 90 centimeter tunnel as one of the most sophisticated passages they would have ever found.
The zigzag-style tunnel over a length of just under six football fields connects warehouses near Tijuana, Mexico, and San Diego, USA.
In the area, there are countless inconspicuous warehouses, which makes it easier to hide drugs-loaded trucks.
The tunnel had been closed before drugs could be transported unrecognisted, authorities said.
During the discovery of the tunnel, eight and a half tons of marijuana and 148 kilograms of cocaine had been secured, according to the court's records.
Three men, who worked as drivers, according to authorities, have been charged with possessing possession and intended sale of marijuana and cocaine.
They face prison sentences of between ten years and for a life sentence in a guilty verdict.
In Nogales, Arizona, smugglers rely on an extensive underground network of discharge channels.
The tunnel is the eighth major passage that has been discovered in San Diego since 2006.
Some of the largest tunnels were discovered after the marijuana harvest in central Mexico in October, which presents the drug cartels with the challenge of how they can bring their product quickly to consumers.
In 2010, authorities found a nearly 640-meter-long passage with rails running from a kitchen in a residential building in Tijuana to two warehouses in San Diego.
Orlando Bloom and Miranda Kerr still love each other
Actor Orlando Bloom and model Miranda Kerr want to go their separate ways in the future.
However, in an interview Bloom said he and Kerr still love each other.
Miranda Kerr and Orlando Bloom are parents of two-year-old Flynn.
Actor Orlando Bloom has commented on the split from his wife, top model Miranda Kerr.
In an interview with US journalist Katie Couric, which was to be broadcast on Friday (local time), Bloom said "life sometimes doesn't go exactly as we plan or hope."
Kerr and himself still loved each other, the 36-year-old insisted.
“We will support each other and love each other as parents of Flynn.”
Kerr and Bloom have been married since 2010 and their son Flynn was born in 2011.
Veganism is, according to Lexika, subject to vegetarianism, but differs from it in a very considerable way.
Because while vegetarians allow themselves the consumption of cream cake, ice cream or a hefty edamer, these feasts aren’t on a vegan’s menu.
Milk, cheese, egg, in addition to the absence of meat and fish, the vegan abandons from all consumption of animal products - honey is also denied.
What remains, one may wonder, but on closer inspection the vegan food world is characterized by a considerable ingenuity.
"Bread spreads are very popular," explains Ute Henkelmann, the owner of the local reform house, which offers a variety of special products for vegans.
Reuben Proctor also likes to visit the shop in Domgasse.
For 13 years, the native New Zealander has been vegan, in Lampertheim he finds (almost) everything he needs to live. "In 1997 I changed my diet and became a vegetarian".
At the turn of the century, the next step took place and I became vegan.
"However, the conviction came not only because of the diet, but because of the choice of shoes," says Proctor, who prefers imitation leather to normal leather.
He realized that it is also possible in other everyday matters without animal products.
First and foremost, being vegan is an ethic.
"I don't want animals to have to die for me," Proctor expresses Vegan's maxim, which extends to overall consumer behavior.
Instead of mourning burgers, scrambled eggs or gummy bears, the vegan quickly discovered completely new products, the enthusiasm increased.
"It's a double bonus," Proctor says today, "actually being vegan doesn't represent renunciation, it's an asceticism, it's an enrichment." The assumption that with the diet change you're reducing the choice of dining options, Proctor rather attributes to the power of habit.
And it is precisely these conditionings that caused some well-intentioned attempts to fail.
"The addiction factor is a very decisive factor that allows many to give up quickly, similar to smoking," Proctor makes a comparison.
And object: "Certain tastes cannot be attached to the animal".
Often it is the spices or the type of preparation that make up the taste.
"However, it is also possible to create the same effect in another way," the vegan explains.
Proctor brings, for example, eggplants, mushrooms and a few vegan sausages to a barbecue.
He pays attention to a balanced, varied diet, in which it should taste hearty.
Of course, you want to have something between your teeth, that’s legitimate.
"It's supposed to taste, but that's not just reduced to animal food," Proctor said.
He did not suffer any health damage to eating disorders, a further widespread assumption.
For me, the transition had a positive aspect.
I don’t absorb foreign cholesterol and have an increased vitamin content.
There are high-performance athletes who eat exclusively vegan.
"My blood levels are great, I often let myself be checked," Proctor reveals.
In particular, many vegansimic critics warn of the lack of vitamin B12.
"Since animals are nourished with feed instead of grazing in the pasture, it is questionable whether the vitamin B12 content is high in the meat you buy in the refrigerated chest," Proctor finds.
Vegans also have the opportunity to consume the coenzyme in the form of tablets.
"My B12 values are fine," he explains.
Proctor does not have to take long distances for food procurement.
After all, staples are everywhere and every supermarket now has soy milk and other products.
Everything I need, I'll find on site.
Only every now and then I buy something special.
"Many people overlook the fact that only the finished products are expensive, but that's the case with "ordinary" finished products.
In any case, the preparation of vegan dishes is far cheaper and less time-consuming than generally assumed.
"There's a lot to discover in the home kitchen, with a few small tricks you can achieve good, if not partly better results," says Proctor.
Margarine instead of butter, slightly more baking soda and carbonic acid instead of egg, with its vegan cheesecakes Proctor could also amaze his in-laws.
Family celebrations give the vegan a new spice.
This has momentum, if you cook together, it has a new importance.
"The situations that can arise in the process are as different as the people themselves," Proctor says.
Of course, however, he has also been faced with skepticism.
You don't like my food anymore?
"Should I feel bad now, because I drink milk?" - with such questions you have to act as a vegan with sensitivity.
"I don't reject people, I'm part of this society," Proctor doesn't want to make an enclave existence.
And more precisely, the everyday world doesn't seem as vegan as suspected - and the vegan doesn't seem as restricted in everyday life as commonly assumed.
Fast food is also allowed.
Fries are everywhere!
"Many kebab shops offer falafel, or a vegetarian yufka," says Proctor.
In a pizzeria, he dodges pasta dishes, or just orders a pizza without cheese, all a matter of habit.
The restaurants are slowly adjusting to it.
"When I started eating vegan 13 years ago, everything was much more difficult," the New Zealander can find a development: "You have to talk to the people, make them aware of it".
"If enough requests come, they may be inclined to complement the menu," he hopes.
Vegan restaurants are developing, such as the galley in Mannheim or the Café Vogelfrei.
Proctor has the wish that other restaurants have at least a vegan dish on the menu.
Basically, vegan dishes are there for everyone.
Anyone can eat them, so they're just the opposite of exclusive.
"Meat dishes, on the other hand, are exclusive, and in the truest sense of the word, because they exclude others," Proctor notes.
"If you want to avoid food scandals, veganism is a safe way to make most of it impossible from the outset," says Proctor.
Purchasing vegan products, however, can be a bit difficult at the beginning.
In particular, if one learns to understand that animal additives in peanuts can also be contained as a dyer, one seems to quickly leave courage with the observance of ethics with so many hurdles and traps.
Reuben Proctor and Lars Thomsen have written a book not only for this reason: "Veganissimo - animal ingredients and their alternatives" explains the many ingredients that are buried with food and cleaning products.
"Gradually, you develop a scanner view and know which signs and logos you have to pay attention to "It's sometimes better to take small steps than to stop or stumble," Proctor knows.
Veganism, it is an important moral finger pointing at consciousness.
Perhaps he doesn’t quite manage to put off his habits, but at least he can teach consumers to critically scrutinize their habits.
Exchange operator Nasdaq OMX continues to keep its customers in breath.
Trading at Nasdaq Options Market was disrupted on Friday afternoon German time.
In a communication, the operator blamed technical problems.
The other eleven U.S. option markets, including two of the Nasdaq OMX, continued their business unhindered.
The latest incident continues a whole series of minor and major breakdowns at the exchange operator.
As recently as Tuesday, Nasdaq's indices had not been calculated for an hour because of errors in data transport.
There were two breakdowns in one week in August of this year alone.
Initially, U.S. investment bank Goldman Sachs had sent en mass faulty buying orders to the options markets because of technical problems.
As a result, the exchange operators needed almost a whole day to look through the orders and canceled.
Two days later, half of all stock trading in the U.S. came to a standstill for several hours because of a computer breakdown on the Nasdaq exchange.
Last year, Facebook’s IPO also caused a stir.
Nasdaq systems hadn't coped with the flood of buy and sell orders, the SEC's stock market regulator later noted, donating the company to a record $10 million fine.
Constance: Cyclist drives 63-year-old
According to the police, the accident occurred when a 26-year-old man traveled illegally on Thursday evening, around 22 o'clock, with a women's bike illegally on the left pavement from the station square in the direction of the marketplace.
When a 63-year-old man suddenly stepped onto the sidewalk from a restaurant, the cyclist could no longer brake in time, according to police.
In the ensuing collision, the pedestrian was pressed against the house wall, went to the ground in the further course and contracted an approximately 15-centimeter-long laceration to the head.
An ambulance team took the injured man to the hospital for medical treatment.
British police send Assange an extradition notice
British police have today handed over an extradition notice to WikiLeaks founder Julian Assange, who has sought refuge at the Ecuadorian embassy in London and applied for asylum.
Scotland Yard stated that the 40-year-old Australian had been delivered a “call to stand up” to ask him to appear at a police station, adding that if he did not comply, he was threatened with arrest.
Assange threatens extradition to Sweden over the indictment in a sexual offence has exhausted his options under British law since the Supreme Court rejected his opposition to extradition earlier this month.
Fearing Stockholm would extradite him to the United States, he had sought refuge at the Ecuadorian embassy in London on June 19 and asked the South American country for political asylum.
Scotland Yard "has sent a 40-year-old man a request to stand at a police station at a time chosen by us," a spokesman said.
He continues to violate the terms of his release on bail.
The embassy made no comment on the delivery of the police request.
Assange feares being extradited from Sweden to the United States and charged with possible espionage accusations after posting over 25,000 diplomatic messages on WikiLeak's disclosure website.
Rider who crashed between his legs at 210 km/h and hot drink gets £1,000 fine
A motorist was fined £1,000 for driving between his legs at up to 210 km/h and a hot drink.
Andrew Howie, 35, from Tiptree, Essex, was halted on May 27 with his Mercedes Benz on the A120 at Braintree.
After the police stopped him, they spotted the drinks cup between his legs.
Howie pleaded guilty to Colchester Local Court, without the necessary care and attention.
He received seven points and a six-month driving ban.
Howie has also been convicted of paying £90 and a £100 surcharge.
Tax for foreign property owners against London's speculative bubble
The Ministry of Finances has provisionally calculated the CGT measure, but is still awaiting the final decision of Chancellor George Osborne, who had imposed a 7% stamp duty in the 2012 budget on apartments that cost over two million pounds, as well as annual fees for buyers who hold apartments as a company rather than individuals.
Already, revenues from stamp duty for home ownership in the Westminster and Kensington & Chelsea districts, which stood at £708 million in the 2012/13 tax year, exceed revenues for Northern Ireland, Wales, Scotland, the north-east, northwest, Yorkshire and Humber region combined.
Cook said: “Following the increases in stample duty on high-priced apartments and the introduction of the associated legislation against circumventivation, it’s hard to claim that high-value properties are taxed too low, notwithstanding the impact of the outdated municipal tax system.”
"Yet this move could make some foreign investors more restrained when it comes to buying real estate in London, or stopping owners from selling," he added.
Top-notch real estate – the top five to ten per cent of the housing market by price – in the wealthy south-west beld that stretches from Fulham to Wimbledon, has priced up 11.8% last year.
Prices in Central London showed steady annual growth of 5.6%, but were overshadowed by an emerging “domestic market” where the southwestern part of the city, the north (7.4%) and the east (6.5%) experienced an upward trend, according to a study by Savills.
Scientists were able to shed more light on the context of a dog's tacification with its mood.
Previous research had shown that happy dogs wagged their tail more to the right (from the dog's point of view), while in nervous dogs it was the left side.
Yet now scientists say that other dogs can perceive these subtle differences and react to them.
Professor Georgio Vallortigara, a neuroscientist at the University of Trento, said: “In humans, it is well known that the left and right hemispheres react differently to stimuli that cause positive or negative emotions.”
Here we have trieded to look at other species.
He also noted that in dogs, just like in humans, the right hemisphere of the brain is responsible for the movement of the left side and vice versa, and that the two hemispheres played different roles in feelings.
To learn more about how dogs react to their conspecific's sidal tacification, the researchers monitored the animals as these films looked at other dogs.
They measured the heart rate of the four-legged friends and analyzed the behavior.
Probably we will soon understand why the tail sometimes moves into one direction, sometimes in the other
Professor Vallortigara said, “We’ve shown the dogs movies with dogs – either a lifelike version or a silhouette to eliminate distracting other aspects, and we were thus able to steer the movement of the tail more to the left or right.”
When the animals saw an otherwise expressionless dog moving the tail to the right (seen from the perspective of the wagging dog), they remained completely relaxed.
But if the tail inclination was mainly to the left (again from the perspective of the dog wagging), their heart rate increased and they looked restless.
According to Professor Vallortigara, the dogs do not intentionally communicate with each other through these movements.
Rather, he is convinced that the dogs would have learned from experience what movements they should worry about and when not.
He said: “If you have different encounters with other dogs and waiting with the tail in one direction is rather linked to friendly behavior and the right side rather produces less friendly behavior, then you react on the basis of these experiences.”
The researchers say the findings could help dog owners, veterinarians and trainers better understand their animal's emotions.
John Bradshaw, an expert on dog behavior and visiting fellow at the Faculty of Veterinology at the University of Bristol, explained that this was not the first study to investigate whether left and right was important for dogs.
Last year, a team from the University of Lincoln found that dogs turn their heads to the left when they look at an aggressive dog, and to the right, if it's a satisfied dog.
In another research paper at the University of Victoria in Canada, “Dogs are more likely to approach a robot dog when its ‘tail’ wags on the left, rather than becoming restless – just the other way around.”
According to his statement, the differences could be due to the fact that the dogs in the various studies did not interpret the animals in the films or the robot dogs as dogs.
A study of how dogs respond to real dogs could help, he explained.
“While there is considerable evidence for many different mammals that the two hemispheres of the brain are used for different purposes, many of the details have yet to be determined—and dogs are no exception,” Bradshaw said.
However, however, their behavior can simply be recorded, it will probably not take long for us to understand why the tail sometimes moves in one direction, sometimes in the other.
Arctic Monkeys postpone appearance in Glasgow due to Alex Turner's illness
Rock band Arctic Monkeys has postponed an appearance in Glasgow after its lead singer contracted laryngeal inflammation.
The band from Sheffield was scheduled to play at the Hydro on Friday.
However, due to the illness of the lead singer Alex Turner, the concert must now be postponed.
The band's announcement came after already having to postpone an appearance at the LG Arena in Birmingham on Thursday.
In a statement on their official website, the Arctic Monkeys said: “Following the decision to cancel the show at the LG Arena in Birmingham tonight as well as on medical advice, the Arctic Monkeys will also have to cancel the show at the Hydro in Glasgow on Friday, November 1.”
“Alex Turner has been diagnosed with laryngeal inflammation, which is why he can’t occur.”
The show at the LG Arena in Birmingham will now take place on November 20, which has been relocated at the Hydro in Glasgow to November 21.
All tickets remain valid for these shows.
We apologise to all ticket holders for any inconveniencence caused thereby.
Please contact customer service at the outlets where you have purchased your ticket if you need further assistance.
Pope Francis Appoints First Cardinals in February
Pope Francis will be appointed new cardinals of the Catholic Church for the first time on February 22, the Vatican announced on Thursday.
Cardinals are the highest-ranking clerics in the Catholic Church after the pope, and it is also they who elect a pope, so Francis will thereby appoint the first group of men who will ultimately participate in the election of his successor.
There are currently 201 cardinals.
However, a cardinal who has reached an age of 80 can no longer participate in the papal election – this falls to a group of 120 “eligiblinated cardinals”.
Speaking in a statementing the news, Father Federico Lombardi, a spokesman for the Vatican, said there would be a meeting of all cardinals before the ceremony to appoint the new cardinals, which was also known as the Consistory, was held.
“Pope Francis has decided to announce his decision to convene the Consistorium in February in advance so that other meetings that require the participation of cardinals from all over the world can be planned,” Lombardi said.
Jack Valero of Catholic Voices said the number of eligible cardinals was likely to have dropped by February.
He explained that a pope usually appoints as many cardinals as necessary, so that the number of cardinals entitled to vote would be 120 again, and as many cardinals over 80 as he wishes.
The Consistory next year is significant because it is the first since the election of Francis in March this year, according to Valero.
There is currently a certain overhang on the part of Europe and in particular Italy.
"It will be interesting to see if the new pope will appoint cardinals from the rest of the world to restore balance," he said.
Forty percent of Catholics live in South America, but they only have a tiny number of cardinals.
The cardinals will also be the first to be invoked since the formation of the Council of Cardinals founded by Francis, a group of eight cardinals from around the world tasked with finding opportunities to reform the Church.
In the past, the pope decided everything alone.
“Now Francis has chosen these eight cardinals to help him,” Valero said.
He declared that it was "well possible" that Francis asked the cardinals for advice.
But so far we haven’t been in this situation – it’s all new.
Valero said that typically the bishops from great places would be called to cardinals, but Francis was “full of surprises – so we don’t know who he is appointing.”
GM recalls new pickup trucks in U.S. to repair seat reside
General Motors is recalling nearly 19,000 of its brand-nead Chevrolet Silverado and GMC Sierra pick-up trucks built in 2014 to fix a problem with the manually adjustable seat reside, according to the U.S. regulatory authority for vehicle safety on Friday.
For some of the cars, there may be a defect in the mechanism for resetting the front seat residence.
This is why they don't meet the federal guidelines for vehicle safety on headrests.
“If the vehicle is approached from behind, it is possible that the headrest does not protect the occupants properly, so that there is an increased risk of injury,” the message on the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration (NHTSA) website read.
The recalled models were manufactured between August 1 and September 10.
The delivery of the GM trucks began in June and represents the leading U.S. automaker's most important model launch since the bankruptcy-related restructuring in 2009.
GM informed the vehicle owners about the defect in the first half of October.
NHTSA was unable to review the notification letter because of the 16-day government shutdown, which had a negative impact on car sales in October.
Silverado and Sierra trucks, newly developed for the 2014 model year, were up 20 percent in the first ten months of the year, GM said on Friday.
In October, GM sold 42,660 Silverado and 16,503 Sierra pickup trucks.
GM shares rose 1.4 percent to $37.47 on the New York Stock Exchange Friday afternoon.
Desperation cry of an Obama voter
I've elected President Obama twice in the hope that change is possible
He says Obama has made commendable efforts that have been thwarted by Republicans blockade tactics
The blockade policy doesn't apologize Obamacare's website problems, the drone strikes
Obama's memoirs about the 2008 election campaign are a sad memory of what would have been possible
Nathaniel P. Morris is a second-year student at Harvard Medical School.
I'm currently reading a terribly sad book.
It's a book that I thought it'd cheer me uprise during the gloomy second year of my medical studies and give me hope again.
It's called "The Audacity to Win" and chronicles the memoirs of Barack Obama on the 2008 presidential election.
When I'm done with my patient records in the evening and going to bed, the book takes me back to a time when politics inspired millions and could take their breath away from a speech.
The election became a landslide victory and newspokers paused to demonstrate the historical significance of that hour.
My classmates were crying with joy and my parents collected all the newspapers they could find.
A young team of visionaries were on their way to the White House and the nation was ready for change.
When Obama moved into office at the time in 2008, he had 82% approval ratings.
And then I close the book.
Switchinging back to the present is a rude awakening, as if you're being ripped out of a dream.
It's hard to remember these optimistic times - they seem to be a vague memory, a sad reminder of missed opportunities.
In the years since I cast my vote for the first time, something has actually changed.
But it's just nothing I could have imagined.
I acknowledge the great and multifaceted things Obama has achieved, from the adoption of the Affordable Care Act for health insurance to the military withdrawal from Iraq, the end of the forced concealment of homosexuality in the army and the killing of Osama bin Laden.
I, too, am of the view that that partisan blockade tactics have overturned too many aspirations that our nation should move forward: immigration reform, a public option for health insurance, and the shutdown of Guantanamo Bay, to name a few.
But having defended the Obama administration countless times against colleagues and acquaintances, I have come to an end with my attempts to explain.
I've reached a point of political desperation.
The republican blockade policy cannot explain why the interception of foreign heads of state is allowed or the killing of innocent children by drones overseas.
It cannot explain why the National Security Agency collects data on the private lives of Americans and why whistleblowers are being punished who expose government misconduct.
It cannot explain why Anwar al-Awlaki, an American citizen, was assassinateed without a trial, nor why public financing and issuance limits are circumvented during the presidential election.
It doesn't justify the findings of a report stating the White House's efforts to immobilize the media are the "most aggressive ... since the Nixon administration."
And most recentially, it can't apologise the inability to design a simple website in the more than three years since the Affordable Care Act was passed.
I don't know if that's what I should've expected.
Whether I should have understood at 18 that government work could contradict its previous political campaigns.
Clearly, deputies office isn't a foreseeable course, as an opposition party and accidental events such as the Newtown Massacre shape public discussion.
However, when I look at the examples mentioned above, they seem to be largely elected by the government itself.
And it bothers me the most.
I re-elected Obama again in 2012, not because I was thrilled about his candidacy.
Mitt Romney represented a confusing and unsophisticated alternative, he didn't seem to be able to establish his direction and positions.
I had the impression that a second term for Obama, free from the pressure of future elections, would fulfill the hope we'd heard about for so long.
However, with the decline in approval ratings for Obama to below 45% this week, the return to 2008 through this book has become all the harder.
It arouses in me the longing for the many promises that have dissolved into air.
This week I was reading a section in the book detailing how Obama had to take a heavy loss against Clinton in the Pennsylvania primaries.
In a meeting with election workers following the decision, he told his associates they needed to get back on track and follow the purpose of their cause.
"I want us to find our magic again," he said.
We need to remember who we are.
It's five years later, Mr. President, and I couldn't agree with you.
The views expressed in this commentary are solely those of Nathaniel Morris.
Clive Palmer claims Prime Minister Tony Abbott has conflict of interest on parental leave
Billionaire and MP Clive Palmer says Prime Minister Tony Abbott is in a conflict of interest in his plans for parental leave because his daughters become pregnant and could therefore benefit.
The mining magnate, who is in a legal battle over paying $6 million in carbon tax, made the claim when he wanted to swipe questions off the table about whether it was in a conflict.
The Palmer United Party could control up to four votes in the Senate, which are important for deciding whether to cut or cut carbon and mining taxes.
Yet Palmer claimed that only ministers could have conflicts of interest and Abbott's daughters would personally benefit from the laws.
“He finds himself in a huge conflict of interest when it comes to paid parental leave, because if one of his daughters becomes pregnant, he has a direct interest in whether she receives leave or not,” Palmer said.
Two months after the election, Electoral Commission officially declared Palmer's winner of Fairfax's Sunshine Coast seat with a 53-vote lead after the recount.
Palmer called for a revision of the vote counting process so that it would take place more quickly.
Tony Abbott's daughters Frances and Bridget.
Should these elections be decided two months after the end of voting?
“We need a better system,” he said.
Why shouldn't we have a system where you're marching in, entering the data into a computer, dialing immediately, and then having the result at 6:30 in the evening?
Palmer also criticized the use of pencils when marking the ballots.
Do you use them, because you can erase the results if you don’t like them?
Voting with pencils these days is unbelievable.
The Electoral Commission has considered the options for an electronic election and recently published a joint legislative proposal with New Zealand.
Palmer, 59, stated that his policy includes an international airport for the Sunshine Coast and that he would take his new office "very seriously."
A public office is about the service to the public.
“We strive for no reward except that of history, namely that we can serve at a critical time of this community,” he said.
RBS suspends two foreign exchange traders
The Royal Bank of Scotland has suspended two traders from its foreign exchange division, according to two people familiar with the situation; this is another indication that the global regulatory investigation into the alleged manipulation of the currency market is picking up rapidly.
Some of the world's largest banks, including UBS, Barclays, Deutsche Bank and RBS, confirmed they are working with the authorities on investigations in the world's largest financial market, where €5.3 quadrillion changes hands every day.
The two traders would be the first RBS employees to be suspended in the widening investigating scandal surrounding the Libor interbank business.
The bank, which made no comment on the suspensions, confirmed this month that it had received requests for information from authorities.
“Our investigation into this issue continues and we are fully cooperating with the FCA and other regulators,” the bank said.
Last month, people close to the situation indicated that RBS had forwarded the records of emails and instant messages sent by and to a previous trader to Britain's regulator, the Financial Conduct Authority.
This trader, Richard Usher, had left the RBS in 2010 and is said to have been on leave from his current position as European head of forex spot trading at JPMorgan.
Rouhan Ramchandani, head of European spot trading at Citi, was on leave this week and Matt Gardiner, a former senior currency trader at Barclays and UBS, was suspended at Standard Chartered this week.
None of these traders have been accused of any wrongdoing.
Usher's instant message group included bankers at Barclays and Citigroup, people close to the situation said.
UBS announced this week that it had taken steps against some of its employees after Switzerland's regulator Finma said it was investigating alleged manipulation of the foreign exchange market at a number of Swiss banks.
At least six authorities worldwide – the European Commission, Finma, the Swiss competition authority Weko, the FCA, the U.S. Department of Justice and the Hong Kong Monetary Authority – are currently dealing with accusations that bankers have agreed on transactions on the currency market.
HSBC, Citigroup, JPMorgan and Credit Suisse have also launched internal investigations or received requests for information from authorities, people familiar with the situation said.
Banks are combing instant messages and emails from several years looking for cases of misconduct.
The news about these investigations has thrown traders into turmoil in an area that has belonged to one of one of the most profitables of trading units of investment banks in recent years but has been under pressure this year because low volatility in currencies restricts the chances for speculators.
Some bankers have attempted to downplay the affair, saying that it is almost impossible to manipulate the vast and highly liquid foreign exchange market, but leading traders believe this is not necessarily true.
One leading trader noted that despite the enormous daily volume in currency trading, the fragmentation of liquidity between the various trading platforms and the increasing use of their own internal platforms by banks means that “even with very low ticket prices you can influence the market”.
The news came out on the same day Credit Suisse announced it had terminated a trader of its London Exchange Traded Funds office this week after causing a loss of just under six million dollars at the end of last year.
The bank promptly informed the relevant authorities and cooperated with them.
“We are certain that the trader acted alone and that the matter is contained,” Credit Suisse said.
Painted the construction fence on Bechtle property
A group of the citizens' initiative "Swiss meadow" has beautified the construction fence on the Bechtle property on the spa promenade in Bad Herrenalb.
In addition, she cleaned and widened the footpath, according to a press release.
