Supreme Court uphold Obama's health care law
It's a big victory for the Obama administration: The U.S. Supreme Court decided today that the health care law closely linked to Barack Obama is constitutional.
By five to four votes, the judges ruled the Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act -- it forces U.S. citizens to take out health insurance or pay a penalty by 201 - is constitutional under the state's tax authority.
Chief Justice John Roberts sided with the four more liberal members of the chamber, while judges Scalia, Thomas, Alito and Kennedy ruled against it.
The court also upheld the remaining sections of the 2700-page law and also took the view that the health law stipulated that states would either have to extend the admission requirements for Medicaid or would otherwise all state Medicaid funding be removed was not unconstitutionally restrictive.
The lawsuit against the law had been brought by 26 states and the National Federation of Independent Business.
The law had been decidedly rejected by all leading Republican presidential nominees, including eventual challenger Mitt Romney, in the 2012 pre-election campaign.
Norway: Norwegian place makes light with giant mirrors
With huge mirrors, the inhabitants of a Norwegian village have brought light to their gloomy valley.
Due to the low level of sun incidence, Rjukan, located in the Vestfjord Valley, usually sinks from autumn to spring in the shadow of the surrounding mountains.
With three gigantic reflectors at a height of 45 meters, a dream of the century came true on Wednesday.
"Finally!" Mayor Steinar Bergsland enthused at the launch ceremony on TV2.
Some dwellers of the valley arranged sun chairs, others put on sunglasses for safety.
Until now, sun-seekers have had to take a cable car to a nearby summit in winter.
Ten years agove, the local artist Martin Andersen had presented the proposal to steer the beams into the valley with mirrors.
However, this basic idea has existed since 1913.
After several years of debate, the city council had finally adopted the 5 million kroner (around 615 000 euros) project.
For several years now, a similar construction has given the Italian Alpine resort Viganella wintry sunshine.
