Nursing day at the small Engländer hut
For the now fifth nursing day in the succession of the Natura 2000 project Upper Hotzenwald, a group of committed participants met at the small Engländerhütte in Ibach.
Due to suggestions from the population, the view into the valley in the area of the hut should be cut free on this day, said mayor Helmut Kaiser.
Many hikers described this area as the highlight of the canyon trail, on which the landscape offers the ideal setting for magnificent views.
He thanked those present for their readiness to cooperate and emphasized that nursing day was a suitable tool to strengthen the connection between people and nature.
It offers every citizen the opportunity to set accents in nature itself.
At the same time as the tourist aspect, the ecological consideration can also be taken into account.
Friederike Tribukait from the Department of Nature Conservation of the Regional Presidium Freiburg explained this ecological aspect following the welcome by District Administrator Tilman Bollacher, who had taken over the patronage and wished the action a good success.
According to Tribukait, the tourist destination of the far-sighted view coincides with the aim of preserving the now heavily overgrown ollmend weid field with its specific protected habitat types by opening and connecting to the still existing pastures.
Certain species, such as rare butterflies and grasshoppers, cannot overcome barriers in the form of forest areas.
They need these free connections for species conservation.
Tribukait said she did not want to misk breaking a lance for the planned biosphere area at this point.
In this planning, the current measure does not only integrate seamlessly.
A biosphere area also offers the opportunity for sustainable economic and ecological development on the basis of the internationally recognized Unesco status and thus a continuous addition to what has been achieved for the future with the Life project as well as the previous nursing days.
According to these various explanations, district manager Christoph Wehle and Life project manager Cornelia Bischoff divided the helpers into groups.
The larger trees had already been knocked in advance, the forestry tractor would be ready if necessary.
A group of helpers should take care of the exemption of juniper bushes and the maintenance of a drying stone bar.
The goal was the clear view at the viewpoint.
