Google, Samsung, Huawei sued over Nortel patents
The group, which owns thousands of former Nortel patents, on Thursday sought an avalanche of patent infringement lawsuits against cellphone makers, including Google, the company that was outbid by him at Nortel's bankruptcy auction.
Rockstar, the consortium that bought the Nortel patents for $4.5 billion, sued Samsung Electronics Co Ltd, HTC Corp, Huawei and four other companies for patent infringements in the U.S. district court in Texas.
Rockstar is jointly owned by Apple, Microsoft, BlackBerry, Ericsson and Sony.
Google is accused of patent infringement in seven cases.
The patents concern technology that reconcile search terms on the Internet with relevant advertising, according to the lawsuit that hits the core of Google's search engine business.
Representatives of Samsung, Huawei, HTC and Rockstar were not immediately reachable.
Samsung, Huawei and HTC manufacture phones that work with Google's Android operating system, which is in sharp competition with the mobile products of Apple and Microsoft.
In 2011, Google made an initial bid over $900 million for the Nortel patents.
Google boosted its supply several times and most recently offered $4.4 billion.
After outperforming Rockstar's Nortel patents, Google acquired Motorola Mobility for $12.5 billion, a business that was also partly motivated by Motorola's library of patents.
“Although Google has failed with its attempt to acquire the lawsuit patents at the auction, Google has infringed the patents and continues to do so,” he said.
Rockstar is demanding hefty damages from Google as it claims Google's patent infringement is intentional, according to the allegation.
Egypt sworn in first freely elected president
Mohammed Morsi takes the oath of office, but his day of triumph should not mean the end of political disputes in Egypt.
Islamist Mohammed Morsi promised a "new Egypt" when he was sworn in as the country's first freely elected president, succeeding Husni Mubarak, who had been expelled from office 16 months earlier.
With his inauguration before the Supreme Constitutional Court, Morsi also became the first freely elected Islamist president in the Arab world and Egypt's fifth head of state since overthrowing the monarchy about 60 years ago.
He took the oath in front of the judges of judgment, dressed in black robes, which is based on the Nile and is modeled on an ancient Egyptian temple.
"We are aiming for a better future, a new Egypt and a second republic," Morsi said at the solemn ceremony, which was broadcast live on state television.
“Today, the Egyptian people have laid the foundation for a new life – absolute freedom, a real democracy and stability,” said 60-year-old Morsi, a U.S. educated engineer and member of the Muslim Brotherhood, a fundamentalist group that, since its inception 84 years agove, had mostly been sharply targeted as a banned organization by the respective government.
Hundreds of soldiers and police guarded the building when Morsi arrived in a small car convoy shortly after eleven a.m. local time.
Only a few hundred supporters had gathered in front of the court to cheer for the new president, and, contrary to the presidential pomp from the Mubarak period, traffic was stopped only briefly so that the car convoy could cross the usually busy road that connects the city centre with the southern suburbs.
Mocked as an uncharismatic “replacement wheel” of the Brotherhood, his personal prestige has increased since his victory and a Friday speech, in which he presented himself not only as a candidate of the Islamists, but all those who wanted to bring the aspirations of the 2011 uprising against authoritarian Mubarak.
“Egypt today is a civilian, national, constitutional and modern state,” Morsi, dressed in a blue suit with a red tie, explained to the judges in the wood-panelled chamber where he took the oath of office.
Morsi later drove to the University of Cairo, where he was supposed to give his inaugural address.
He was officially greeted by an army chapel that played the national anthem he listened to.
The military leader Field Marshal Hussein Tantawi was present.
Upon his arrival he was greeted by hundreds of attendees in the university's largest lecture hall with chants of "The army and the people are one hand."
Established in 1908 as a bastion of secular education, the University of Cairo developed into a stronghold of Islamist student groups in the 1970s.
Morsi took a symbolic oath in Tahir Square on Friday, the birthplace of the uprising that led last year to Mubarak's authoritarian rule, and he vowed to reclaim presidential powers that the military council had taken over from office-haunted head of state.
However, by taking the official oath before the court rather than as usual before parliament, he bows to the will of the military, pointing out that the power struggle will continue.
Morsi's speech in Tahir Square was full of dramatic populist gestures.
