Everyone involved will be able to live with that.
You thought travel agencies were a thing of the past thanks to the internet?
Flight Centre appears to be resisting the trend.
The company has increased its full-year profit prospects and is expecting record revenue from holidaymakers in Australia and the UK.
The travel company now expects an adjusted pre-tax profit of between $325 and $340 million, compared to $305 to $315 million in its previous forecast.
If the current targets are reached, that means a growth of 12 to 17 percent compared to the record $290.4 million in 2011/12.
As managing director Graham Turner announced, Flight Centre posted eight per cent profit in the first half of the year and posted a strong start to the second half of the year, particularly in Australia's and UK's private business.
“Since the beginning of the year, our ten countries have been profitable and some of them are on track towards record-breaking earnings before interest and taxes,” he explained.
This includes Australia and the UK, which are typically one of our strongest earners.
In Australia, the leisure industry has recovered in the second half of the year, making up for a slightly weaker market for business travel.
Things were doing similarly well for Flight Centre's leisure business in the UK, while business customers spent less here, too.
U.S. business recovered compared to the seasonally weaker first half of the year, so a full-year profit is expected for the third year in a row.
Flight Centre's shares rose 3 cents to $38.20 yesterday.
Schools are encouraged to focus on mathematics, spelling and grammar
In courses on English literature, pupils will in future have to deal with at least one play by Shakespeare, a 19th-century novel, romantic lyricism and contemporary British novels from 1914.
“Unseen texts” are also included in the exam in order to encourage broader reading.
The combined course from English literature and language is abolished.
Beginning in 2015, pupils will have to take an independent GCSE exam for language, with strong incentivities to choose English literature as a separate qualification.
The Ministry of Education will publish the new curricula for English and mathematics tomorrow – the first subjects that have been radically reshaped.
Other core subjects will be changed next year.
In a separate initiative, Ofqual, the regulator of examinations, will announce a reorganization of the GCSE structures, including a new grading system and less coursework.
In a speech in the summer, Education Minister Michael Gove said that there is "broad consensus that we need to reform our audit system to restore public trust," stressing the GCSEs would become "challenging, more ambitious and stricter."
Studies show that in English schools there is less time spent on math – 116 hours a year or three hours a week per school year – than in most other countries.
On the other hand, Australian schools teach an average of 143 hours a year and students in Singapore receive 138 hours.
Although there will be no formal requirement to provide more time for mathematics in the timetable, it is said that the comprehensive math exam – combined with a greater weighting of the subject in leaderboards – will presumably encourage schools to start more hours.
The curriculum pays more attention to “problems from real life”, including financial mathematics.
