Salem: Johanna Rahner at the Ecumenical Discussion Forum
The next Ecumenical Discussion Forum on Monday, October 21, in the New Museum of the Castle will focus on the topic "To hell with hell - Theological reflections on today's dealings with the last things".
The speaker is Professor Johanna Rahner.
The Ecumenical Discussion Forum is an initiative of the Catholic and evangelical parishes of Salem, Salem Palace and the Bodenseekreis Cultural Office.
Johanna Rahner wants to investigate in the forum the question of how today people classify the "last things" after death.
Had people known too much about it in the past centuries and relied on those images of heaven and hell "that still shape our ideas to this day," the contemporary prophetic doctrine of the hopes of perfection is rather characterized by a certain embarrassment or even speechlessness.
"The greater hope has been duly driven out by the criticism of religion," says the speaker.
“It provided us with so many question marks that the exclamation marks seemed to have been lost.”
Johanna Rahner goes on to say: "But the decisive challenge remains the annoyance of death".
Associated with this is the question of that longing "which dreams that in the end everything may be good".
Johanna Rahner, born in 1962 in Baden-Baden, studied Catholic theology and biology at the Albert Ludwigs-University Freiburg from 1982 to 1989.
She received her doctorate in 1997 for Dr. theol., also in Freiburg.
Habilitation for Fundamental Theology and Ecumenical Theology followed in 2003 at the Westfälische Wilhelms-Universität.
Johanna Rahner has held a Chair of Systematic Theology at the Institute of Catholic Theology at the University of Kassel since 2010.
Geislingen: More than 100 young Catholics from Kleiner Heuberg reaffirm their creection at the Confirmation
In two services, 101 15- and 16-year-old young people of the pastoral unit Am Kleinen Heuberg have been confirmed.
In the morning accompanied the group Laudato-Si, in the afternoon the youth choir The Spirit.
With the reception of the Sacrament of Firm, the young people said yes to their baptism and requested the power of the Holy Spirit.
In the past few months, under the direction of deacon Reiner Dehner and the help of numerous parishioners, the firmlings had been preparing for the big festivity.
In various projects, they got to know various tasks of Christian community life and experienced the youth day together in Untermachtal.
Illmensee: Second mountain bike race inspires participants
In wonderful weather, 214 cyclists came to Illmensee to manage the circuit over the hills around the lake.
This is announced by Ulrich Knobel from the organizing sports club Illmensee.
Among the participants, especially on the short distance over 15 kilometers and 300 meters of altitude, there were also numerous everyone's cyclists, who wanted to practice their hobby once in a competitive atmosphere.
Two junior riders of the top German class were also at the start in this competition: Felix Bader from Bad Waldsee and Pascal Treubel from Aach-Linz came with the recommendation good placings at the German championships.
These two were able to set themselves apart from the field as expected.
As in the previous year, Felix Bader was able to save a small time advantage to the finish and defended his title as the overall winner of the short distance.
Pascal Treubel was once again able to place in second place.
With the ladies it was Theresa Duelli from Team Albtraum, who made it to the top of the podium.
In second and third place came Anne Adel from Illmensee and Leonie Treiber from Owingen.
A gratifying development is the increasing number of riders living or working in the municipality.
Not only in terms of performance, the whole range of athletes was represented.
The age structure also ranged from ten years for the youngest to the youngest to the oldest starters.
In the main race this year there were even more absolute top athletes at the start.
Four drivers were able to set themselves apart from the top regional drivers.
Including Philipp Pangerl, a mountain bike semi-professional from the Black Tusk racing team.
Pangerl, together with a team-mate, has twice been world champion in twelve-hour driving.
This year, he became European champion in this specialty discipline.
Roland Ballerstedt, who already owns two German championship titles in duathlon.
After 45 kilometers and 900 meters of altitude, there was an eyelash final in the finish line in front of the three-lake hall.
Pangerl was the first to race across the finish line.
Only one second behind the veteran Ballerstedt was able to secure second place in the overall standings.
At the same time Hermann and Warthmann finished third in the overall standings.
In the women's standings, Christiane Cohsmann was the quickest in the main race.
Areane Blersch from Binzwangen secured second place ahead of Natascha Werner from Stuttgart.
Athletes and helpers were very satisfied with the smooth and well-organized course of the event.
Travelling through muds, rivers and jungles for free medical supplies
Dr Georges Bwelle brings free medical assistance to remote villages in Cameroon
Bwelle and his team spend treating hundreds of patients almost every weekend
There aren't many doctors in the West African country, just one for 5,000
Vote here or with your mobile device
Dr. Georges Bwelle is one of the top 10 CNN Heroes of 2013.
You can vote for him or one of the other heroes in the top 10 to choose him for “CNN Hero of the Year”.
The winner will receive $250,000 to continue his extraordinary work.
For 21 years, George's Bwelle watched his sick father keep losing consciousness and traveling to hospitals that didn't have the means to help him.
Jamef Bwelle was injured in a car accident near Yaoundé, Cameroon's capital, in 1981.
At first he only had a broken arm, but then an inflammation formed, which spread to his brain and caused a hematoma there, which would affect him for the rest of his life.
“There were no neurosurgeons in Cameroon,” says Georges Bwelle.
We'd have brought him out of Cameroon if we'd have had the money.
Instead, Bwelle spent years accompanying his father to overcrowded clinics and hospitals to get the treatment they had to offer.
“It’s not easy,” Bwelle said.
You can leave the house at five in the morning and run to the hospital to be the first, and yet you are not first.
There are very many patients.
Some people even die because they have to wait.
The situation has barely changed since Bwelle's father died in 2002.
There is only one doctor in Cameroon for 5,000 people each, according to the World Health Organization.
On the other hand, the ratio in the United States rests with a doctor for 413 people.
However, even if there was a doctor for them, most Cameroonians could not afford the visit.
Two out of five people in the country live below the poverty line and nearly three-quarters of health spending in the country is private.
“The only problem they have is poverty,” Bwelle says.
Poverty makes it impossible to enjoy life.
His father and so many of his compatriots are suffering in Bwelle’s decision to do something about it matured.
Dr. Georges Bwelle and his volunteer team performed 700 free surgeries last year.
He became a doctor himself and works as a vascular surgeon at Central Hospital in Yaoundé.
In addition, he founded a non-profit organization called ASCOVIME, which travels to rural areas on weekends and offers free medical care there.
Since 2008, he and his volunteer group have helped nearly 32,000 people.
Almost every Friday, he and up to 30 people squeeze into minibuses and drive with the tied medical equipment on the roof over unpaved terrain to the villages that need help.
Everything doesn't always go smoothly.
More than than once, they had to push the vehicles through rivers and mud.
But when they arrive, they will receive like heroes: a feast, singing and dancing, and the best accommodation the place has to offer.
In these villages, free medical care is truly a cause for celebration and Bwelle – with his wide smile and boundless energy – likes to attend the joyful celebration.
The next morning, the team then begins examining hundreds of patients.
“We treat 500 people per trip,” says Bwelle.
They come from a radius of 60 kilometers around the village – and that on foot.
Each of these weekend clinics provide medical care in a number of areas.
Many people are treated for malaria, tuberculosis, malnutrition, diabetes, parasites and sexually transmitted diseases.
Others receive crutches, donated glasses, or free birth certificates – a document that is required for school, but many poor families simply can’t afford.
In the evening, the team performs simple operations with local anesthesia.
The surgeries usually take place in a school, the town hall or an apartment; after the procedure, the patients get up and go into the recreation area to make room for the next.
The lighting for surgery and disinfection equipment is powered by the group's generator and that's how Bwelle and his volunteers work until the early hours of Sunday.
It's an exhausting effort, but the village musicians usually help keep the team motivated.
“They’re drumming all night so that we can stay awake and continue our work,” says Bwelle.
On Sunday, the team then drives back to the city, tired but proud of the work done.
The group – a mixture of Cameroonian physicians and foreign medical students – has conducted 700 free surgeries in the last year and knows that with its work it makes a huge difference for the people it helps.
One man explained that the free operation of a hernia, which he had received, was now allowing him to return to work.
“This changes my family’s future,” the man said.
In addition to the weekend clinics and his work as a surgeon in the hospital, Bwelle also works in private clinics in Yaoundé at night.
With this second job, he finances 60% of his charitable activity, according to Bwelle; the rest comes from private donations.
"I really don't know when he's asleep," says Katie O'Malley, who's studying medicine at Drexel University in Philadelphia for the second year, volunteering in Bwelle's group.
He's always either in the hospital or trying to raise money for the organization so he can go on these campaigns.
For medical and nursing students like O'Malley, who come from the U.S. and Europe and support Bwelle, this is a chance to gain hands-on experience they'd never get at home.
“We can be involved in surgeries where we’re helping to dab blood or keep the instruments for Dr. Bwelle,” O’Malley explains.
This would never be possible in America as a second-year medical student.
The students usually pay for their trip to Cameroon themselves and frequently bring donated medical supplies.
However, once they are in Yaoundé, Bwelle takes over the accommodation, the transport and the instructions.
"He's without a doubt a hero," says O'Malley.
He devotes his life to this organization and his desire to help the Cameroonian people knows no boundaries.
For Bwelle, the almost constant work is not a burden.
Helping others to a happier life and thereby fulfill a promise that he has made to his father gives him great joy.
“I’m so happy when I do this work,” Bwelle said.
And I'm thinking about my dad.
I'm hoping he'll see what I'm doing.
I'm doing it to make people laugh again and to ease the pain.
Visit ASCOVIME’s website and find out how to help.
Excellently mastered the battle
The role of Santiago, the old man in Hemingway's novela "The Old Man and the Sea," Horst Janson is cut to the body.
Janson himself is an old rabbit in his profession, acting.
No one can fool the old man Santiago while fishing.
Thus, the two simply complement each other great.
It is almost believed to be able to see the wrinkles in the neck of the old man's boy, of which Manolo speaks.
If Santiago sits tired of life bent in the chair, that's real with Janson.
In addition, the role of the old fisherman is through and through a character role that Janson masters excellently.
Hemingway told the story of a fighter, a courageous man who doesn't give up and ultimately wins the fight against the fish through his knowledge and willpower.
Janson is on stage in long distances as a solo actor.
He manages to focus the audience's attention on himself, so his monologues aren't boring for a moment.
His efforts to finally pull the fish to the surface of the water are so real that the spectators forget that the sea consists only of blue-lit water bottles.
Director Jens Hasselmann lets the old man act in the boat in front of the fishing village.
In the course of the performance, he puts the active scene in the light: the hut of Santiago, the bar of the singer Marie-Luise Gunst, who sings with her songs and her glorious voice the lifestyle of Cuba.
The four musicians play Cuba’s rhythm and are also guests of the bar.
Tourism: Descent to the Romans
At a length of about 120 meters, a sewer from the Roman period is preserved below Cologne.
If you visit Cologne, you won’t be able to miss the cathedral.
Around 157 meters, the two towers rise into the sky and make it the second-highest church building in Europe.
However, only a few visitors who admire the two filigree church towers suspect that there is also a lot to discover under the cathedral.
Rainer Schulze is an expert in the Cologne underworld.
The meeting point for its guided tours is the service center of Cologne-Tourismus directly at the cathedral.
The tour begins quite unspectacularly: "We start in the underground car park," says Schulze.
In the seventies, the city fathers wanted a car-friendly city, and therefore of the remnants of the remnants of the old city wall stood only that which was not too big.
Most users of the parking garage therefore rush past the Roman remains today and only park their car.
But if you take a closer look, you will discover an archaeological site separated by metal lattice in the parking deck D2.
About five-meter-high ruins of palm-sized stones can be seen.
"It's remnants of the Roman city wall built in the second half of the first century AD," Schulze recounts.
Before it goes back to daylight, Schulze lets the participants on the upper parking level take a look into a dark, 15-meter-deep shaft.
This is the fountain of the old cathedral, which is unfortunately hardly noticed.
The next stop of the tour is the praetorium, and here the participants descend into the depths again.
We are witnessing here the official seat of the once most powerful man north of the Alps, the praetor of the CCAA.
What CCAA means, a visitor wants to know.
"The abbreviation stands for "Colonia Claudia Ara Agrippinensium", which in good German means as much as "City Roman law and city of Agrippinensians, founded under Emperor Claudius at the site of the altar for the imperial cult".
And the Praetorium is the former governor's palace, the power center of the Roman Empire on the Rhine.
From the anteroom of the exhibition, a tunnel access leads to the Roman sewer, which is preserved here over a length of around 120 meters.
"You can quietly walk along the so-called Cloaca Maxima, if you want," Schulze cheers the guests.
Schulze still has a surprise.
After picking up the key at the cash register, he leads the group to a 16-meter-deep, walk-in shaft.
"We are now in the mikvehy, a ritual Jewish bath," he explains.
The Jewish community built its bath deep into the ground to get to the groundwater that was essential for the ritual ablutions.
To this day, in the lower end of the mikveh, whose origins date back to the eighth century, different water levels can be seen by changing levels of the Rhine.
The mikveh will become part of an archaeological zone on the town hall square, for which the construction work is scheduled to begin shortly.
However, a few more years will pass before completion.
Leg almost chopped off: Reissue of Rocker process
A bloody attack in the rocker milieu will be re-rolled after more than four years.
A trial for attempted manslaughter, dangerous bodily harm and property damage against two 30-year-olds has begun before the district court of Frankfurt (Oder).
He will be accompanied by strict safety precautions.
The defendants remained silent at the kick-off.
They are said to have been active in the rocker scene and belonged to the Bandidos at that time.
Prosecutors accuse them of stabbing and smashing three men in a car together with unknown accomplices in Finowfurt in June 2009.
All the victims were seriously injured, a man almost lost his leg.
The victims are said to have belonged to the competing Hells Angels group.
One is said to have been a high-ranking member from Berlin.
Preceded was apparently a chase with several cars.
According to the indictment, it was about precinct battles and a power demonstration.
The two defendants had been acquitted in an initial trial in early 2012.
A court spokesman said at the time it had not been proven that the men had actually been involved in the bloody feud.
The perpetrators had been wearing masks and had not been recognized.
The Federal Court overturned the verdict after appeal by the prosecutor's officence and referred it back to the district court for retrial.
One of the defendants is currently in open custody for another offence.
At the beginning, it was mainly about the reconstruction of the processes on the night of the crime.
As a witness, the commissioner investigating at the time was invited.
At the center were mobile phone conversations of the defendants and other persons, which the police had wiretapped.
What's difficult is that rockers in court almost always abide by a law that's brazen for them - and don't testify.
This also applies to victims.
In the first trial, only one of the victims had unwrapped.
Prosecutor Stefan Golfier of the public prosecutor's officence Frankfurt (Oder) said on request that new traces had surfaced since the last proceedings.
Eleven more days of negotiations are scheduled for the trial.
The throwaway society isn't thinking
The night was long, the music loud and the mood good, but eventually it's going home.
Only: In the bellies of the people in the passenger and back seat, the hunger is noticeable.
Sure, one or the other cocktail or champagne may be responsible for that!
What's more obvious than chilled to the next drive-in to treat yourself to a trifle?
I admit, I like to be there when it comes to fast-food-fetching late at night or early in the morning.
A few fries, a coke, a burger - and then nothing like going home and into bed!
However, on the way home, the obvious differences between me and other hungry disco-goers become noticeable.
After all, what do you have to lack that you throw your fast food bags out of the car window with constant regularity?!
Especially on the weekends, discarded paper bags lie on roadsides and in parking lots.
"Why?" I ask myself.
Are people too dumb to take the bags home or until the next bin?
It’s not just that the paper stuff is dismantling the landscape.
Bags have already concealed traffic facilities such as guardrails and thus triggered accidents.
But the said "throwaway society" doesn't think about something like that, she probably doesn't even know what this word actually means.
If you travel more often in the evening, especially with several people in the car, you know what a back seat can look like after celebrated nights: clothes, bottles and other rubbish tower there.
There would be a few bags more or less nothing - in contrast to the street, where none wants to trample on the leftovers of others.
I can already see the day coming when the cities and towns become too dumble - and the fast food chains have to demand deposits for their bags.
Obama's backsliding on health care policy
After heavy criticism, President Obama yesterday made a backsliding on his frequently-repeated, unequivocal promise: “If you like your health insurance, you can keep it.”
After hundreds of thousands received a letter of termination from their insurer, Republicans accused the president in recent days that he had deceived the American public.
Yesterday Obama relativized his original promise.
“The vast majority of citizens who have functioning health insurance can keep them,” he said during a speech in Boston.
In response to the numerous media reports on the dismissals, Obama urged those Americans who have received such notifications to look for new insurance on the open market.
Most citizens could thus receive better, more comprehensive health insurance at the same price or cheaper than predicted.
“They’re coming a better deal,” he said.
The government described it as unsurprising that the five percent of the population, who have taken out insurance themselves, would have to look for a different tariff, since their contract does not comply with the new standards under the Affordable Care Act.
"I would like to say directly to these Americans: they deserve better," Sebelius said at a hearing before the Energy and Trade Committee of the House of Representatives in Washington.
Sebelius, responsible for implementing the Affordable Care Act, stated that the launch of the online platform had been “miserable” since October.
"I'm just as frustrated and angry as everyone else," she said.
I'm determined to regain your trust.
An upset Sebelius's statement to an adviser sitting behind her at yesterday's hearing in the House of Representatives was taken up after an argument with Republican lawmaker Billy Long of Missouri, about whether she should be required to participate in Obamacare.
After more than three hours of hearing, Long Sebelius repeatedly harassed why the “architect” of the Affordable Care Act had not voluntarily waived a state-sponsored insurance company in order to take a tariff on HealthCare.gov, which it now wants to sell millions of Americans.
Government in Jerusalem confirms attack on Syrian air base
An American government official, who asked to remain anonymous, claimed Thursday to the American news channel CNN that Israeli warplanes had attacked an air base in Syria's Latakia a day earlier.
The target was "rockets and associated equipment" that the Israeli government had feared were destined for Lebanese Hezbollah, according to CNN.
Syrian and Lebanese media, as well as Arab broadcaster Al-Arabiya had earlier reported that the base in the port city of Latakia had been shelled in the night from Wednesday to Thursday, blaming the Israelis.
The Israeli television channel Channel 2 showed satellite images of the base in Latakia, on which Russian defense missiles of the type S-125 Newa and a battery of SA-3 missiles could be seen, according to Channel 2, have a range of 35 kilometers and can transport warheads up to 70 kilograms.
Uncertain The government in Jerusalem has not officially confirmed the attack.
However, the Reuters news agency quotes an anonymous Israeli government official as saying that he believed that Israel carried out the attack, but is not certain.
A spokesman for the Israeli Defense Ministry said: "We are not commenting on these reports".
Journalist Ron Ben-Yishai emphasized in the daily Yedioth Ahronoth that the Syrian regime had earlier, partly successfully, tried to deliver surface-to-air missiles to Hezbollah.
The Israeli government had repeatedly warned that any attempt by Syria to supply Hezbollah with chemical or other dangerous weapons would mean crossing a "red line" that a military response would follow.
John Kerry declares in an unparalleled admission that U.S. espionage has "gone inappropriately too far"
John Kerry hinted with an unexpected confession a softening of U.S. defensive stance regarding its surveillance programs by saying the espionage had "gone inappropriately too far" in certain cases.
The Secretary of State also admitted that, together with Barack Obama, he was responsible for switching to “autopilot” when whistleblower Edward Snowden’s explosive revelations about the NSA’s espionage activities became public.
Due to the leaked information, the U.S. government has fallen into a diplomatic storm of indignation from its allies.
Speaking a video-connected speech at the Open Government conference in London, Kerry said: “Not without question, the president and I, as well as others in the government, have learned of some things that have virtually expired by autopilot, simply because of the ability to do so, which still amounted to World War II and the difficult years of the Cold War and, of course, 9/11.”
Subsequently, he became the first high-ranking member of the U.S. government to acknowledge that U.S. espionage had crossed a line boundary, but also emphasized that no rights had been abused.
He said, “In some cases, that’s gone inappropriately too far.”
The president is determined to provide clarity and is therefore carrying out a thorough investigation so that no one has to feel that abuse is taking place here.
I assure you that the rights of innocent persons will not be compromised in this process.
However, Kerry insisted that the NSA was a force of good and many lives had been saved by its surveillance operations.
“We are dealing with a new world where people are willing to blow themselves up.”
There is radical extremism in the way of killing people, blowing people and attacking governments.
So, what if you were able to intercept and stop this before it happens?
We have actually prevented plane crashes, attacks on buildings and the murder of people, because we learned of such plans in advance.
Meanwhile, U.S. lawmakers will travel to Europe to dispel concerns about alleged American espionage and convince Europeans of the need to continue working with the U.S. on anti-terrorism efforts, the chairman of the Senate Subcommittee on European Affairs stated on Thursday.
Senator Chris Murphy from Connecticut said he had spoken to representatives of the European Parliament and other people this week and was concerned about their threats to stop participating in counter-terrorism organizations due to frustration over surveillance by the National Security Agency.
“It’s very important to U.S. national security interestences that Europeans stay on board with regard to our joint efforts against terrorism,” Murphy, who sits in the Senate for the first time as a Democrat and serves as chairman of the Senate Subcommittee on Foreign Relations and European Affairs, said in an interview in Washington.
In addition, I am going to Europe to make it clear that we must continue to work together in the fight against terrorism, regardless of their anger about the NSA programs.
Media reports that the NSA captured millions of phone data in Europe have strained relations with some U.S. allies, although the head of intelligence this week said the reports were inaccurate and were based on a misunderstanding of the metadata collected by NATO allies and exchanged with the United States.
Other revelations cited from documents leaked by Snowden, according to that the NSA had monitored the mobile phone of Chancellor Angela Merkel and up to 34 other heads of state worldwide.
The head of national intelligence, James Clapper, defended the espionage among allies as necessary and declared that this was customary on both sides.
In all the tumult, Murphy said his agency was preparing the congressional trip, which is scheduled to take place this year, and hoped the delegation would include members from both parties and chambers.
The names of the other participating MPs are due to be published in the coming days.
He said the exact itinerary is still being worked out.
While Murphy emphasized that the purpose of the trip was to improve relations, he also spoke of the fact that there would be some “serious words.”
He said European governments needed to be honest with their people about the kind of espionage programs they themselves have been using for years.
“While we can change our surveillance programs and better protect the rights of Europeans, they also have to come to terms with the fact that we aren’t spying,” Murphy said.
In the meantime, a trip to the Middle East and Poland is scheduled for John Kerry over the weekend to deal with grudges over U.S. strategy in Syria, Egypt and Iran, as well as U.S. surveillance activities.
A stubborn injury on the ankle joint threatens to prevent Nicolai Müller's use in FSV Mainz 05's Bundesliga away game at FC Augsburg.
Mainz fears for a use of Nicolai Müller.
He's going to try to train with the team tomorrow.
Until now, he could only do that individually.
"We will decide in the short term about his deployment," coach Thomas Tuchel said.
The 40-year-old hopes to have his top scorer with side.
Mainz already have to do without the injured performers Niki Zimling, Julian Baumgartlinger and Niko Bungert.
Australian appeals against prison sentence in Thailand
A 21-year-old woman from Sydney, who was sentenced to 15 days imprise in Phuket for falsely claiming to have been raped by a taxi driver has appealed against the verdict and was released on bail.
Stevie Rochelle Bamford was initially found guilty by a provincial court in Phuket on June 15 for making a false statement after she told the Thai police that a local taxi driver had raped her in the morning hours on Sunday, June 10, while two other men detained her.
Later, however, the footage from a video camera showed that she had arrived safely at her hotel after being separated from her Australian boyfriend.
Police in Phuket interrogated Bamford for two days until she confessed to making up history.
Until the trial, she was in police custody at the scene.
Bamford has been sentenced to a 15-day imprisonment at a low-security detention facility on the outskirts of Phuket, rather than in a prison for adult women.
She is the daughter of Peter Tunks, a former player of the Australian Rubgy League, who has appealed to the Foreign Ministry in Canberra asking for help.
Tunks told the Sunday Telegraph in Sydney that the whole family was "extremely concerned" about his daughter's well-being and wanted her back in Australia.
“Of course we’re very worried, but we’re hoped she’ll be back home as soon as possible,” Tunks said.
Bamford has appealed the verdict and is on the loose on a bail of 50,000 baht.
In Australian reports, it was said that she is vacationinging in the holiday resort Krabi in southern Thailand in the meantime.
On the part of the court, Bamford was represented by a local lawyer in Phuket and had been warned that the appeal could also lead to the imposition of a higher sentence of up to two years in an adult prison.
However, it is also possible that Thailand, following the recent assassination of travel clerk Michelle Smith in Phuket, could make an effort to improve its ailing tourist image, and therefore an acquittal.
